window.AA={"v":1,"units":[{"n":1,"level":"Beginner","platform":"Both","kicker":"Beginner · Meta + Google · The big picture","title":"The job, the auction and the two machines","sub":"What a performance marketer actually does, and how Meta and Google decide who sees an ad","goal":["Explain the performance marketer's job in one sentence.","Explain how Meta's auction and Google's Ad Rank pick a winner, and why a better ad can beat a bigger bid.","Tell the difference between demand capture (Google Search) and demand creation (Meta)."],"lead":["Welcome to Ads Academy. This first tutorial gives you the map. By the end you will know what the job is, how both platforms decide which ad to show, and why the quality of your ad matters as much as your budget.","Every number and rule in this course comes from Meta's and Google's own help pages, and the sources are listed under every unit. Where a figure is just an example to practise with, we say so."],"lessons":[{"title":"The job in one sentence.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"A performance marketer turns ad money into measurable results, and proves it with numbers.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Results means a purchase, a booked consultation, a lead, or an app install. Not likes. Not \"brand buzz\". Something a client can count and put a value on.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"That changes how you think. A brand marketer asks, \"Did people like it?\" A performance marketer asks, \"What did one customer cost us, and was it worth it?\"","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"p","text":"At an agency like SoScale, the work splits into a few daily jobs. You plan campaigns. You brief and write creative. You launch and watch the numbers. You decide what to cut and what to scale. And you explain all of it to the client in plain words.","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"One sentence you can say in an interview","text":"\"My job is to spend the client's money so that every krona comes back as more than a krona, and to show them why.\" That is the whole role, and it is what agencies hire for.","k":"L1.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write your own one-sentence version of the job, using the word \"measurable\". Say it out loud until it sounds natural. You will use it in every interview and cold email.","k":"L1.5"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"Two machines: demand capture and demand creation.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Google Search and Meta work in opposite directions.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"On Google Search, the person comes to you. They type \"running shoes for flat feet\" or \"dentist Södermalm\". The need already exists. Your job is to show up with the right answer at that moment. That is demand capture.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"On Meta, which means Facebook, Instagram, Messenger and more, nobody is searching for you. People are scrolling through friends, Reels and news. Your ad interrupts them. Your job is to make them want something they were not thinking about a second ago. That is demand creation.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"p","text":"This one difference explains most of the course. On Google, keywords and intent do much of the work, so relevance and the landing page win. On Meta, the creative does the heavy lifting: the hook, the image, the first three seconds.","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d01-capture-create.svg","cap":"Diagram: Google Search captures demand that already exists; Meta creates demand by interrupting a scroll.","k":"L2.4"},{"t":"ex","head":"Same product, two jobs","text":"A fictional Stockholm skincare brand sells a fragrance-free face cream. On Google, it bids on searches like \"fragrance free moisturiser sensitive skin\" and answers the exact question. On Meta, it shows a ten-second video of a real customer's dry winter skin, with a hook that stops the scroll.","k":"L2.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"For any product you see, ask two questions. What would someone type into Google when they already want this? And what would make a scroller stop and want it? Two answers, two campaigns.","k":"L2.6"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"How Meta's auction picks a winner.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Every time there is a chance to show someone an ad on Meta, an auction runs. Meta says billions of these auctions happen every day.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta's help centre says the winner is the ad with the highest total value. Total value combines three things. First, your bid: what you are willing to pay for the result you want. Second, estimated action rates: how likely this person is to click, buy or sign up after seeing this ad. Third, ad quality, judged from things like people hiding the ad, and from low-quality signs such as clickbait, sensational language and withholding information.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Here is the key line. Meta says estimated action rates and ad quality together measure relevance, and an ad that is more relevant to a person can win the auction against ads with higher bids.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"p","text":"So a great ad is not just nicer to look at. It is cheaper. It wins auctions you would otherwise have to overpay for.","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d01-meta-auction.svg","cap":"Diagram: Meta's total value = bid + estimated action rates + ad quality (Meta Business Help Center, \"About ad auctions\").","k":"L3.4"},{"t":"warn","text":"Engagement bait does not help. Meta says clickbait and asking for likes or comments do not improve ad performance, and those tactics count against ad quality.","k":"L3.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"When an ad is expensive, don't reach for a bigger budget first. Ask: is it relevant to the people seeing it? Would they hide it? Fix the ad before you fix the bid.","k":"L3.6"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"How Google decides: Ad Rank.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Google Search runs an auction too, every time someone searches. Google uses a value called Ad Rank to decide whether your ad can show at all, and where it shows.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Google's help page says Ad Rank is calculated from several factors: your bid, the quality of your ads and landing page, the Ad Rank thresholds, the context of the search, and the expected impact of your assets, such as extra links under the ad.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Ad Rank is calculated twice in each auction. Once to decide if your ad is eligible to show, and once to decide its position against the other eligible ads.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"p","text":"And Google says the same thing Meta says: with highly relevant ads and assets, you can win a higher position at a lower price, even against competitors with higher bids.","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d01-adrank.svg","cap":"Diagram: inputs to Google's Ad Rank (Google Ads Help, \"Ad Rank: Definition\").","k":"L4.4"},{"t":"ex","head":"Two dentists, one search","text":"Someone searches \"emergency dentist open Saturday\". Dentist A bids more but sends people to a generic homepage. Dentist B bids less, has the words \"open Saturdays\" in the ad, and links straight to a booking page. Dentist B can win the better position, at a lower cost per click.","k":"L4.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"Before you write any Google ad, write down the exact search you want to win. Then check: does my ad repeat that need? Does my landing page answer it in the first screen?","k":"L4.6"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"What both platforms reward.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Put the two auctions side by side and the lesson is the same. Both platforms are paid by advertisers, but they only keep users if the ads are useful. So both reward relevance.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"That gives you three levers, in this order. First, the offer and the message: is this something the person actually wants? Second, the creative or the ad text: does it make that clear in a second? Third, the landing page: does it keep the promise the ad made?","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Budget and bids matter, but they sit on top of those three. A weak ad with a big budget just loses money faster.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"p","text":"This is why the old copywriters still matter. Claude Hopkins wrote about specific claims a hundred years ago. Today the auction literally prices specificity and relevance for you.","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/meta-ad-library.png","cap":"Screenshot: Meta Ad Library (facebook.com/ads/library). Every active ad on Meta is public here, so you can study what real advertisers run. Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L5.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Take one ad you saw today. Score it from one to five on offer, message and landing page. The lowest score is the first thing a performance marketer would fix.","k":"L5.5"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"How this course works.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Each unit has three steps. Listen, where you hear a short tutorial like this one and read along. Try, where you do drills: picking winners, writing ads, sorting keywords. And Check, a short quiz, one question at a time.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"You only ever need to press the big Continue button on the home screen. It always knows your next step.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"The Beginner level teaches the machines: account structure, keywords, tracking and the core metrics. Intermediate is about running real accounts: targeting, bidding, creative strategy and policies. Expert is how senior media buyers think: diagnosing, testing, automation, measurement, scaling and reporting to clients.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"p","text":"All the old practice tools are still here, in the Practice area, when you want extra reps.","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Press Try now. The drills take about ten minutes. Then do the Check quiz. Aim for eighty percent or more, and retry as often as you like.","k":"L6.4"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["Performance marketing = ad spend in, measurable results out, proven with numbers.","Google Search captures existing demand (intent). Meta creates demand (interrupts a scroll).","Meta auction: highest total value wins = bid + estimated action rates + ad quality. Relevance can beat bigger bids.","Google Ad Rank: bid, ad + landing page quality, thresholds, search context, expected impact of assets. Calculated twice: eligibility, then position.","Fix in this order: offer and message, then creative or ad text, then landing page. Budget comes after."],"src":[{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About ad auctions","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/430291176997542"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About ad quality","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/423781975167984"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: Ad Rank, definition","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/1752122"},{"label":"Meta for Business: Mobile-first creative","url":"https://en-gb.facebook.com/business/news/mobile-first-creative"}],"quiz":[{"q":"According to Meta, what three things make up an ad's \"total value\" in the auction?","o":["Budget, audience size and placement","Bid, estimated action rates and ad quality","Bid, number of ads and campaign objective","Click-through rate, frequency and reach"],"a":1,"e":"Meta's help centre lists bid, estimated action rates and ad quality. The last two together measure relevance."},{"q":"Can an ad with a lower bid win a Meta auction against a higher bid?","o":["Yes, if it is more relevant to the person (higher estimated action rate and ad quality)","No, the highest bid always wins","Only on Instagram, not on Facebook","Only if the advertiser pays for priority placement"],"a":0,"e":"Meta says a more relevant ad can win against ads with higher bids."},{"q":"Which statement about Google's Ad Rank is correct?","o":["It is calculated once a day for each keyword","It only uses your bid","It is calculated in each auction, first for eligibility and then for position","It is the same as Quality Score"],"a":2,"e":"Google calculates Ad Rank twice per auction: to decide if the ad can show, and where."},{"q":"Google Search is mainly a tool for…","o":["Demand capture: reaching people who are already searching for a solution","Demand creation: interrupting people who are not looking","Brand awareness only","Collecting likes and followers"],"a":0,"e":"On Search the person comes to you with intent. On Meta you interrupt a scroll and create demand."},{"q":"Your Meta ad is expensive. What should a performance marketer check first?","o":["Raise the bid so it wins more auctions","Whether the ad is relevant to the people seeing it (offer, message, creative)","Turn off the campaign and start a new ad account","Add more interests to the audience"],"a":1,"e":"Relevance is part of the auction. Fixing the ad often lowers cost more than raising bids."},{"q":"Which of these does Meta say counts against ad quality?","o":["Showing the product clearly","Using a real customer photo","Engagement bait and sensational language","Writing a specific price"],"a":2,"e":"Meta lists clickbait, engagement bait, sensational language and withholding information as low-quality attributes."},{"q":"What is a \"result\" in performance marketing?","o":["A like or a share","A measurable action with value, such as a purchase, lead or booking","An impression","A follower"],"a":1,"e":"Performance marketing is judged on measurable actions the client can value."}],"drills":[{"type":"sort","title":"Capture or create?","prompt":["Is each campaign mainly capturing existing demand or creating new demand?"],"checks":[],"model":["On Search the need already exists, so you capture it. In the feed nobody is looking, so the creative has to create the want."],"items":["Ad shown to people who searched \"buy running shoes size 44\" => Capture (Google Search)","Reel showing a new sparkling-water maker to people scrolling Instagram => Create (Meta feed)","Ad on the search \"dentist open Saturday Stockholm\" => Capture (Google Search)","Story ad for a skincare brand most viewers have never heard of => Create (Meta feed)","Ad on the search \"aarke carbonator price\" => Capture (Google Search)","Facebook feed video for a new protein snack => Create (Meta feed)"],"cats":["Capture (Google Search)","Create (Meta feed)"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"pick","title":"Which ad wins more auctions?","prompt":["Both platforms reward relevance. Pick the line that is more relevant and specific."],"checks":[],"model":[],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[{"a":"\"Quality dental care for the whole family.\"","b":"\"Emergency dentist, open Saturdays 9-15. Book online in 2 minutes.\"","ok":"B","e":"Specific, matches an urgent search, gives a next step. The first could be any clinic."},{"a":"\"Like and share if you love coffee!\"","b":"\"Fresh-roasted beans, delivered within 48 hours of roasting.\"","ok":"B","e":"Engagement bait counts against ad quality. A specific benefit earns real clicks."},{"a":"\"The ultimate skincare revolution.\"","b":"\"Fragrance-free cream for skin that stings in winter.\"","ok":"B","e":"Calls out the reader and the problem. Hype words are a low-quality signal and say nothing."}],"calc":[]},{"type":"write","title":"Your one-sentence job pitch","prompt":["Write one sentence that explains what a performance marketer does, the way you would say it in an interview."],"checks":[{"kind":"include","arg":"measur,result,return,krona,profit","label":"Mentions measurable results"},{"kind":"maxwords","arg":"30","label":"30 words or fewer"},{"kind":"avoid","arg":"","label":"No vague hype words"}],"model":["\"I spend a client's ad budget so that every krona comes back as more than a krona, and I prove it with the numbers.\""],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t1-1-07","app":"taste","id":"t1-1-07","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t1-1-07","title":"Hopkins drill: which line uses reason-why?","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"1.1"},{"ref":"taste:t1-3-02","app":"taste","id":"t1-3-02","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t1-3-02","title":"Meta video: hook → body → CTA","kind":"Mini-lesson","module":"1.3"},{"ref":"taste:t1-3-07","app":"taste","id":"t1-3-07","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t1-3-07","title":"Good or bad? Rate it, then reveal the breakdown.","kind":"Good or bad?","module":"1.3"}],"words":1319,"audio":{"duration":526.06,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. The job in one sentence.","s":31.55},{"t":"2. Two machines: demand capture and demand creation.","s":111.61},{"t":"3. How Meta's auction picks a winner.","s":208.49},{"t":"4. How Google decides: Ad Rank.","s":298.63},{"t":"5. What both platforms reward.","s":385.94},{"t":"6. How this course works.","s":452.75},{"t":"End","s":518.12}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":4.08},{"k":"lead.0","s":4.98,"d":13.056},{"k":"lead.1","s":18.636,"d":12.312},{"k":"L1.h","s":31.548,"d":3.024},{"k":"L1.0","s":35.072,"d":6.312},{"k":"L1.1","s":41.984,"d":11.352},{"k":"L1.2","s":53.936,"d":10.488},{"k":"L1.3","s":65.024,"d":17.832},{"k":"L1.4","s":83.456,"d":15.024},{"k":"L1.5","s":99.08,"d":11.928},{"k":"L2.h","s":111.608,"d":4.512},{"k":"L2.0","s":116.62,"d":3.624},{"k":"L2.1","s":120.844,"d":13.536},{"k":"L2.2","s":134.98,"d":18.048},{"k":"L2.3","s":153.628,"d":17.04},{"k":"L2.5","s":171.268,"d":23.64},{"k":"L2.6","s":195.508,"d":12.384},{"k":"L3.h","s":208.492,"d":3.336},{"k":"L3.0","s":212.328,"d":8.712},{"k":"L3.1","s":221.64,"d":29.976},{"k":"L3.2","s":252.216,"d":13.296},{"k":"L3.3","s":266.112,"d":8.064},{"k":"L3.5","s":274.776,"d":11.616},{"k":"L3.6","s":286.992,"d":11.04},{"k":"L4.h","s":298.632,"d":3.528},{"k":"L4.0","s":302.66,"d":10.056},{"k":"L4.1","s":313.316,"d":15.192},{"k":"L4.2","s":329.108,"d":10.608},{"k":"L4.3","s":340.316,"d":10.776},{"k":"L4.5","s":351.692,"d":22.248},{"k":"L4.6","s":374.54,"d":10.8},{"k":"L5.h","s":385.94,"d":2.88},{"k":"L5.0","s":389.32,"d":11.808},{"k":"L5.1","s":401.728,"d":18.0},{"k":"L5.2","s":420.328,"d":7.848},{"k":"L5.3","s":428.776,"d":12.048},{"k":"L5.5","s":441.424,"d":10.728},{"k":"L6.h","s":452.752,"d":2.616},{"k":"L6.0","s":455.868,"d":15.672},{"k":"L6.1","s":472.14,"d":5.808},{"k":"L6.2","s":478.548,"d":22.296},{"k":"L6.3","s":501.444,"d":5.664},{"k":"L6.4","s":507.708,"d":9.816},{"k":"end","s":518.124,"d":6.336}],"file":"aa-t01-the-job-the-auction-and-the-two-machines.mp3"}},{"n":2,"level":"Beginner","platform":"Meta","kicker":"Beginner · Meta · Account structure","title":"Meta Ads Manager: campaign, ad set, ad","sub":"The three levels, the six objectives, and which setting lives where","goal":["Name what is decided at the campaign, ad set and ad level.","Pick the right objective out of Meta's six for a business goal.","Explain conversion location and performance goal, and why \"Sales\" is not always the answer."],"lead":["Every Meta campaign you will ever run is built from the same three boxes inside each other: campaign, ad set and ad. Once you know which decision lives in which box, Ads Manager stops looking scary.","This tutorial follows Meta's own help centre. We describe settings by name, not by exact button position, because Meta changes its screens often."],"lessons":[{"title":"Three boxes inside each other.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Meta's help centre describes three levels. One campaign contains one or more ad sets. Each ad set contains one or more ads.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"The campaign is the top box. Here you choose the objective, the overall result you want, and the buying type. Most advertisers use the auction buying type, which is what this whole course is about.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"The ad set is the middle box. Meta lists four things it controls: the audience, the placements, the budget and the schedule. All ads inside one ad set share those settings.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"p","text":"The ad is the bottom box. Here you build what people actually see: the format, such as a single image or video, a carousel or a collection; the creative, meaning the images, videos, text, headlines and links; and the identity, meaning which Facebook Page and Instagram account the ad runs from.","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d02-meta-structure.svg","cap":"Diagram: Meta campaign → ad sets → ads, and what each level controls (Meta Business Help Center, \"What are the advertising levels in Meta Ads Manager?\").","k":"L1.4"},{"t":"ex","head":"A simple first account","text":"Campaign: Sales objective for a fictional skincare shop. Ad set: Sweden, adults, Advantage+ placements, daily budget. Ads: three different creatives, a customer video, a static with the price, and a before-bedtime routine clip.","k":"L1.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"When you look at any account, first say out loud what each campaign is for, what each ad set is for, and what each ad is testing. If you can't, the structure is the problem.","k":"L1.6"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"The six objectives.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Meta now uses six simplified objectives. Awareness, Traffic, Engagement, Leads, App promotion and Sales.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Awareness reaches the most people likely to remember your ad. Traffic sends people to a destination, such as your website or app. Engagement finds people likely to interact, message you or take actions on your ad or Page. Leads collects contact details through messages, calls or sign-up forms. App promotion gets installs or actions in an app. Sales finds people likely to buy, and you can also optimise for other actions, such as adding to cart.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"The objective matters because it tells the delivery system which people to look for. Choose Traffic and Meta finds people who click. Choose Sales and it looks for people who buy. Those are often not the same people.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d02-objectives.svg","cap":"Diagram: Meta's six simplified objectives and the business goal each one serves (Meta Business Help Center).","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"warn","text":"A classic beginner mistake is running a Traffic campaign for an online shop because clicks look cheap. Cheap clicks from people who never buy are expensive. If the goal is purchases and your tracking works, use Sales.","k":"L2.4"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/meta-help-objectives.png","cap":"Screenshot: Meta Business Help Center, \"How to choose the right ad objective in Meta Ads Manager\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L2.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"For every brief, write the business goal first, in the client's words, then match it to one of the six objectives. One goal, one objective.","k":"L2.6"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"Conversion location and performance goal.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"After the objective, Meta asks two more questions. Meta's help centre defines them like this.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Conversion location is where you want people to take action: for example your website, an app, or a messaging app such as Messenger or WhatsApp. For the Leads objective, Meta also lists phone calls and sign-ups.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Performance goal is the specific outcome Ads Manager optimises delivery for. For example, inside the Sales objective you might optimise for purchases, or for adds to cart.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Think of it as three layers of the same instruction. Objective: I want sales. Conversion location: on my website. Performance goal: maximise the number of purchases.","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"A clinic that books by phone","text":"A fictional laser clinic gets most bookings by phone. A Leads campaign with calls as the conversion location can fit better than sending people to a website they will leave. The goal follows how the business actually takes customers.","k":"L3.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Before you build, write the three layers in one line: \"Objective, location, goal.\" If you can't fill in all three, ask the client how a customer actually buys.","k":"L3.5"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"Budget and bid strategy, in one minute.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"You set the budget either on the campaign or on each ad set. Meta calls the campaign version Advantage+ campaign budget: one budget that is distributed in real time to the ad sets with the best opportunities. With ad set budgets, you control the spend of each ad set yourself.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Budgets can be daily or lifetime. Meta's help centre says that on days with better opportunities it may spend up to seventy-five percent over your daily budget, but no more than one hundred and seventy-five percent of the daily budget in a day, and no more than seven times the daily budget in a week. Over a week it evens out.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"The bid strategy tells Meta how to bid. Meta groups them into three types. Spend-based, such as highest volume or highest value, which spends your budget to get the most results or value. Goal-based, such as a cost per result goal or a ROAS goal, which tries to stay near a target. And manual, the bid cap, for advertisers who can calculate the right bid themselves.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"p","text":"For beginners, highest volume is the normal start. You learn your real costs first, then you set goals.","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d02-budget-bid.svg","cap":"Diagram: where the budget lives and the three bid strategy types (Meta Business Help Center: budgets, daily budgets, bid strategies).","k":"L4.4"},{"t":"warn","text":"Don't panic when one day spends more than your daily budget. Meta documents that daily spend can flex up to seventy-five percent over, while staying within the weekly limit.","k":"L4.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write down the client's real limit per week, not per day. Then check that daily budget times seven fits inside it.","k":"L4.6"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"Naming and a clean account.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Agencies live and die by clean accounts. A good name tells anyone what a campaign, ad set or ad is, without opening it.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"A simple pattern works. Campaign: the market, the objective and the product, for example \"SE, Sales, Face cream\". Ad set: the audience and placement idea, for example \"Broad, Advantage+ placements\". Ad: the concept and format, for example \"Winter dry skin, UGC video, v1\".","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Clean names make reporting faster, make tests readable and stop expensive mistakes, such as pausing the wrong ad.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"p","text":"And fewer, bigger ad sets are usually better than many tiny ones. Meta's learning phase guidance says that when you create many ads and ad sets, the system learns less about each one, and that combining similar ad sets also combines learnings. We go deep on this in the Intermediate level.","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Messy versus clean","text":"Messy: \"Campaign 3 copy (2)\". Clean: \"SE, Sales, Face cream, Nov launch\". A client reading the report understands the second in one second.","k":"L5.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Pick a naming pattern now and use it in every drill, plan and portfolio piece. Agencies notice.","k":"L5.5"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"Your first launch checklist.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Before you press publish, run five checks. They catch most beginner disasters.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"One. Is the objective the real business goal? Two. Is tracking working, so Meta can see the result you chose? We cover the Pixel and the Conversions API in Unit 5. Three. Does the budget fit the client's weekly limit, including the daily flex? Four. Do you have more than one genuinely different ad, so the system has something to choose from? Five. Does every ad lead to a page that keeps the ad's promise?","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Then leave it alone. New ad sets go through what Meta calls the learning phase, and frequent edits reset it. Unit 9 explains exactly how that works.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"The two-minute pre-launch call","text":"An account manager reads the five checks out loud with the media buyer before every launch. It takes two minutes and saves days of wasted spend.","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Copy the five checks into your notes. Use them on every drill in this course, and later on every real launch.","k":"L6.4"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["Campaign: objective + buying type (auction). Ad set: audience, placements, budget, schedule. Ad: format, creative, identity.","Six objectives: Awareness, Traffic, Engagement, Leads, App promotion, Sales.","Objective → conversion location (website, app, messaging, calls, forms) → performance goal (what delivery optimises for).","Budget: Advantage+ campaign budget (one budget, shared in real time) or ad set budgets. Daily can flex up to 75% over; ≤175% per day; ≤7× daily per week.","Bid strategies: spend-based (highest volume / value), goal-based (cost per result goal, ROAS goal), manual (bid cap).","Name things: Market, Objective, Product / Audience, Placement / Concept, Format, version."],"src":[{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: What are the advertising levels in Meta Ads Manager?","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/621956575422138"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: How to choose the right ad objective","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/1438417719786914"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About campaign budgets and ad set budgets","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/458847204894307"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About daily budgets","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/190490051321426"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About bid strategies","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/1619591734742116"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About the learning phase","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/112167992830700"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do you choose the objective in Meta Ads Manager?","o":["Campaign level","Ad set level","Ad level","In Events Manager"],"a":0,"e":"The campaign is the top level: objective and buying type."},{"q":"Which settings does Meta list at the ad set level?","o":["Objective and buying type","Audience, placements, budget and schedule","Format, creative and identity","Pixel and Conversions API"],"a":1,"e":"Ad sets control who, where, how much and when. All ads in an ad set share those settings."},{"q":"An online shop wants more purchases and its tracking works. Which objective fits best?","o":["Traffic, because clicks are cheap","Awareness","Sales","Engagement"],"a":2,"e":"The objective tells Meta which people to look for. Sales looks for buyers; Traffic looks for clickers."},{"q":"What is the \"performance goal\"?","o":["The client's yearly revenue target","The specific outcome Ads Manager optimises delivery for, such as purchases","The maximum you will pay per click","The number of ads in an ad set"],"a":1,"e":"Meta defines the performance goal as the specific outcome delivery is optimised for."},{"q":"Your daily budget is 500 kr. According to Meta, what is the most it can spend on a single day?","o":["500 kr exactly","1,000 kr","875 kr (175% of the daily budget)","There is no limit"],"a":2,"e":"Meta may spend up to 75% over the daily budget, but never more than 175% of it in a day (without budget sharing)."},{"q":"Which bid strategy is spend-based?","o":["Highest volume","Cost per result goal","ROAS goal","Bid cap"],"a":0,"e":"Highest volume and highest value are spend-based. Cost per result and ROAS goals are goal-based. Bid cap is manual."},{"q":"Why does Meta advise against creating very many ad sets with tiny budgets?","o":["It is not allowed","The system learns less about each one; combining similar ad sets combines learnings","It makes ads look worse","It turns off Advantage+ placements"],"a":1,"e":"From Meta's learning phase guidance: high ad volumes mean less learning per ad set."}],"drills":[{"type":"sort","title":"Which level?","prompt":["Where do you set each of these in Meta Ads Manager?"],"checks":[],"model":["Campaign = the why (objective). Ad set = who, where, how much, when. Ad = what people see."],"items":["Objective: Sales => Campaign","Audience: Sweden, adults => Ad set","The video and the primary text => Ad","Placements => Ad set","Which Facebook Page and Instagram account the ad runs from => Ad","Schedule: start 1 December => Ad set","Buying type: auction => Campaign","Carousel or single image format => Ad"],"cats":["Campaign","Ad set","Ad"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"sort","title":"Match the goal to the objective","prompt":["A client says each of these. Which of Meta's six objectives fits?"],"checks":[],"model":["Start from the business goal in the client's own words. Note: Meta also lets you reach messaging through other objectives; the match above follows the main goal described."],"items":["\"We just changed our name and want people to remember it.\" => Awareness","\"We want more people reading our new blog guide.\" => Traffic","\"We want people to message us on WhatsApp to ask questions.\" => Engagement","\"We need sign-ups for a free consultation, through a form.\" => Leads","\"We want more installs of our booking app.\" => App promotion","\"We want more online orders from our webshop.\" => Sales"],"cats":["Awareness","Traffic","Engagement","Leads","App promotion","Sales"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"write","title":"Name the campaign","prompt":["Write clean names for a campaign, an ad set and an ad for this brief: a fictional Swedish coffee subscription wants online orders, broad audience in Sweden, testing a UGC video about \"fresh beans in 48 hours\"."],"checks":[{"kind":"maxwords","arg":"10","label":"10 words or fewer"},{"kind":"filled","arg":"","label":"Written"}],"model":["Campaign: SE, Sales, Coffee subscription","Ad set: Broad, Advantage+ placements","Ad: Fresh in 48h, UGC video, v1"],"items":["Campaign name","Ad set name","Ad name"],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t6-1-06","app":"taste","id":"t6-1-06","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t6-1-06","title":"Mixed: which ad set structure fits Meta's Andromeda guidance?","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"6.1"},{"ref":"taste:t1-3-03","app":"taste","id":"t1-3-03","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t1-3-03","title":"Creative is targeting (Andromeda era)","kind":"Mini-lesson","module":"1.3"}],"words":1311,"audio":{"duration":559.684,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. Three boxes inside each other.","s":31.45},{"t":"2. The six objectives.","s":125.65},{"t":"3. Conversion location and performance goal.","s":208.83},{"t":"4. Budget and bid strategy, in one minute.","s":292.34},{"t":"5. Naming and a clean account.","s":390.81},{"t":"6. Your first launch checklist.","s":479.63},{"t":"End","s":551.92}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":5.568},{"k":"lead.0","s":6.468,"d":13.752},{"k":"lead.1","s":20.82,"d":10.032},{"k":"L1.h","s":31.452,"d":3.312},{"k":"L1.0","s":35.264,"d":9.552},{"k":"L1.1","s":45.416,"d":12.552},{"k":"L1.2","s":58.568,"d":12.192},{"k":"L1.3","s":71.36,"d":20.544},{"k":"L1.5","s":92.504,"d":20.64},{"k":"L1.6","s":113.744,"d":11.304},{"k":"L2.h","s":125.648,"d":2.4},{"k":"L2.0","s":128.548,"d":9.264},{"k":"L2.1","s":138.412,"d":29.736},{"k":"L2.2","s":168.748,"d":13.416},{"k":"L2.4","s":182.764,"d":14.544},{"k":"L2.6","s":197.908,"d":10.32},{"k":"L3.h","s":208.828,"d":3.888},{"k":"L3.0","s":213.216,"d":6.744},{"k":"L3.1","s":220.56,"d":13.848},{"k":"L3.2","s":235.008,"d":12.6},{"k":"L3.3","s":248.208,"d":12.288},{"k":"L3.4","s":261.096,"d":18.144},{"k":"L3.5","s":279.84,"d":11.904},{"k":"L4.h","s":292.344,"d":4.224},{"k":"L4.0","s":297.068,"d":18.336},{"k":"L4.1","s":316.004,"d":18.912},{"k":"L4.2","s":335.516,"d":24.864},{"k":"L4.3","s":360.98,"d":7.416},{"k":"L4.5","s":368.996,"d":12.264},{"k":"L4.6","s":381.86,"d":8.352},{"k":"L5.h","s":390.812,"d":2.712},{"k":"L5.0","s":394.024,"d":8.976},{"k":"L5.1","s":403.6,"d":23.472},{"k":"L5.2","s":427.672,"d":7.512},{"k":"L5.3","s":435.784,"d":18.648},{"k":"L5.4","s":455.032,"d":14.976},{"k":"L5.5","s":470.608,"d":8.424},{"k":"L6.h","s":479.632,"d":3.048},{"k":"L6.0","s":483.18,"d":5.592},{"k":"L6.1","s":489.372,"d":29.088},{"k":"L6.2","s":519.06,"d":10.248},{"k":"L6.3","s":529.908,"d":12.84},{"k":"L6.4","s":543.348,"d":7.968},{"k":"end","s":551.916,"d":6.168}],"file":"aa-t02-meta-ads-manager-campaign-ad-set-ad.mp3"}},{"n":3,"level":"Beginner","platform":"Google","kicker":"Beginner · Google · Search structure","title":"Google Search: campaigns, ad groups, keywords and match types","sub":"How a search becomes an ad, and how to stop paying for the wrong searches","goal":["Draw the Google Ads structure: account, campaign, ad group, keywords and ads.","Explain broad, phrase and exact match, and read the brackets and quotes.","Use the search terms report and negative keywords to cut waste."],"lead":["On Google Search the customer tells you what they want, in their own words. Your job is to be there with the right ad when they type it, and to stay away from the searches that will never buy.","Everything here follows the Google Ads Help Center."],"lessons":[{"title":"The structure: account, campaign, ad group.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Google's help centre describes the account as layers. At the top is the account, with access and payment. Inside it are campaigns. Inside each campaign are ad groups. Inside each ad group are keywords and ads.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"The campaign holds the big settings: how much you are willing to spend, the networks, and the locations where your ads can show. Google's advice is simple: when you need different campaign settings, such as a different budget or different locations, create a separate campaign.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"The ad group holds one narrow theme. Google recommends that you pick a narrow theme for each ad group and use keywords related to that theme, and consider mentioning at least one of your keywords in the ad's headline.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d03-google-structure.svg","cap":"Diagram: Google Ads account → campaigns (budget, locations, networks) → ad groups (one theme) → keywords + ads (Google Ads Help, \"Organize your account with ad groups\").","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"A physio clinic near Odenplan","text":"Campaign: \"Search, Stockholm, Physio\", with its own budget and a location setting around the clinic. Ad group one: knee pain, with knee keywords and knee-specific ads. Ad group two: back pain. Ad group three: sports massage. Each ad speaks to exactly what was searched.","k":"L1.4"},{"t":"warn","text":"Google also warns that reorganising an account later wipes out the data you have collected. Plan the structure before you build.","k":"L1.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"Before you build a search campaign, write the website's main pages as a list. Google says your site structure is a good starting point: often one ad group per page or category.","k":"L1.6"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"Keywords are not searches.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"This is the most important idea in Google Search. A keyword is what you add to your account. A search term is what a real person actually typed.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Match types decide how closely a search has to match your keyword for your ad to be considered for the auction.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"That means you never buy a keyword. You buy a family of searches, and the match type decides how big that family is.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"Every time you add a keyword, imagine ten different ways a real person might type that need, including misspellings and longer questions. Ask: which of those do I want, and which do I not want?","k":"L2.3"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"Broad, phrase and exact match.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Google has three match types.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Broad match is the default. Google says your ad may show on searches related to your keyword, which can include searches that don't contain the keyword itself. To find relevant matches, broad match may also look at the user's recent searches, your landing page and the other keywords in your ad group. You type a broad match keyword plainly, with no symbols.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Phrase match shows your ad on searches that include the meaning of your keyword. You write it in quotation marks, like \"tennis shoes\".","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Exact match shows your ad on searches with the same meaning or the same intent as the keyword. You write it in square brackets, like [red shoe]. Google says exact match gives you the most steering over who sees your ad, but reaches the fewest searches.","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"p","text":"And broader types include the narrower ones. A phrase keyword can match everything the same exact keyword would match, and broad matches everything phrase and exact would, plus more.","k":"L3.4"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d03-match-types.svg","cap":"Diagram: exact sits inside phrase, which sits inside broad. Syntax: keyword, \"keyword\", [keyword] (Google Ads Help, \"About keyword matching options\").","k":"L3.5"},{"t":"warn","text":"Google states that it is critical to use Smart Bidding with broad match. Broad match without automated bidding can buy a lot of loosely related searches at the same price. We cover Smart Bidding in Unit 9.","k":"L3.6"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/google-help-match-types.png","cap":"Screenshot: Google Ads Help, \"About keyword matching options\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L3.7"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write one keyword three ways: plain for broad, in quotes for phrase, in brackets for exact. Then write three real searches and decide which match type would catch each.","k":"L3.8"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"Negative keywords and the search terms report.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Negative keywords stop your ads from showing on searches with that term. Google's example: a hat company that doesn't sell baseball hats adds \"baseball hats\" as a negative.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Negatives have their own match rules, and they behave differently. With negative broad, the default, your ad won't show if the search contains all your negative words, in any order. With negative phrase, it won't show if the search contains the words in the same order. With negative exact, it won't show only if the search is exactly those words, with nothing extra. Google also notes that negatives don't automatically cover synonyms or singular and plural forms, so you add those yourself.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Where do you find bad searches? In the search terms report, which shows how your ads performed when triggered by actual searches. Read it every week. Every search that will never buy becomes a negative.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d03-negatives.svg","cap":"Diagram: the weekly loop — read search terms, add winners as keywords, add losers as negatives (Google Ads Help: negative keywords, search terms report).","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"A dental implant ad","text":"The search terms report shows clicks from \"implant dentist jobs\", \"dental implant course\" and \"implant cost abroad\". None of these people want an implant in Stockholm next month. Add \"jobs\", \"course\" and \"abroad\" as negatives.","k":"L4.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Make a starter negative list for every client before launch: jobs, salary, course, free, DIY, and any service the client doesn't offer. Then grow it every week from the report.","k":"L4.5"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"Intent beats volume.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Not all searches are equal. \"Dentist\" might mean someone looking for a job, a school project, or a booking. \"Emergency dentist open now Södermalm\" means someone in pain with a card in hand.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Beginners chase the biggest keywords. Performance marketers chase intent: words like price, near me, book, open now, and the exact product name. Those searches cost more per click, but they convert.","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Google's own help also shows how Search fits with Performance Max, a campaign type you meet in the Expert level. If a search is identical to an exact match keyword in your Search campaign, the Search campaign is prioritised. Your exact keywords are your strongest steering wheel.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"Three searches, three values","text":"\"Running shoes\" is research. \"Best running shoes flat feet\" is comparison. \"Asics Gel-Kayano 31 size 44 price\" is ready to buy. Put your best ad and your highest bids behind the last kind.","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"For any business, write five high-intent searches and five low-intent searches. Your first campaign goes after the high-intent five.","k":"L5.4"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"Worked example: from zero to a first search campaign.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Let's build one together, out loud. The client is a fictional bike repair shop in Södermalm. They fix punctures, service gears and brakes, and they want more walk-in repairs.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Step one, the campaign. One Search campaign, location set around Södermalm and the neighbouring areas their customers come from, and a budget the owner is comfortable with per week.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Step two, ad groups from the website. The site has three service pages: puncture and tyre repair, full service, and brakes. So we build three ad groups, one per page.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Step three, keywords per ad group. For puncture repair, a few close variations of the same need: an exact keyword for the most valuable search, a phrase keyword for the core service, and, once Smart Bidding has conversion data, broad keywords to find new searches.","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"p","text":"Step four, the starter negatives: jobs, course, used bikes for sale, and car repair.","k":"L6.4"},{"t":"p","text":"Step five, the ads. Each ad repeats the need from its ad group, for example \"Puncture fixed while you wait\", and sends people to the matching page, not the homepage.","k":"L6.5"},{"t":"p","text":"Step six, the weekly habit. Every Monday, open the search terms report. Searches that bring repairs become keywords. Searches that never will become negatives.","k":"L6.6"},{"t":"p","text":"That's it. Six steps, and every one of them is something Google's own help centre recommends.","k":"L6.7"},{"t":"apply","text":"Do this six-step build on paper for a business on your street. It takes fifteen minutes and is a perfect portfolio piece.","k":"L6.8"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["Account → campaign (budget, networks, locations) → ad group (one narrow theme) → keywords + ads.","Keyword = what you add. Search term = what the person typed. Match type decides the gap.","Broad: keyword (related searches, default; use Smart Bidding). Phrase: \"keyword\" (includes the meaning). Exact: [keyword] (same meaning or intent).","Negatives: broad (all words, any order), phrase (same order), exact (only that search). Add synonyms and plurals yourself.","Read the search terms report weekly: winners become keywords, losers become negatives.","Exact match keyword identical to the search beats Performance Max for that search."],"src":[{"label":"Google Ads Help: Organize your account with ad groups","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/6372655"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About keyword matching options","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/7478529"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About negative keywords","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/2453972"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About the search terms report","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/2472708"}],"quiz":[{"q":"When should you create a separate Google Ads campaign rather than a new ad group?","o":["When you need different campaign settings, such as a different budget or locations","Whenever you add a new keyword","When you write a new ad","Never; one campaign is always best"],"a":0,"e":"Google: different budget or location settings → separate campaign."},{"q":"How do you write a phrase match keyword?","o":["[running shoes]","\"running shoes\"","+running +shoes","running shoes (no symbols)"],"a":1,"e":"Quotes = phrase. Brackets = exact. No symbols = broad."},{"q":"Which match type reaches the most searches?","o":["Broad match","Phrase match","Exact match","Negative exact match"],"a":0,"e":"Broad is widest and includes what phrase and exact would match, plus related searches."},{"q":"Google says it is critical to pair broad match with…","o":["Manual CPC bids","Smart Bidding","Exact match negatives only","Display campaigns"],"a":1,"e":"Smart Bidding sets the right bid for each query, so broad match doesn't overpay for loose searches."},{"q":"You add the negative broad keyword \"running shoes\". Which search can still show your ad?","o":["shoes running","blue running shoes","running shoe","running shoes"],"a":2,"e":"Negative broad blocks searches containing all the words in any order. \"running shoe\" (singular) is not blocked; add plurals yourself."},{"q":"Where do you see what people actually typed before clicking your ad?","o":["Keyword Planner","The search terms report","Quality Score column","Ads Transparency Center"],"a":1,"e":"The search terms report shows performance by actual search."},{"q":"Which search shows the highest buying intent for a dental clinic?","o":["dentist","dentist salary sweden","emergency dentist open now södermalm","how to become a dentist"],"a":2,"e":"Urgency, location and \"open now\" signal someone ready to book."}],"drills":[{"type":"sort","title":"Broad, phrase or exact?","prompt":["Which match type is each keyword written in?"],"checks":[],"model":["No symbols = broad. \"Quotes\" = phrase. [Brackets] = exact."],"items":["[teeth whitening stockholm] => Exact","\"teeth whitening\" => Phrase","teeth whitening price => Broad","[physio odenplan] => Exact","\"sports massage\" => Phrase","running shoes => Broad"],"cats":["Broad","Phrase","Exact"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"sort","title":"Keep or make it a negative?","prompt":["You run Search ads for a dental implant clinic in Stockholm. These searches appeared in your search terms report. Keep, or add a negative?"],"checks":[],"model":["People researching jobs, courses or DIY will not book. Price questions and \"near me\" are buyers."],"items":["dental implant price stockholm => Keep","dental implant jobs => Add negative","implant dentist near me => Keep","dental implant course online => Add negative","how much is one tooth implant => Keep","dental implant diy kit => Add negative"],"cats":["Keep","Add negative"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"write","title":"Your first ad group","prompt":["Build one ad group for a fictional bike repair shop in Södermalm. Write the ad group theme, three keywords with different match types, and three negative keywords."],"checks":[{"kind":"filled","arg":"","label":"Written"},{"kind":"include","arg":"[,\",near,repair,service","label":"Uses match-type symbols or service words"}],"model":["Theme: puncture and tyre repair","Keywords: [bike puncture repair södermalm] · \"bike tyre repair\" · bike repair near me","Negatives: jobs · course · used bikes for sale"],"items":["Ad group theme","Three keywords (use plain, \"quotes\" and [brackets])","Three negative keywords"],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t10-2-01","app":"taste","id":"t10-2-01","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t10-2-01","title":"Keywords, match types and negatives","kind":"Mini-lesson","module":"10.2"},{"ref":"taste:t10-2-02","app":"taste","id":"t10-2-02","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t10-2-02","title":"Which negative keyword list fits a dental clinic's search campai…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"10.2"},{"ref":"taste:t10-2-04","app":"taste","id":"t10-2-04","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t10-2-04","title":"Search term report shows your 'tandimplantat' ad got clicks from…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"10.2"},{"ref":"taste:t10-2-03","app":"taste","id":"t10-2-03","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t10-2-03","title":"Which search shows the highest commercial intent for a physio cl…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"10.2"}],"words":1319,"audio":{"duration":548.668,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. The structure: account, campaign, ad group.","s":22.69},{"t":"2. Keywords are not searches.","s":120.13},{"t":"3. Broad, phrase and exact match.","s":162.36},{"t":"4. Negative keywords and the search terms report.","s":258.7},{"t":"5. Intent beats volume.","s":354.74},{"t":"6. Worked example: from zero to a first search campaign.","s":433.62},{"t":"End","s":540.52}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":6.24},{"k":"lead.0","s":7.14,"d":10.872},{"k":"lead.1","s":18.612,"d":3.48},{"k":"L1.h","s":22.692,"d":4.488},{"k":"L1.0","s":27.68,"d":14.928},{"k":"L1.1","s":43.208,"d":16.056},{"k":"L1.2","s":59.864,"d":12.624},{"k":"L1.4","s":73.088,"d":23.424},{"k":"L1.5","s":97.112,"d":9.0},{"k":"L1.6","s":106.712,"d":12.816},{"k":"L2.h","s":120.128,"d":2.784},{"k":"L2.0","s":123.412,"d":9.216},{"k":"L2.1","s":133.228,"d":6.384},{"k":"L2.2","s":140.212,"d":7.176},{"k":"L2.3","s":147.988,"d":13.776},{"k":"L3.h","s":162.364,"d":4.008},{"k":"L3.0","s":166.872,"d":2.136},{"k":"L3.1","s":169.608,"d":21.744},{"k":"L3.2","s":191.952,"d":7.776},{"k":"L3.3","s":200.328,"d":16.32},{"k":"L3.4","s":217.248,"d":11.88},{"k":"L3.6","s":229.728,"d":14.592},{"k":"L3.8","s":244.92,"d":13.176},{"k":"L4.h","s":258.696,"d":4.056},{"k":"L4.0","s":263.252,"d":11.736},{"k":"L4.1","s":275.588,"d":31.896},{"k":"L4.2","s":308.084,"d":12.72},{"k":"L4.4","s":321.404,"d":17.448},{"k":"L4.5","s":339.452,"d":14.688},{"k":"L5.h","s":354.74,"d":2.784},{"k":"L5.0","s":358.024,"d":12.816},{"k":"L5.1","s":371.44,"d":14.4},{"k":"L5.2","s":386.44,"d":17.376},{"k":"L5.3","s":404.416,"d":17.784},{"k":"L5.4","s":422.8,"d":10.224},{"k":"L6.h","s":433.624,"d":5.04},{"k":"L6.0","s":439.164,"d":11.064},{"k":"L6.1","s":450.828,"d":11.28},{"k":"L6.2","s":462.708,"d":12.408},{"k":"L6.3","s":475.716,"d":17.808},{"k":"L6.4","s":494.124,"d":6.336},{"k":"L6.5","s":501.06,"d":11.112},{"k":"L6.6","s":512.772,"d":11.184},{"k":"L6.7","s":524.556,"d":6.216},{"k":"L6.8","s":531.372,"d":8.544},{"k":"end","s":540.516,"d":6.552}],"file":"aa-t03-google-search-campaigns-ad-groups-keywor.mp3"}},{"n":4,"level":"Beginner","platform":"Both","kicker":"Beginner · Meta + Google · Copy","title":"Writing the ad: hook, headline, offer and call to action","sub":"The parts of a Meta ad and a Google search ad, and the direct-response rules that make each part sell","goal":["Name the parts of a Meta feed ad and a Google responsive search ad.","Write a hook that calls out a specific reader and a specific outcome.","Replace vague claims with specific, checkable facts, and end with one clear action."],"lead":["The platforms decide who sees your ad. The words and the creative decide whether anyone cares. This unit gives you the parts of an ad and the old, proven rules for writing each part.","Every rep you do in the Copy Workshop later trains exactly these parts."],"lessons":[{"title":"The parts of a Meta ad.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"A standard Meta feed ad has a few parts, and each has one job.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"The primary text sits above the image or video. Its first line is your hook: it has to make the right person stop. The creative, meaning the image or video, does the heavy lifting in the feed. The headline sits under the creative, next to the button. The call-to-action button, such as Shop now or Learn more, tells people what happens when they tap.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta's ads guide gives text recommendations for an image ad in the Facebook feed: primary text of fifty to one hundred and fifty characters, and a headline of about twenty-seven characters, with a four by five image. Those are recommendations, not hard limits. The lesson: the first line and the headline have to work in very few words.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d04-meta-ad-anatomy.svg","cap":"Diagram (not a real ad): the parts of a Meta feed ad. Text recommendations from Meta's Ads Guide, Facebook Feed image ads.","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Before you write anything, give each part its job in one line. Hook: stop the right person. Creative: show it. Headline: the promise. Button: the next step.","k":"L1.4"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"The parts of a Google search ad.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"On Google Search, the main text format is the responsive search ad. You write several headlines and several descriptions, and Google tests combinations.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Google's help centre gives the limits. You can enter up to fifteen headlines and four descriptions. You need at least three headlines and two descriptions. Each headline can be up to thirty characters, each description up to ninety, and the two display path fields up to fifteen each.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Thirty characters is tiny. \"Emergency dentist, open today\" is twenty-nine. So every headline has to earn its place: the keyword, the benefit, the proof, the offer, or the next step.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d04-rsa-anatomy.svg","cap":"Diagram (not a real ad): a responsive search ad's headlines, descriptions and display path, with Google's character limits.","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Headlines with different jobs","text":"\"Physio near Odenplan\" repeats the search. \"Appointments within 48 hours\" is a specific benefit. \"4.8 stars from 210 reviews\" is proof, written here with placeholder numbers. \"Book online in 2 minutes\" is the next step. Four headlines, four jobs.","k":"L2.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write headlines in families: keyword, benefit, proof, offer, action. If two headlines say the same thing in different words, delete one.","k":"L2.5"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"The hook: call out one reader and one outcome.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Claude Hopkins and Victor Schwab both taught that the headline's job is to pick the reader. In a feed, the first line of text and the first second of video are your headline.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"A strong hook does two things fast. It tells the right person \"this is for you\". And it promises something they want.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Weak: \"Exciting news from our clinic!\" Strong: \"Runners in Stockholm: knee pain after five kilometres?\" Hold on, that one asks about a health condition, and Meta's rules forbid ads that assert or imply a person's medical condition. A policy-safe version: \"Knee pain after long runs is common. Here's what our physios check first.\" Same reader, same outcome, no assumption about the viewer.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"p","text":"We cover policy properly in Unit 11. For now, the habit: name the situation, not the person's condition.","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Three hooks for a coffee subscription","text":"\"Your coffee was roasted months ago. Ours was roasted on Tuesday.\" \"Fresh beans, delivered within 48 hours of roasting.\" \"The coffee shop taste, at about half the price per cup,\" which only works if the client can prove it.","k":"L3.4"},{"t":"warn","text":"Never invent a number to make a hook stronger. Use placeholders like [X days] in drafts, and fill them only with facts the client can prove.","k":"L3.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write five hooks for one product, each from a different angle: the problem, the outcome, a number, a comparison, a question. Then cut the vague ones.","k":"L3.6"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"Specific beats clever.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Hopkins wrote that general claims slide off the reader, while a definite fact is believed. Specific claims sound measured. Vague ones sound like advertising.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Run every line through one test: could any competitor say this exact sentence? If yes, it says nothing. \"High quality coffee\" fits every coffee brand on earth. \"Roasted in Västerås, shipped the same week\" belongs to one.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Then add reason-why. Tell people why the claim is true. \"Same-week shipping, because we roast to order instead of storing bags in a warehouse.\"","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"Before and after","text":"Before: \"Premium skincare for amazing results.\" After: \"Fragrance-free, so it won't sting dry winter skin. Thirty-day money-back if it does.\" The second has a reader, a reason and a promise you can test.","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Circle every adjective in your draft: amazing, premium, best, innovative. Replace each one with a fact, a number or a demonstration, or delete it.","k":"L4.4"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"The offer and the call to action.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Your offer is what the person gets, what it costs, and why it's safe to say yes. A good offer often beats a good line.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Make it concrete: a price, a free first step, a guarantee. \"Free fifteen-minute patch test before you book\" removes fear. \"Thirty days to return it, we pay the shipping\" removes risk.","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Then ask for one action. One button, one next step. Two choices make people choose neither.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"p","text":"On Meta the button is a fixed label, so your text sets it up: \"Tap Shop now to get the starter kit.\" On Google, one of your headlines can be the action: \"Book online in 2 minutes\".","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write your offer as one sentence with three parts: what they get, what it costs, and the risk you remove. If any part is missing, it's not an offer yet.","k":"L5.4"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"Worked example: one ad, built part by part.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"A fictional Swedish coffee subscription wants online orders. Let's write a Meta ad, part by part.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Reader: people who drink coffee every day and buy supermarket beans. Outcome: coffee that tastes fresh.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Hook, the first line of primary text: \"Supermarket coffee can sit on a shelf for months. Ours ships the week it's roasted.\"","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Body, the reason-why: \"We roast to order in small batches, so the beans reach you while they still smell like a café.\"","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"p","text":"Offer: \"First bag at half price. Skip or cancel any month.\"","k":"L6.4"},{"t":"p","text":"Call to action: \"Tap Shop now to choose your roast.\"","k":"L6.5"},{"t":"p","text":"Headline under the creative: \"Roasted this week\". That's seventeen characters, well inside the twenty-seven Meta recommends.","k":"L6.6"},{"t":"p","text":"Creative idea: a phone-filmed clip of a bag being opened, with the roast date on screen.","k":"L6.7"},{"t":"p","text":"Then the honesty check: is \"ships the week it's roasted\" true? If the client can't promise it, change it before it goes live.","k":"L6.8"},{"t":"apply","text":"Build one ad with the same six parts for a product you own. Keep it under the recommended lengths. Then rewrite the hook three more ways and keep the best.","k":"L6.9"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["Meta feed ad: primary text (first line = hook), creative, headline, call-to-action button. Feed image ad guide: primary text 50–150 characters, headline about 27, 4:5 image.","Google RSA: up to 15 headlines (min 3, ≤30 characters), up to 4 descriptions (min 2, ≤90), 2 paths (≤15).","Hook = one reader + one outcome. Name the situation, never the person's health condition (Meta policy).","Specific beats clever: could a competitor say it? Add reason-why.","Offer = what they get + what it costs + risk removed. One call to action.","Never invent numbers: placeholders until the client proves them."],"src":[{"label":"Meta Ads Guide: Facebook Feed image ad specs","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/ads-guide/update/image/facebook-feed"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About responsive search ads","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/7684791"},{"label":"Meta Transparency Center: Privacy violations and personal attributes","url":"https://transparency.meta.com/policies/ad-standards/objectionable-content/privacy-violations-personal-attributes/"},{"label":"Claude C. Hopkins, Scientific Advertising (1923), Library of Congress","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/23009362/"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the job of the first line of Meta primary text?","o":["To be the hook that makes the right person stop","To list every product feature","To show the price only","To thank the reader"],"a":0,"e":"In the feed, the first line works like a headline: it picks the reader."},{"q":"What is the maximum length of a responsive search ad headline?","o":["15 characters","30 characters","60 characters","90 characters"],"a":1,"e":"Headlines up to 30, descriptions up to 90, paths up to 15 (Google Ads Help)."},{"q":"How many headlines can one responsive search ad have?","o":["Exactly 3","Up to 5","Up to 15","Unlimited"],"a":2,"e":"Minimum 3, maximum 15 headlines; 2 to 4 descriptions."},{"q":"Which hook is policy-safe on Meta for a physio clinic?","o":["\"Do you suffer from knee pain?\"","\"Your bad knees are holding you back.\"","\"Knee pain after long runs is common. Here's what our physios check first.\"","\"We know you have arthritis.\""],"a":2,"e":"Meta forbids implying knowledge of a viewer's medical condition. Name the situation, not the person's condition."},{"q":"Which line follows Hopkins' rule about specifics?","o":["\"Premium coffee for true coffee lovers.\"","\"Roasted to order, shipped the same week.\"","\"The best coffee you'll ever taste.\"","\"Coffee, reinvented.\""],"a":1,"e":"A definite, checkable fact is believed; a generality is ignored."},{"q":"What three parts make a complete offer?","o":["What they get, what it costs, and the risk you remove","Logo, slogan and colour","Headline, hashtag and emoji","Audience, budget and schedule"],"a":0,"e":"An offer answers: what do I get, what does it cost, why is it safe?"},{"q":"Why ask for only one action?","o":["Meta only allows one sentence of text","Two choices make people choose neither","Google bans multiple links","It lowers CPM automatically"],"a":1,"e":"One clear next step converts better than a menu of choices."}],"drills":[{"type":"pick","title":"Pick the stronger line","prompt":["Which line would sell better? Think: reader, specific outcome, proof."],"checks":[],"model":[],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[{"a":"\"Exciting news from our café!\"","b":"\"New: oat flat white for 39 kr, until Sunday.\"","ok":"B","e":"Specific product, price and deadline. The first says nothing."},{"a":"\"Fragrance-free cream that won't sting dry winter skin.\"","b":"\"Amazing skincare for amazing skin.\"","ok":"A","e":"A reader, a problem and a reason. The second is pure adjectives."},{"a":"\"Book online in 2 minutes.\"","b":"\"Contact us today for more information about our services and offers.\"","ok":"A","e":"One clear, easy action. The second is long and vague."},{"a":"\"Up to [X] litres per cylinder, no electricity needed.\"","b":"\"The ultimate sparkling water experience.\"","ok":"A","e":"A checkable fact (with a placeholder until verified) beats hype."}],"calc":[]},{"type":"write","title":"Five hooks, five angles","prompt":["Product: a fictional fragrance-free face cream for dry winter skin, 249 kr, 30-day money-back guarantee. Write five hooks (first line of primary text), one per angle."],"checks":[{"kind":"maxwords","arg":"20","label":"20 words or fewer"},{"kind":"avoid","arg":"","label":"No vague hype words"},{"kind":"startsnot","arg":"do you have,are you suffering","label":"Doesn't ask about the viewer's condition"}],"model":["Problem: \"Winter air, radiators, and a face that stings by noon.\"","Outcome: \"Soft skin by morning, without the perfume smell.\"","Fact: \"Zero fragrance. 30 days to return it if it stings.\"","Comparison: \"Thick like a balm, absorbs like a lotion.\"","Question: \"Why does most face cream sting in January? Usually: fragrance.\""],"items":["Problem","Outcome","Number or fact","Comparison","Question (policy-safe: no \"do you have…\")"],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"write","title":"Three RSA headlines","prompt":["Search: \"physio odenplan\". Write three headlines of 30 characters or fewer: one keyword, one benefit, one action. (Use placeholders for numbers you can't prove.)"],"checks":[{"kind":"maxwords","arg":"6","label":"Short (6 words or fewer)"},{"kind":"avoid","arg":"","label":"No vague hype words"}],"model":["Keyword: \"Physio at Odenplan\"","Benefit: \"Appointments within [X] hours\"","Action: \"Book online in 2 minutes\""],"items":["Keyword headline","Benefit headline","Action headline"],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"ws:w:w1-028","app":"ws","id":"w:w1-028","url":"workshop/index.html?embed=1#/w/w1-028","title":"Meta ad headline — Queen West Barbers","kind":"Copy rep"},{"ref":"ws:w:w1-036","app":"ws","id":"w:w1-036","url":"workshop/index.html?embed=1#/w/w1-036","title":"Meta ad headline — Östermalm Dental English","kind":"Copy rep"},{"ref":"ws:w:w2-071","app":"ws","id":"w:w2-071","url":"workshop/index.html?embed=1#/w/w2-071","title":"Primary text: hook line — Bow Strength Studio","kind":"Copy rep"},{"ref":"taste:t2-1-01","app":"taste","id":"t2-1-01","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t2-1-01","title":"Upworthy headline test (000788)","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"2.1"},{"ref":"taste:t10-1-02","app":"taste","id":"t10-1-02","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t10-1-02","title":"Search: 'invisalign pris'. Which headline best matches the intent?","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"10.1"}],"words":1170,"audio":{"duration":491.14,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. The parts of a Meta ad.","s":26.1},{"t":"2. The parts of a Google search ad.","s":92.31},{"t":"3. The hook: call out one reader and one outcome.","s":174.27},{"t":"4. Specific beats clever.","s":277.58},{"t":"5. The offer and the call to action.","s":349.99},{"t":"6. Worked example: one ad, built part by part.","s":405.98},{"t":"End","s":488.82}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":6.144},{"k":"lead.0","s":7.044,"d":12.144},{"k":"lead.1","s":19.788,"d":5.712},{"k":"L1.h","s":26.1,"d":2.304},{"k":"L1.0","s":28.904,"d":4.584},{"k":"L1.1","s":34.088,"d":22.728},{"k":"L1.2","s":57.416,"d":21.768},{"k":"L1.4","s":79.784,"d":11.928},{"k":"L2.h","s":92.312,"d":2.832},{"k":"L2.0","s":95.644,"d":10.008},{"k":"L2.1","s":106.252,"d":18.576},{"k":"L2.2","s":125.428,"d":13.512},{"k":"L2.4","s":139.54,"d":22.704},{"k":"L2.5","s":162.844,"d":10.824},{"k":"L3.h","s":174.268,"d":4.176},{"k":"L3.0","s":178.944,"d":11.064},{"k":"L3.1","s":190.608,"d":7.944},{"k":"L3.2","s":199.152,"d":27.168},{"k":"L3.3","s":226.92,"d":8.28},{"k":"L3.4","s":235.8,"d":18.48},{"k":"L3.5","s":254.88,"d":10.104},{"k":"L3.6","s":265.584,"d":11.4},{"k":"L4.h","s":277.584,"d":2.952},{"k":"L4.0","s":281.036,"d":10.44},{"k":"L4.1","s":292.076,"d":16.392},{"k":"L4.2","s":309.068,"d":9.192},{"k":"L4.3","s":318.86,"d":17.352},{"k":"L4.4","s":336.812,"d":12.576},{"k":"L5.h","s":349.988,"d":3.384},{"k":"L5.0","s":353.872,"d":6.936},{"k":"L5.1","s":361.408,"d":13.176},{"k":"L5.2","s":375.184,"d":6.816},{"k":"L5.3","s":382.6,"d":11.544},{"k":"L5.4","s":394.744,"d":10.632},{"k":"L6.h","s":405.976,"d":4.656},{"k":"L6.0","s":411.132,"d":6.624},{"k":"L6.1","s":418.356,"d":7.872},{"k":"L6.2","s":426.828,"d":8.352},{"k":"L6.3","s":435.78,"d":7.776},{"k":"L6.4","s":444.156,"d":4.8},{"k":"L6.5","s":449.556,"d":3.6},{"k":"L6.6","s":453.756,"d":8.16},{"k":"L6.7","s":462.516,"d":5.808},{"k":"L6.8","s":468.924,"d":7.872},{"k":"L6.9","s":477.396,"d":10.824},{"k":"end","s":488.82,"d":0.72}],"file":"aa-t04-writing-the-ad-hook-headline-offer-and-c.mp3"}},{"n":5,"level":"Beginner","platform":"Both","kicker":"Beginner · Meta + Google · Tracking","title":"Conversion tracking: Pixel, Conversions API, Google tag and GA4","sub":"Why the algorithm is only as smart as the data you send it, and how the tracking pieces fit together","goal":["Explain what the Meta Pixel and the Conversions API do, and why Meta recommends using both.","Explain deduplication with event name and event ID.","Explain the Google tag, primary versus secondary conversions, enhanced conversions and consent mode."],"lead":["Here's the uncomfortable truth: if tracking is broken, nothing else in this course works. Smart bidding, Advantage+ and every report all depend on one thing: the platform seeing who actually bought.","This is the unit that juniors skip and seniors check first. It's short, and it will save you from the most expensive mistakes in the job."],"lessons":[{"title":"Why tracking is the steering wheel.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"When you choose the Sales objective on Meta, or a conversion bid strategy on Google, you are telling the machine: find more people like the ones who bought.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"But the machine only knows who bought if your website tells it. That message is called a conversion event: a purchase, a lead, a booking.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"If events are missing, the system optimises on half the truth. If events are counted twice, it thinks bad ads are good. Either way your budget goes to the wrong place, and your reports lie to the client.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"The silent disaster","text":"A shop changes its checkout page. The purchase event stops firing. Ads Manager shows zero sales for three days, so the system starts treating every ad as a failure. Nothing was wrong with the ads. The tracking broke.","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Make it a habit: on the first day with any account, check that the main conversion is firing and that the numbers roughly match the client's own sales system.","k":"L1.4"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"The Meta Pixel.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Meta's help centre describes the Meta Pixel as a piece of code you put on your website. It logs when someone takes an action, called an event, such as adding an item to their cart or making a purchase.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"You see these events in Events Manager. Then you can use them in three ways that Meta lists: to show ads to the right people, including custom audiences of people who visited or acted on your site; to drive more sales, by letting automatic bidding find people likely to take the action you care about; and to measure the results of your ads.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta also sets two rules you must know. Don't share prohibited information with Meta through the Pixel; Meta names health, financial and children's data as examples. And don't put your Pixel on websites you don't own without permission.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"warn","text":"For clinic and health clients this matters a lot. Event names, URLs and parameters can accidentally reveal health information, for example a URL like \"/book/eczema-treatment\". Review what the Pixel sends before you launch.","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/meta-help-pixel.png","cap":"Screenshot: Meta Business Help Center, \"About Meta Pixel\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L2.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"When you audit a site, write down which events fire on which page: page view, add to cart, purchase, lead. And check that the purchase event sends a value and currency, so you can measure revenue.","k":"L2.5"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"The Conversions API, and why you use both.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"The Pixel runs in the visitor's browser. Browsers, ad blockers and privacy settings can stop it.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"The Conversions API sends events from somewhere else: Meta describes it as a direct connection between your marketing data, from your server, website platform, app or CRM, and Meta's systems. It can handle website events, app events, offline events, such as in-store sales, and messaging events. Meta notes that many integrations don't need a developer, for example through e-commerce platform integrations.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta's developer documentation recommends running the Conversions API alongside the Pixel, and calls this a redundant setup. Browser plus server means fewer lost events.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d05-pixel-capi.svg","cap":"Diagram: the same purchase sent from the browser (Pixel) and the server (Conversions API), matched and counted once (Meta Business Help Center and Meta for Developers).","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"In an interview, say it in one line: \"The Pixel tracks in the browser, the Conversions API sends the same events from the server, and deduplication makes sure each purchase counts once.\"","k":"L3.4"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"Deduplication: count each sale once.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"If the same purchase arrives twice, once from the Pixel and once from the server, Meta needs to know it's one purchase.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta's recommended method: send the same event name, for example Purchase, and the same event ID from both sources. In the Pixel the field is called eventID, in the Conversions API it's event underscore id. When they match, Meta deduplicates them. Meta's documentation says matching events received within forty-eight hours are deduplicated, and it generally keeps the one received first. A second method uses the fbp browser ID or an external ID.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta's help centre explains why it matters: without deduplication you may see artificially high conversion numbers, which inflates conversion rate and ROAS, and misleads optimisation.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"A good event ID","text":"The order number is perfect: \"order 10482\" is generated once and both the browser and the server can send it. Two different random IDs, one per side, can never match.","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"warn","text":"If ROAS suddenly looks amazing right after someone \"improved tracking\", check deduplication before you celebrate.","k":"L4.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"When you check an account, compare purchases in Ads Manager with real orders from the shop for the same week. Way more in Ads Manager is a red flag for double counting.","k":"L4.5"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"Google: the Google tag and conversion actions.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"On Google Ads, website conversions are tracked with the Google tag. Google's help centre says the Google tag goes on all pages of the website, and works together with an event snippet that tells it when a conversion happens, or a phone snippet that tracks calls.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Each thing you count is a conversion action. Google groups them into goals, and splits them into two kinds. Primary actions appear in the Conversions column and are used for bidding. Secondary actions are for observation only: they appear in the All conversions column, but are not used for bidding.","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Get this wrong and Smart Bidding optimises for the wrong thing. If \"page view\" is primary, Google will happily buy you page views.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"p","text":"You can also create conversions from Google Analytics 4 events and share them with Google Ads. Google notes these are secondary by default and can only be made primary inside Google Ads.","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d05-google-conversions.svg","cap":"Diagram: Google tag + event snippet → conversion actions → primary (bidding) vs secondary (observation) (Google Ads Help).","k":"L5.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"In every Google Ads audit, open the conversion actions and ask one question about each: should Google bid for this? Only real business outcomes should be primary.","k":"L5.5"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"Enhanced conversions and consent mode.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Two more tools close the gaps.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Enhanced conversions, on Google, supplement your conversion tags by sending hashed first-party data, such as an email address, name, address or phone number that the customer gave you, to improve measurement accuracy. Hashed means it's scrambled before it's sent. There are versions for website conversions and for leads that close later, offline.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Consent mode, on Google, passes each visitor's cookie consent choice to Google's tags, so the tags respect it. Google is clear that consent mode doesn't provide a consent banner: you still need one. There are two versions. In basic mode, Google tags don't load until the visitor makes a choice, and Google uses a general model to estimate missing conversions. In advanced mode, tags load and send cookieless pings while consent is denied, which supports a model specific to your account.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"p","text":"For Swedish and other European clients, consent is not optional. Google's help mentions regional differences for the European Economic Area, Switzerland and the UK.","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"When a European client's conversions look low, ask two questions: is there a consent banner connected to consent mode, and is enhanced conversions on?","k":"L6.4"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["No tracking, no optimisation. Check the main conversion on day one.","Meta Pixel: website code, logs events (add to cart, purchase); never send health, financial or children's data.","Conversions API: server, platform or CRM → Meta. Use with the Pixel (redundant setup).","Dedup: same event name + same event ID (Pixel eventID = CAPI event_id); within 48 hours; first received kept.","Google tag on all pages + event snippet (or phone snippet).","Primary conversions = reported in Conversions and used for bidding. Secondary = observation only (All conversions). GA4-created conversions start as secondary.","Enhanced conversions: hashed first-party data. Consent mode: passes consent; basic vs advanced; needs a banner."],"src":[{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About Meta Pixel","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/742478679120153"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About Conversions API","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/2041148702652965"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About deduplication for Meta Pixel and Conversions API events","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/823677331451951"},{"label":"Meta for Developers: Handling duplicate Pixel and Conversions API events","url":"https://developers.facebook.com/documentation/ads-commerce/conversions-api/deduplicate-pixel-and-server-events"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: Use the Google tag for conversion tracking","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/7548399"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About primary and secondary conversion actions","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/11461796"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About enhanced conversions","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/9888656"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About consent mode","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/10000067"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Meta Pixel do?","o":["It's website code that logs actions (events) people take, such as purchases","It's a design tool for ad images","It sets your daily budget","It blocks bots from clicking your ads"],"a":0,"e":"Meta: a piece of code on your website that logs events."},{"q":"Why does Meta recommend using the Conversions API together with the Pixel?","o":["Because the Pixel is being removed next year","Browser tracking can be blocked; sending events from the server too means fewer lost events","It doubles your conversions in reporting","It's required for Awareness campaigns"],"a":1,"e":"A \"redundant setup\": browser + server. Deduplication stops double counting."},{"q":"For Meta to deduplicate a Pixel and a Conversions API purchase, what must match?","o":["The ad name and the campaign ID","The event name and the event ID","The price and the time to the second","The customer's age and city"],"a":1,"e":"Same event name (e.g. Purchase) and matching eventID / event_id."},{"q":"Which information must never be sent to Meta through the Pixel?","o":["Page views","Add to cart events","Prohibited information such as health, financial or children's data","Purchase value and currency"],"a":2,"e":"Meta names health, financial and children's data as prohibited information."},{"q":"In Google Ads, what is a secondary conversion action?","o":["An action counted twice","An action for observation only, reported in All conversions but not used for bidding","An action only tracked on mobile","An action Smart Bidding prioritises"],"a":1,"e":"Primary = Conversions column + bidding. Secondary = observation."},{"q":"What does consent mode do?","o":["It shows a cookie banner automatically","It passes visitors' consent choices to Google tags so they respect them","It turns off all tracking in Europe","It makes conversions primary"],"a":1,"e":"Google: consent mode doesn't provide a banner; it interacts with yours."},{"q":"ROAS doubled the day after a tracking change, with no change to ads. What should you check first?","o":["Raise the budget immediately","Whether purchases are now counted twice (deduplication)","Whether the ad images loaded","Whether the campaign name changed"],"a":1,"e":"Double counting inflates conversions and ROAS. Compare with the shop's real orders."}],"drills":[{"type":"order","title":"Follow one purchase","prompt":["Put the journey of one tracked purchase in the right order."],"checks":[],"model":["One action, two messages, one count. The order number is the shared ID."],"items":["The person clicks the ad and lands on the shop","They buy; the order gets a number, e.g. 10482","The browser Pixel sends Purchase with eventID 10482","The server sends Purchase with event_id 10482 through the Conversions API","Meta sees matching name and ID and counts one purchase","The ad set gets credit, and delivery learns who buys"],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"sort","title":"Primary or secondary?","prompt":["A webshop's Google Ads account. Which conversion actions should be primary (used for bidding)?"],"checks":[],"model":["Primary = the real business outcome you want Smart Bidding to find more of. Everything else is for observation, unless the client explicitly values it."],"items":["Purchase => Primary","Page view of the About page => Secondary","Newsletter sign-up (low value) => Secondary","Add to cart => Secondary","Purchase via phone order (tracked) => Primary","Scrolled 50% of a blog post => Secondary"],"cats":["Primary","Secondary"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"pick","title":"Healthy or broken tracking?","prompt":["Which situation looks healthy?"],"checks":[],"model":[],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[{"a":"Ads Manager: 210 purchases last week. Shop system: 400 orders, half from ads (about 200).","b":"Ads Manager: 410 purchases last week. Shop system: 400 orders in total.","ok":"A","e":"Ads can't drive more purchases than the shop received. 410 vs 400 total screams double counting."},{"a":"Purchase event sends value and currency.","b":"Purchase event sends no value.","ok":"A","e":"Without value you can't measure ROAS or use value-based bidding."}],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t11-1-08","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-08","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-08","title":"What's missing or wrong? Tick everything that applies, then reve…","kind":"What's missing?","module":"11.1"}],"words":1216,"audio":{"duration":513.7,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. Why tracking is the steering wheel.","s":32.08},{"t":"2. The Meta Pixel.","s":98.34},{"t":"3. The Conversions API, and why you use both.","s":182.45},{"t":"4. Deduplication: count each sale once.","s":248.9},{"t":"5. Google: the Google tag and conversion actions.","s":345.07},{"t":"6. Enhanced conversions and consent mode.","s":419.92},{"t":"End","s":505.57}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":7.44},{"k":"lead.0","s":8.34,"d":14.16},{"k":"lead.1","s":23.1,"d":8.376},{"k":"L1.h","s":32.076,"d":3.24},{"k":"L1.0","s":35.816,"d":9.144},{"k":"L1.1","s":45.56,"d":9.072},{"k":"L1.2","s":55.232,"d":13.2},{"k":"L1.3","s":69.032,"d":17.688},{"k":"L1.4","s":87.32,"d":10.416},{"k":"L2.h","s":98.336,"d":2.28},{"k":"L2.0","s":101.116,"d":11.736},{"k":"L2.1","s":113.452,"d":20.832},{"k":"L2.2","s":134.884,"d":15.024},{"k":"L2.3","s":150.508,"d":16.872},{"k":"L2.5","s":167.98,"d":13.872},{"k":"L3.h","s":182.452,"d":4.44},{"k":"L3.0","s":187.392,"d":6.648},{"k":"L3.1","s":194.64,"d":27.768},{"k":"L3.2","s":223.008,"d":11.64},{"k":"L3.4","s":235.248,"d":13.056},{"k":"L4.h","s":248.904,"d":4.272},{"k":"L4.0","s":253.676,"d":7.8},{"k":"L4.1","s":262.076,"d":31.224},{"k":"L4.2","s":293.9,"d":12.456},{"k":"L4.3","s":306.956,"d":15.84},{"k":"L4.4","s":323.396,"d":9.456},{"k":"L4.5","s":333.452,"d":11.016},{"k":"L5.h","s":345.068,"d":4.512},{"k":"L5.0","s":350.08,"d":15.888},{"k":"L5.1","s":366.568,"d":18.216},{"k":"L5.2","s":385.384,"d":8.928},{"k":"L5.3","s":394.912,"d":12.048},{"k":"L5.5","s":407.56,"d":11.76},{"k":"L6.h","s":419.92,"d":3.84},{"k":"L6.0","s":424.26,"d":2.352},{"k":"L6.1","s":427.212,"d":21.792},{"k":"L6.2","s":449.604,"d":32.496},{"k":"L6.3","s":482.7,"d":11.304},{"k":"L6.4","s":494.604,"d":10.368},{"k":"end","s":505.572,"d":6.528}],"file":"aa-t05-conversion-tracking-pixel-conversions-ap.mp3"}},{"n":6,"level":"Beginner","platform":"Both","kicker":"Beginner · Meta + Google · Metrics","title":"Metrics 101: CPM, CTR, CPC, CVR, CPA and ROAS","sub":"Six numbers, one chain. Read any ad report in two minutes and know where the problem is.","goal":["Calculate CPM, CTR, CPC, conversion rate, CPA and ROAS from raw numbers.","Explain how the metrics link together into one chain from impression to profit.","Work out break-even ROAS from a margin, and read frequency, hook rate and hold rate."],"lead":["Every report you will ever see, on Meta or Google, is built from the same handful of numbers. Learn the chain once and you can walk into any account.","You will do the maths yourself in this unit. It's simple division, and the drills give you unlimited practice."],"lessons":[{"title":"The chain: from impression to profit.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"An ad's life has four steps. It gets shown, that's an impression. Someone clicks. Someone converts: buys, books, signs up. And the conversion brings in money.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Every metric in this unit describes one link in that chain. Cost metrics tell you what each step cost. Rate metrics tell you how many people made it to the next step.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"This is why the chain is so useful: when results get worse, one link broke. Your job is to find which one, instead of guessing.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d06-metric-chain.svg","cap":"Diagram: the metric chain. Impressions → clicks → conversions → revenue, with the metric for each step (own diagram).","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"When you open a report, read it left to right in chain order: CPM, CTR, CPC, conversion rate, CPA, ROAS. Never start with ROAS alone.","k":"L1.4"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"CPM and CTR: getting seen and getting clicked.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"CPM is cost per thousand impressions. Meta's help centre defines it as the average cost for one thousand impressions: the amount spent, divided by impressions, multiplied by a thousand. The M is the Roman numeral for a thousand. If you spend two thousand kronor for eighty thousand impressions, the CPM is twenty-five kronor.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"CPM tells you what attention costs. Because ads are sold in an auction, it rises when more advertisers compete for the same people, which is why many buyers expect higher CPMs in busy shopping weeks. Treat that as a pattern to check in your own data, not a rule.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"CTR is click-through rate. Google defines it simply: clicks divided by impressions. Five clicks from a hundred impressions is a five percent CTR.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"p","text":"CTR tells you whether the ad earns the click. Google also says CTR feeds your keyword's expected CTR, which is part of how Google judges your ads. On Meta, a low click rate from the right audience usually means the hook or the offer isn't landing.","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Read it","text":"CPM went up, CTR stayed flat. The ad didn't get worse; attention got more expensive. That's the market, not the creative.","k":"L2.4"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/meta-help-metric-cpm.png","cap":"Screenshot: Meta Business Help Center metric definition, \"CPM (cost per 1,000 impressions)\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L2.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"On Meta, check that you're reading link click-through rate, the clicks that go to your destination, rather than a broader click count. Know exactly which click column you're looking at before you compare ads.","k":"L2.6"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"CPC and conversion rate: the click and the landing.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"CPC is cost per click: spend divided by clicks. Three thousand kronor for a hundred and fifty clicks is a CPC of twenty kronor.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"There's a neat link: CPC equals CPM divided by ten times CTR in percent. So a cheap CPC can come from cheap attention or from a great click rate. Either way, the chain tells you which.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Conversion rate is conversions divided by clicks, or as Google puts it, conversions divided by ad interactions that can be tracked to a conversion. Fifty conversions from a thousand clicks is five percent.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Conversion rate mostly belongs to the landing page and the offer. If people click and don't buy, the ad promised something the page didn't deliver, or the page is slow or confusing.","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"warn","text":"Google notes conversion rate can go above one hundred percent if you count every conversion, or track several actions. Know what you're counting before you compare.","k":"L3.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"If CTR is healthy but conversion rate dropped, don't touch the ads first. Open the landing page on your phone, check the price, the stock and the form.","k":"L3.5"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"CPA and ROAS: the numbers the client cares about.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"CPA is cost per acquisition, sometimes called cost per result or cost per conversion: spend divided by conversions. Three thousand kronor for six booked consultations is a CPA of five hundred kronor.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"ROAS is return on ad spend: revenue from ads divided by ad spend. Google's own example: five dollars in sales for one dollar in spend is a five hundred percent ROAS. People also say \"ROAS five\", or \"five x\". Same thing.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Lead businesses, like clinics, usually live on CPA. Shops with a purchase value usually live on ROAS.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"Same chain, two lenses","text":"A shop spends 10,000 kronor and the purchases tracked to ads are worth 32,000 kronor. ROAS is 3.2. If those were 80 orders, the CPA is 125 kronor.","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Always ask the client what a customer is worth to them. A five-hundred-kronor CPA is a disaster for a fifty-kronor product and a bargain for a twenty-thousand-kronor implant.","k":"L4.4"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"Break-even ROAS: is it actually profitable?","items":[{"t":"p","text":"A ROAS of two sounds good. But if the product has a forty percent margin, it's a loss.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Here's the logic. Out of every krona of revenue, only the margin is left to pay for the ads. So to break even, ad spend must be no more than the margin on the revenue it brings. That gives you break-even ROAS: one divided by the margin.","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Forty percent margin: one divided by zero point four is two point five. Below 2.5, the ads lose money on the first order. Twenty-five percent margin: break-even ROAS is four.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"p","text":"This is your own calculation, not a platform metric, and it ignores repeat purchases. A business with loyal returning customers may accept a lower ROAS on the first order on purpose.","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Put break-even ROAS at the top of every report for a shop. Then \"ROAS 3\" means something: above or below the line.","k":"L5.4"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"Frequency, hook rate and hold rate.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Frequency, in Meta's words, is the average number of times each person saw your ad. It's impressions divided by reach. Rising frequency with falling CTR often means the same people are seeing the same ad too often.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"For video, two more numbers matter. They're industry conventions, not official platform names, so define them when you use them.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Hook rate: three-second video plays divided by impressions. It answers: did the first three seconds stop the scroll? Meta has a matching metric in its help centre, \"3-second video plays rate per impressions\": the percentage of impressions that turned into three-second video plays.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Hold rate: ThruPlays divided by three-second video plays. ThruPlay is a Meta video metric, and also a performance goal you can choose for video views. Hold rate answers: once you had them, did the body keep them?","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Diagnose with two numbers","text":"Hook rate 35 percent, hold rate 6 percent: the opening works, the middle loses people. Keep the first three seconds, rewrite the body.","k":"L6.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"For every video ad, write hook rate and hold rate next to CTR. Weak hook: change the first frame and first line. Weak hold: change the body.","k":"L6.5"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["CPM = spend ÷ impressions × 1,000 (cost of attention)","CTR = clicks ÷ impressions (does the ad earn the click?)","CPC = spend ÷ clicks = CPM ÷ (10 × CTR%)","Conversion rate = conversions ÷ clicks (page + offer)","CPA = spend ÷ conversions. ROAS = ad revenue ÷ ad spend.","Break-even ROAS = 1 ÷ margin (40% margin → 2.5). Own calculation, first order only.","Frequency = impressions ÷ reach (Meta).","Hook rate = 3-second plays ÷ impressions (Meta: \"3-second video plays rate per impressions\"). Hold rate = ThruPlays ÷ 3-second plays (industry convention).","Read in chain order. Find the broken link before you change anything."],"src":[{"label":"Google Ads Help: Clickthrough rate (CTR): Definition","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/2615875"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: Conversion rate: Definition","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/2684489"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: Cost-per-thousand impressions (CPM): Definition","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/6310"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About Target ROAS bidding","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/6268637"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: Glossary of Reservation Terms (frequency)","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/230299314945919"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: CPM (cost per 1,000 impressions)","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/753932008002620"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: Impressions","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/675615482516035"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: 3-second video plays rate per impressions","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/1252260652457829"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: Choose an objective (ThruPlay)","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/1438417719786914"}],"quiz":[{"q":"You spend 2,000 kr and get 80,000 impressions. What is the CPM?","o":["2.5 kr","25 kr","40 kr","250 kr"],"a":1,"e":"2,000 ÷ 80,000 × 1,000 = 25 kr."},{"q":"An ad got 150 clicks from 6,000 impressions. What is the CTR?","o":["0.4%","2.5%","4%","25%"],"a":1,"e":"150 ÷ 6,000 = 0.025 = 2.5%."},{"q":"Spend 3,000 kr, 6 booked consultations. CPA?","o":["500 kr","180 kr","18,000 kr","50 kr"],"a":0,"e":"3,000 ÷ 6 = 500 kr."},{"q":"A shop has a 25% margin. What is its break-even ROAS?","o":["1.25","2.5","4","25"],"a":2,"e":"1 ÷ 0.25 = 4. Below ROAS 4 the first order loses money."},{"q":"CTR is steady, but conversion rate halved this week. Where do you look first?","o":["The ad's headline","The landing page, price, stock and form","The CPM","The audience age range"],"a":1,"e":"People still click, so the ad works. The break is after the click."},{"q":"Frequency is 6 and CTR keeps falling. What's the most likely story?","o":["The pixel is broken","The same people have seen the same ad many times; it's worn out","The CPM is too low","The ad is too new"],"a":1,"e":"Frequency = average times each person saw the ad. High and rising plus falling CTR suggests fatigue."},{"q":"A video has a 32% hook rate and a 5% hold rate. What do you change?","o":["The first three seconds","The body of the video, after the opening","The budget","Nothing, it's perfect"],"a":1,"e":"Strong hook, weak hold: people stop, then leave. Fix the middle."}],"drills":[{"type":"calc","title":"Do the maths","prompt":["Calculate each metric. Round as shown."],"checks":[],"model":["Every metric is one division. Say the formula out loud, then put the numbers in."],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[{"q":"Spend 4,500 kr, 150,000 impressions. CPM in kr?","a":30.0,"tol":0.5,"e":"4,500 ÷ 150,000 × 1,000 = 30"},{"q":"240 clicks from 12,000 impressions. CTR in %?","a":2.0,"tol":0.05,"e":"240 ÷ 12,000 = 0.02 = 2%"},{"q":"Spend 3,600 kr, 240 clicks. CPC in kr?","a":15.0,"tol":0.1,"e":"3,600 ÷ 240 = 15"},{"q":"18 purchases from 600 clicks. Conversion rate in %?","a":3.0,"tol":0.05,"e":"18 ÷ 600 = 0.03 = 3%"},{"q":"Spend 9,000 kr, 30 leads. CPA in kr?","a":300.0,"tol":1.0,"e":"9,000 ÷ 30 = 300"},{"q":"Spend 10,000 kr, ad revenue 32,000 kr. ROAS (as x)?","a":3.2,"tol":0.05,"e":"32,000 ÷ 10,000 = 3.2"},{"q":"Margin 40%. Break-even ROAS?","a":2.5,"tol":0.05,"e":"1 ÷ 0.40 = 2.5"},{"q":"50,000 impressions, 12,500 three-second plays. Hook rate in %?","a":25.0,"tol":0.1,"e":"12,500 ÷ 50,000 = 25%"},{"q":"90,000 impressions, 30,000 reach. Frequency?","a":3.0,"tol":0.05,"e":"90,000 ÷ 30,000 = 3"}]},{"type":"order","title":"Read the report in chain order","prompt":["Put the metrics in the order you read a report: from first contact to profit."],"checks":[],"model":["Left to right is the customer's journey. A problem shows up at the first link that broke."],"items":["CPM (cost of attention)","CTR (did they click?)","CPC (cost per click)","Conversion rate (did the page convert?)","CPA (cost per result)","ROAS vs break-even ROAS (is it profitable?)"],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"sort","title":"Which link broke?","prompt":["Each line is a change since last week. Which part of the chain is the problem?"],"checks":[],"model":["Find the first link that moved. That's where you work."],"items":["CPM up 40%, CTR and conversion rate unchanged => Market / auction","CTR halved, same audience, CPM unchanged => Ad (creative)","CTR unchanged, conversion rate dropped from 3% to 1% => Landing page / offer","Hook rate fell from 30% to 12% => Ad (creative)","Checkout page loads in 9 seconds on mobile => Landing page / offer","Black Friday week, every metric the same except CPM up => Market / auction"],"cats":["Market / auction","Ad (creative)","Landing page / offer"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t11-1-01","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-01","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-01","title":"The ad metrics you must read fluently","kind":"Mini-lesson","module":"11.1"},{"ref":"taste:t11-1-02","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-02","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-02","title":"Example numbers: spend 3,000 kr, 150 clicks, 6 booked consultati…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.1"},{"ref":"taste:t11-1-03","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-03","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-03","title":"Example numbers: spend 2,000 kr for 80,000 impressions. CPM?","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.1"},{"ref":"taste:t11-1-04","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-04","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-04","title":"Example numbers (DTC store): ad spend 10,000 kr, revenue 32,000 …","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.1"},{"ref":"taste:t11-1-05","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-05","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-05","title":"Example numbers: a Reel got 50,000 impressions and 12,500 three-…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.1"},{"ref":"taste:t11-1-06","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-06","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-06","title":"Ad A: CTR 2.5%, CPA 900 kr. Ad B: CTR 0.9%, CPA 350 kr. Goal: bo…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.1"},{"ref":"taste:t11-1-07","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-07","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-07","title":"Example: CPM rose from 60 kr to 95 kr this week while CTR and co…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.1"},{"ref":"taste:t11-1-09","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-09","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-09","title":"Example: a DTC store has a 40% gross margin. What's the break-ev…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.1"}],"words":1152,"audio":{"duration":500.308,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. The chain: from impression to profit.","s":29.17},{"t":"2. CPM and CTR: getting seen and getting clicked.","s":80.17},{"t":"3. CPC and conversion rate: the click and the landing.","s":179.88},{"t":"4. CPA and ROAS: the numbers the client cares about.","s":257.64},{"t":"5. Break-even ROAS: is it actually profitable?","s":334.96},{"t":"6. Frequency, hook rate and hold rate.","s":401.94},{"t":"End","s":492.71}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":9.888},{"k":"lead.0","s":10.788,"d":9.912},{"k":"lead.1","s":21.3,"d":7.272},{"k":"L1.h","s":29.172,"d":3.408},{"k":"L1.0","s":33.08,"d":11.328},{"k":"L1.1","s":45.008,"d":11.04},{"k":"L1.2","s":56.648,"d":8.616},{"k":"L1.4","s":65.864,"d":13.704},{"k":"L2.h","s":80.168,"d":5.424},{"k":"L2.0","s":86.092,"d":22.032},{"k":"L2.1","s":108.724,"d":17.28},{"k":"L2.2","s":126.604,"d":10.152},{"k":"L2.3","s":137.356,"d":16.896},{"k":"L2.4","s":154.852,"d":11.664},{"k":"L2.6","s":167.116,"d":12.168},{"k":"L3.h","s":179.884,"d":5.064},{"k":"L3.0","s":185.448,"d":9.408},{"k":"L3.1","s":195.456,"d":13.2},{"k":"L3.2","s":209.256,"d":12.84},{"k":"L3.3","s":222.696,"d":11.808},{"k":"L3.4","s":235.104,"d":10.728},{"k":"L3.5","s":246.432,"d":10.608},{"k":"L4.h","s":257.64,"d":5.568},{"k":"L4.0","s":263.708,"d":14.04},{"k":"L4.1","s":278.348,"d":16.92},{"k":"L4.2","s":295.868,"d":7.56},{"k":"L4.3","s":304.028,"d":17.976},{"k":"L4.4","s":322.604,"d":11.76},{"k":"L5.h","s":334.964,"d":4.68},{"k":"L5.0","s":340.144,"d":5.784},{"k":"L5.1","s":346.528,"d":16.272},{"k":"L5.2","s":363.4,"d":13.968},{"k":"L5.3","s":377.968,"d":12.12},{"k":"L5.4","s":390.688,"d":10.656},{"k":"L6.h","s":401.944,"d":4.104},{"k":"L6.0","s":406.548,"d":14.928},{"k":"L6.1","s":422.076,"d":8.616},{"k":"L6.2","s":431.292,"d":18.528},{"k":"L6.3","s":450.42,"d":14.712},{"k":"L6.4","s":465.732,"d":13.848},{"k":"L6.5","s":480.18,"d":11.928},{"k":"end","s":492.708,"d":6.0}],"file":"aa-t06-metrics-101-cpm-ctr-cpc-cvr-cpa-and-roas.mp3"}},{"n":7,"level":"Intermediate","platform":"Meta","kicker":"Intermediate · Meta · Targeting and automation","title":"Meta targeting today: Advantage+, broad audiences and creative as targeting","sub":"Why the modern Meta buyer spends less time picking interests and more time briefing creative","goal":["Explain Advantage+ audience, which controls are hard limits and which are only suggestions.","Choose between custom audiences, lookalikes, detailed targeting and broad for a given brief.","Explain creative diversification and why Meta calls creative the lever for finding audiences."],"lead":["Ten years ago a Meta buyer's skill was finding clever interests. Today Meta's own guidance says the focus has shifted from niche targeting to creative diversification.","This unit shows you what changed, which controls you still own, and how to set up a Meta account the way Meta's systems are built to work."],"lessons":[{"title":"The targeting options you still have.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Meta's help centre lists the audience choices at the ad set level. You can let Meta automate targeting with Advantage+ audience, or define the audience yourself.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Defining it yourself means three tools. Basic demographics: location, age, gender. Detailed targeting: demographics, interests and behaviours. And custom audiences, built from your own data.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta lists several kinds of custom audience: website visitors tracked by the Pixel, people who interacted with your catalog, customer lists of emails or phone numbers, offline customers such as in-store buyers, and people who engaged with your videos, lead forms or profiles on Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp and Threads. You can also make an exclusion-only list, used just to keep people out.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Lookalike audiences start from a source audience, like your customers, and find new people who share similar characteristics. Meta notes that a smaller percentage matches the source more closely, and that a source of your best customers may beat a list of all customers.","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"warn","text":"Meta also notes that lookalikes are limited or unavailable for some ads about financial products, employment or housing, and that targeting options are limited when the audience includes people under 18.","k":"L1.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"For every new client, ask for three lists on day one: all customers, best customers by value, and people who must be excluded, like current subscribers.","k":"L1.5"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"Advantage+ audience: suggestions versus hard limits.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"With Advantage+ audience, Meta's ad technology finds the audience and adjusts over time. The important detail is which settings it will respect.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"According to Meta's developer documentation, some settings remain hard controls: location, minimum age, language, and excluded custom audiences. Others, such as age range, gender, detailed targeting and custom audiences, become suggestions. The system starts there, but can go beyond them if it finds better results.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"So if a clinic must only reach people in Stockholm who are over eighteen, set those as hard controls. If you think women aged 30 to 45 are your core buyers, that's a suggestion, and Meta may prove you wrong.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d07-advantage-audience.svg","cap":"Diagram: Advantage+ audience. Hard controls (location, minimum age, language, exclusions) form the fence; suggestions are a starting point inside it (Meta for Developers).","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/meta-help-advantage-audience.png","cap":"Screenshot: Meta Business Help Center, \"Audience targeting options for ads\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L2.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"When a client insists on a narrow audience, explain the difference in one line: \"The fence is fixed, the suggestion is a starting point. We'll see who actually buys.\"","k":"L2.5"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"Advantage+ sales campaigns.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"For the Sales objective, Meta describes Advantage+ sales campaigns, formerly Advantage+ shopping campaigns, as its most efficient way to run sales. They use the most advanced AI optimisations when Advantage+ audience, placements and budget are all on.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta's own figure: on average, a nine percent improvement in cost per conversion. Treat any platform number as the platform's claim, measured by the platform.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta's best practices for these campaigns include giving the system first-party data, using diverse creative, using value tools, and defining audience segments, such as existing customers, so you can report on new versus returning buyers.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"Simple structure","text":"A shoe brand runs one Advantage+ sales campaign with one campaign budget, thirty varied ads, and existing customers defined as a segment. Reporting splits new and returning customers. That's the whole structure.","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"In an interview, describe the trend in one sentence: fewer campaigns, broader audiences, more creative, and clean conversion data.","k":"L3.4"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"Andromeda and creative diversification.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Meta Andromeda is what Meta calls a next-generation AI model in its ads system. Meta connects it directly to creative diversification: more volume and variety of creative means more eligible ads to personalise for each person.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta defines creative diversification as creating a wide range of ads with different themes, messages and visuals for different audience segments. Its argument: with AI tools, the focus has shifted from niche targeting to creative diversification as the best lever to find the most relevant audiences.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"In plain words, your creative is now your targeting. A video about \"back pain after long drives\" finds drivers. A video about \"running again after an injury\" finds runners. Same clinic, same broad audience, different people.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta's practical guidance: launch new ads regularly, with leaders launching weekly; keep at least twenty diversified ads in an Advantage+ sales campaign; and spend twenty to thirty percent of budget on testing new creative.","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"warn","text":"Diversified means genuinely different: different angle, person, format, message. Ten versions of one image with a new background colour are not diversification.","k":"L4.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"For each new brief, list at least three personas with different needs or blockers, and write one ad angle for each. Meta itself suggests building personas this way.","k":"L4.5"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"Placements and Advantage+ creative.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Placements are where ads show: feeds, Stories, Reels and more. Meta reports that in its experiments, Advantage+ placements gave an eleven point seven percent lower cost per action on average. For the Leads, App promotion and Sales objectives, Meta has made Advantage+ placements the default.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Advantage+ creative is different: it lets Meta adjust your images, videos and text, for example adjusting brightness and contrast, or text improvements, to fit each person and placement. Meta labels which enhancements use generative AI.","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta's help page notes that some new creative features may be unavailable or tested differently for certain industries, including pharma and health.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"warn","text":"For clinics and other regulated clients, review every enhancement before launch. An automatically rewritten line can break a health policy you were careful to follow.","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Supply assets for every shape: 9:16 for Stories and Reels, 4:5 or 1:1 for feeds. Then placements don't have to crop your message away.","k":"L5.4"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"Putting it together: a modern Meta setup.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Here's a sensible starting structure for most small and medium accounts, built from Meta's guidance.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"One prospecting campaign, sales or leads objective, with Advantage+ audience. Hard controls only where the business truly needs them: country or city, minimum age, language, existing-customer exclusions if you want new buyers only.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Inside it, many genuinely different ads, refreshed regularly. A clean conversion signal from the Pixel and the Conversions API. And a budget big enough to exit learning, which you'll cover in Unit 9.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"p","text":"What you should not do: split one small budget across ten interest ad sets. Each one gets too few conversions to learn, and they compete for the same people.","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Before and after","text":"Before: eight ad sets, each with 100 kronor a day and its own interest, all stuck in learning. After: one ad set, broad, with the same 800 kronor and twelve diverse ads. More results per ad set, so the system can learn.","k":"L6.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Look at the last account you saw, or the example in the drill, and count: how many ad sets, and how many conversions each got last week? Fewer, fatter ad sets is usually the first fix.","k":"L6.5"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["Audience tools: demographics, detailed targeting, custom audiences (Pixel, lists, engagement, offline), lookalikes, Advantage+ audience.","Advantage+ audience hard controls: location, minimum age, language, excluded custom audiences. Everything else is a suggestion.","Advantage+ sales campaigns: Meta's most efficient way to run Sales; Meta claims 9% better cost per conversion on average.","Andromeda + diversification: creative is the lever for finding audiences. Launch new ads regularly, ≥20 diversified ads, 20–30% of budget to testing (Meta guidance).","Advantage+ placements default for Leads, App promotion, Sales; Meta claims 11.7% lower CPA.","Review Advantage+ creative changes for regulated clients.","Fewer, fatter ad sets. Many different ads."],"src":[{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: Audience targeting options","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/1277541120855335"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About custom audiences","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/744354708981227"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About lookalike audiences","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/164749007013531"},{"label":"Meta for Developers: Advantage+ audience","url":"https://developers.facebook.com/docs/marketing-api/audiences/reference/targeting-expansion/advantage-audience/"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About Advantage+ sales campaigns","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/1362234537597370"},{"label":"Meta for Business: Unlocking the power of diversification with Meta Andromeda","url":"https://en-gb.facebook.com/business/news/the-creative-advantage-unlocking-the-power-of-diversification-with-meta-andromeda"},{"label":"Meta for Business: Three steps to optimize your performance with creative diversification","url":"https://en-gb.facebook.com/business/news/three-steps-to-optimize-your-performance-with-creative-diversification"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About Advantage+ placements","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/196554084569964"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About Advantage+ creative","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/297506218282224"}],"quiz":[{"q":"In Advantage+ audience, which of these is a hard control the system will not go beyond?","o":["Detailed interests","Gender","Location","Your suggested age range"],"a":2,"e":"Hard controls: location, minimum age, language, excluded custom audiences."},{"q":"What does Meta say is now the best lever for finding the most relevant audiences?","o":["Niche interest targeting","Creative diversification","Manual placements","Lower bids"],"a":1,"e":"Meta: the focus shifted from niche targeting to creative diversification."},{"q":"Which is real creative diversification?","o":["The same image in five background colours","Three ads with different angles: price, a customer story and a demo","One ad copied into five ad sets","One video with five different CTA buttons only"],"a":1,"e":"Different themes, messages and visuals, not cosmetic tweaks."},{"q":"A lookalike from which source audience may give better results, according to Meta?","o":["All website visitors ever","Your best customers","People who liked one post","A purchased email list"],"a":1,"e":"Meta notes quality matters: best customers may beat all customers."},{"q":"A client wants only new customers. What do you set?","o":["Exclude a customer-list custom audience of existing customers","Pick interests that existing customers don't have","Lower the budget","Turn off Advantage+ placements"],"a":0,"e":"Excluded custom audiences are a hard control."},{"q":"A small account has 8 interest ad sets at 100 kr a day each, all stuck in learning. Best first move?","o":["Add more interests","Consolidate into fewer, broader ad sets with more diverse ads","Lower each budget","Pause the Pixel"],"a":1,"e":"Too many small ad sets split conversions. Consolidate so each can learn."},{"q":"Why review Advantage+ creative enhancements for a clinic?","o":["They always reduce CTR","Automatic edits could break a health policy you followed carefully","They are only for Stories","They cost extra"],"a":1,"e":"Regulated industries need a human check of every automatic change."}],"drills":[{"type":"sort","title":"Hard control or suggestion?","prompt":["With Advantage+ audience switched on, is each setting a hard limit or a suggestion?"],"checks":[],"model":["The fence is location, minimum age, language and exclusions. Everything else is a starting hint."],"items":["Country: Sweden => Hard control","Minimum age 18 => Hard control","Interest: running => Suggestion","Language: Swedish => Hard control","Women only => Suggestion","Exclude: existing customers list => Hard control","Age 30–45 => Suggestion"],"cats":["Hard control","Suggestion"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"pick","title":"Which setup fits Meta's current guidance?","prompt":["Pick the setup that fits how Meta's systems are built today."],"checks":[],"model":[],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[{"a":"1 Advantage+ sales campaign, broad, 25 varied ads, customers as a segment.","b":"9 campaigns, one per interest, 2 ads each.","ok":"A","e":"Consolidation plus diversified creative gives the system data and options."},{"a":"3 personas, 3 angles, new ads every week.","b":"1 \"perfect\" ad running until it dies.","ok":"A","e":"Meta recommends regular launches and diverse ads, not a single perfect ad."},{"a":"Exclude existing customers when the goal is new buyers.","b":"Target an interest you hope new buyers have.","ok":"A","e":"Exclusions are hard controls; interests are only suggestions."}],"calc":[]},{"type":"write","title":"Three personas, three angles","prompt":["A physio clinic in Malmö wants more first visits. Write three ad angles, one line each, for three different personas. Avoid implying anything about the reader's health (Unit 11 explains why)."],"checks":[{"kind":"include","arg":"runn,office,desk,golf,parent,drive,lift,sport,senior,garden","label":"Names a specific persona or situation"},{"kind":"minwords","arg":"15","label":"At least 15 words in total"},{"kind":"avoid","arg":"your pain,your injury,do you suffer,are you suffering","label":"Doesn't assert the reader's health condition"}],"model":["\"Runners: get a return-to-running plan from a physio who runs.\" / \"Desk workers: a 30-minute posture check before the stiffness sets in.\" / \"Golfers: a swing-friendly mobility session, booked online.\""],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t6-1-06","app":"taste","id":"t6-1-06","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t6-1-06","title":"Mixed: which ad set structure fits Meta's Andromeda guidance?","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"6.1"},{"ref":"taste:t7-1-11","app":"taste","id":"t7-1-11","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t7-1-11","title":"Physio clinic in Malmö: which audience setup fits Meta's current…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"7.1"},{"ref":"taste:t6-1-05","app":"taste","id":"t6-1-05","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t6-1-05","title":"Mixed: an aesthetic clinic wants more consultations. Which ad co…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"6.1"}],"words":1187,"audio":{"duration":550.18,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. The targeting options you still have.","s":28.72},{"t":"2. Advantage+ audience: suggestions versus hard limits.","s":127.59},{"t":"3. Advantage+ sales campaigns.","s":193.68},{"t":"4. Andromeda and creative diversification.","s":272.4},{"t":"5. Placements and Advantage+ creative.","s":373.41},{"t":"6. Putting it together: a modern Meta setup.","s":452.54},{"t":"End","s":542.53}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":7.272},{"k":"lead.0","s":8.172,"d":10.896},{"k":"lead.1","s":19.668,"d":8.448},{"k":"L1.h","s":28.716,"d":3.24},{"k":"L1.0","s":32.456,"d":10.584},{"k":"L1.1","s":43.64,"d":14.76},{"k":"L1.2","s":59.0,"d":24.624},{"k":"L1.3","s":84.224,"d":17.04},{"k":"L1.4","s":101.864,"d":12.768},{"k":"L1.5","s":115.232,"d":11.76},{"k":"L2.h","s":127.592,"d":5.544},{"k":"L2.0","s":133.636,"d":9.912},{"k":"L2.1","s":144.148,"d":21.96},{"k":"L2.2","s":166.708,"d":13.776},{"k":"L2.5","s":181.084,"d":12.0},{"k":"L3.h","s":193.684,"d":3.6},{"k":"L3.0","s":197.784,"d":18.312},{"k":"L3.1","s":216.696,"d":10.896},{"k":"L3.2","s":228.192,"d":15.72},{"k":"L3.3","s":244.512,"d":17.184},{"k":"L3.4","s":262.296,"d":9.504},{"k":"L4.h","s":272.4,"d":4.176},{"k":"L4.0","s":277.076,"d":16.392},{"k":"L4.1","s":294.068,"d":20.4},{"k":"L4.2","s":315.068,"d":16.776},{"k":"L4.3","s":332.444,"d":14.64},{"k":"L4.4","s":347.684,"d":11.976},{"k":"L4.5","s":360.26,"d":12.552},{"k":"L5.h","s":373.412,"d":3.864},{"k":"L5.0","s":377.776,"d":20.592},{"k":"L5.1","s":398.968,"d":16.944},{"k":"L5.2","s":416.512,"d":9.456},{"k":"L5.3","s":426.568,"d":12.696},{"k":"L5.4","s":439.864,"d":12.072},{"k":"L6.h","s":452.536,"d":4.056},{"k":"L6.0","s":457.092,"d":6.408},{"k":"L6.1","s":464.1,"d":16.992},{"k":"L6.2","s":481.692,"d":13.128},{"k":"L6.3","s":495.42,"d":10.8},{"k":"L6.4","s":506.82,"d":20.328},{"k":"L6.5","s":527.748,"d":14.184},{"k":"end","s":542.532,"d":6.048}],"file":"aa-t07-meta-targeting-today-advantage-broad-aud.mp3"}},{"n":8,"level":"Intermediate","platform":"Google","kicker":"Intermediate · Google · Search ads","title":"Responsive search ads, Ad Strength, Quality Score and Ad Rank","sub":"How Google builds your ad from parts, and how it decides whether you show and where","goal":["Write a responsive search ad that follows Google's limits and best practices.","Explain the difference between Ad Strength, Quality Score and Ad Rank, and which one affects the auction.","Diagnose a low Quality Score from its three components and fix the right thing."],"lead":["On Google Search you don't write one ad. You write a box of parts, and Google assembles them for every search.","Three scores then judge your work. Two of them are only diagnostics. One decides whether you show at all. Juniors mix them up; you won't."],"lessons":[{"title":"How a responsive search ad works.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"A responsive search ad, or RSA, is an ad you give many pieces. Google's limits: at least three and up to fifteen headlines, at least two and up to four descriptions. Headlines can be up to thirty characters, descriptions up to ninety, and the two display path fields up to fifteen each.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Google then tests combinations and learns which perform best. Up to three headlines can show in the headline position, and parts of your text may appear in bold when they match the search.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Two consequences matter. First, any headline may appear next to any other, in any order, so each must make sense on its own. Second, if something must always show, such as a legal line, you pin it to headline position one, two, or description position one.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d08-rsa-anatomy.svg","cap":"Diagram: an RSA as a box of parts. Up to 15 headlines and 4 descriptions are combined into the ad a searcher sees (Google Ads Help).","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"warn","text":"Pinning reduces the combinations Google can test. Pin only what truly must appear, like a required disclaimer.","k":"L1.4"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/google-help-rsa.png","cap":"Screenshot: Google Ads Help, \"About responsive search ads\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L1.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write your first three headlines as if they will appear together, as Google suggests. Then write the rest as alternatives that each cover a different angle.","k":"L1.6"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"Writing headlines that cover different angles.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Google's advice is to include at least one of your keywords in your headlines, and to make headlines relevant to the keywords you target. A keyword must sit entirely inside one headline to count; it can't be split across two. If a keyword is longer than thirty characters, put it in a description instead.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Beyond keywords, think in angles, so Google has real choices. Use a keyword headline, a benefit, a proof point, an offer, a call to action, and a reason to choose you over competitors.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"ex","head":"Bike repair shop, ad group \"bike service stockholm\"","text":"Headlines: \"Bike Service in Stockholm\" (keyword). \"Ready Same Day if In by 10\" (benefit). \"4.8 Stars from 900 Reviews\" (proof, only if true). \"Fixed Price Service: 695 kr\" (offer, only if true). \"Book Online in 2 Minutes\" (action). Each makes sense alone.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"Count your characters. Thirty is short. \"Bike Service in Stockholm\" is twenty-five, so it fits; \"Professional Bicycle Servicing in Stockholm\" doesn't.","k":"L2.3"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"Ad Strength: feedback while you write.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Ad Strength rates an RSA from Incomplete through Poor, Average, Good and Excellent, with action items to improve it.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Google is explicit that Ad Strength doesn't decide whether your ad can serve, and calls it a common misconception that it's used for Ad Rank, Quality Score or auction wins. It's a feedback tool for asset variety and relevance to your keywords.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Google lists what it looks at: whether your headlines and descriptions use your most relevant keywords, how many combinations you provide, and sitelinks. Google says having six or more sitelinks at account, campaign or ad group level improves the score.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Google's recommendations: at least two RSAs with Good or Excellent Ad Strength per ad group, each with a unique final URL. And Google's own figure: advertisers who improve Ad Strength from Poor to Excellent see fifteen percent more conversions on average.","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Use Ad Strength as a checklist while writing, not as a goal. A Good-rated ad with a strong offer beats an Excellent-rated ad that says nothing.","k":"L3.4"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"Quality Score: a diagnostic, not a target.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Quality Score is a number from one to ten at the keyword level. Google calls it a diagnostic tool that shows how your ad quality compares to other advertisers.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"It has three components, each rated Above average, Average or Below average, compared with other advertisers who showed for the exact same search over the last ninety days. Expected click-through rate: how likely your ad is to be clicked. Ad relevance: how closely your ad matches the intent behind the search. Landing page experience: how relevant and useful your page is.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Google stresses two things. Quality Score is not an input in the ad auction. And it's not a key performance indicator, so don't optimise it or average it across your account.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"Read the components","text":"Keyword \"emergency plumber\": expected CTR Average, ad relevance Below average, landing page Below average. The ad doesn't mention emergencies and sends people to the home page. Fix: a headline with \"Emergency Plumber\" and a landing page about emergency call-outs.","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/google-help-quality-score.png","cap":"Screenshot: Google Ads Help, \"About Quality Score for Search campaigns\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L4.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Fix the weakest component, not the score. Ad relevance: tighten the ad group's theme and match headlines to the keywords. Landing page: send the click to the most specific page. Expected CTR: a sharper offer and call to action.","k":"L4.5"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"Ad Rank: the score that runs the auction.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Ad Rank is what actually decides whether your ad is eligible to show and where it appears. Google lists its factors: your bid, the quality of your ads and landing page, Ad Rank thresholds, the context of the search, such as location, device and time, and the expected impact of assets and other ad formats.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"It's calculated twice in each auction: first to decide whether your ad is eligible, then to rank it against other eligible ads. And Google says that even if competitors bid more, you can still win a higher position at a lower price with highly relevant ads and assets.","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"So Quality Score is like a school report on the components. Ad Rank is the actual race.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d01-adrank.svg","cap":"Diagram: Ad Rank inputs. Bid, ad and landing page quality, thresholds, search context and asset impact (Google Ads Help).","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"When a client asks \"Why are we paying more than competitors?\", look at the quality side first. Better relevance can lower cost without raising bids.","k":"L5.4"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"Assets: more space, more reasons to click.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Assets add extra information to your ad: links to specific pages, called sitelinks, short callouts, phone numbers, locations and more. Google counts the expected impact of assets as part of Ad Rank, and counts sitelinks towards Ad Strength.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Google's RSA documentation also notes that unused headlines can sometimes be shown as link-based assets, pointing to the same domain as your ad.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"ex","head":"Sitelinks for the bike shop","text":"\"Prices\", \"Book a Service\", \"E-bike Repairs\", \"Opening Hours\", \"Winter Storage\", \"Reviews\". Six useful links, each to a real page.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"For every Search campaign, add at least six sitelinks to real, relevant pages. It's quick, it helps Ad Strength, and it gives searchers more ways in.","k":"L6.3"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["RSA: 3–15 headlines (≤30 chars), 2–4 descriptions (≤90), 2 paths (≤15). Any order: each headline must stand alone.","Pin only what must always show (positions H1, H2, D1). Pinning limits testing.","Keyword inside one headline; long keywords go in descriptions.","Ad Strength: Incomplete → Excellent; feedback only; not used for Ad Rank or Quality Score. ≥2 Good/Excellent RSAs per ad group; ≥6 sitelinks help.","Quality Score 1–10: expected CTR, ad relevance, landing page. Diagnostic, not an auction input, not a KPI.","Ad Rank: bid + ad/landing page quality + thresholds + context + asset impact. Calculated twice per auction.","Fix the weakest component, not the number."],"src":[{"label":"Google Ads Help: About responsive search ads","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/7684791"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About Ad Strength for responsive search ads","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/9921843"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About Quality Score for Search campaigns","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/6167118"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: Ad Rank: Definition","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/1752122"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What are the minimum and maximum headlines in a responsive search ad?","o":["1 to 5","3 to 15","5 to 30","2 to 4"],"a":1,"e":"Minimum 3, up to 15. Descriptions: 2 to 4."},{"q":"Which of these is used in the ad auction?","o":["Quality Score","Ad Strength","Ad Rank","Optimisation score"],"a":2,"e":"Quality Score and Ad Strength are diagnostics. Ad Rank decides eligibility and position."},{"q":"Which headline fits Google's 30-character limit?","o":["\"Bike Service in Stockholm\"","\"Professional Bicycle Servicing in Stockholm\"","\"Stockholm's Most Trusted Bicycle Repair Workshop\"","\"Get Your Bike Fixed Today by Expert Mechanics\""],"a":0,"e":"25 characters. The others are over 30."},{"q":"Quality Score shows landing page experience \"Below average\". What's the best fix?","o":["Raise the bid","Add more headlines","Send the click to a more relevant, useful page for that keyword","Change the match type"],"a":2,"e":"Fix the weak component. Google: changing match type doesn't change Quality Score."},{"q":"When should you pin a headline?","o":["Always, to control the ad","Only when that text must always appear, such as a required disclaimer","Never, it's not allowed","When Ad Strength is Excellent"],"a":1,"e":"Pinning reduces combinations Google can test."},{"q":"How many sitelinks does Google say improve Ad Strength?","o":["1","3","6 or more","15"],"a":2,"e":"Google: 6 or more sitelinks at account, campaign or ad group level."},{"q":"A competitor bids more but you rank higher. How is that possible?","o":["Google rotates randomly","Ad Rank also counts ad and landing page quality, context and assets","Your Quality Score is a direct auction input","Your Ad Strength is Excellent"],"a":1,"e":"Google: relevant ads and assets can win a higher position at a lower price."}],"drills":[{"type":"write","title":"Five headlines, five angles","prompt":["Ad group: \"teeth whitening stockholm\". Write five RSA headlines, one per line: keyword, benefit, proof, offer and action. Max 30 characters each. Only use facts you would check with the client."],"checks":[{"kind":"lines","arg":"5","label":"Five headlines, one per line"},{"kind":"include","arg":"whiten","label":"At least one headline contains the keyword"},{"kind":"include","arg":"book,call,get,start,try","label":"Has an action headline"},{"kind":"avoid","arg":"","label":"No vague hype words"}],"model":["Teeth Whitening Stockholm / Whitening in One Visit / Rated 4.9 by 500 Patients / Free Shade Check First / Book Online in 2 Minutes"],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"sort","title":"Which score is this?","prompt":["Match each statement to the score it describes."],"checks":[],"model":["Ad Strength and Quality Score are report cards. Ad Rank is the race."],"items":["Rated Poor to Excellent while you write the ad => Ad Strength","1–10 at the keyword level => Quality Score","Decides whether your ad is eligible and where it shows => Ad Rank","Counts sitelinks and keyword use in headlines => Ad Strength","Expected CTR, ad relevance, landing page experience => Quality Score","Calculated twice in every auction => Ad Rank"],"cats":["Ad Strength","Quality Score","Ad Rank"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"pick","title":"Which fix fits the diagnosis?","prompt":["Pick the right fix for each Quality Score diagnosis."],"checks":[],"model":[],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[{"a":"Ad relevance: Below average. Fix: split the ad group so headlines match a tighter keyword theme.","b":"Ad relevance: Below average. Fix: raise the bid by 30%.","ok":"A","e":"Relevance is about the match between keyword and ad. Bids don't fix relevance."},{"a":"Expected CTR: Below average. Fix: sharpen the offer and call to action in headlines.","b":"Expected CTR: Below average. Fix: change broad match to exact.","ok":"A","e":"Google: changing match type doesn't change Quality Score. A better reason to click does."}],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t10-1-01","app":"taste","id":"t10-1-01","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t10-1-01","title":"Search ads answer a question the person just typed","kind":"Mini-lesson","module":"10.1"},{"ref":"taste:t10-1-04","app":"taste","id":"t10-1-04","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t10-1-04","title":"Two RSAs in an ad group. Which set of headlines gives Google mor…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"10.1"},{"ref":"taste:t10-1-05","app":"taste","id":"t10-1-05","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t10-1-05","title":"Good or bad? Rate it, then reveal the breakdown.","kind":"Good or bad?","module":"10.1"},{"ref":"taste:t10-1-07","app":"taste","id":"t10-1-07","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t10-1-07","title":"What's missing or wrong? Tick everything that applies, then reve…","kind":"What's missing?","module":"10.1"},{"ref":"taste:t10-1-03","app":"taste","id":"t10-1-03","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t10-1-03","title":"Search: 'fysioterapeut nära mig'. Which description is stronger?","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"10.1"}],"words":1101,"audio":{"duration":476.788,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. How a responsive search ad works.","s":23.87},{"t":"2. Writing headlines that cover different angles.","s":101.1},{"t":"3. Ad Strength: feedback while you write.","s":176.93},{"t":"4. Quality Score: a diagnostic, not a target.","s":252.5},{"t":"5. Ad Rank: the score that runs the auction.","s":347.25},{"t":"6. Assets: more space, more reasons to click.","s":409.67},{"t":"End","s":468.83}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":5.928},{"k":"lead.0","s":6.828,"d":6.984},{"k":"lead.1","s":14.412,"d":8.856},{"k":"L1.h","s":23.868,"d":3.168},{"k":"L1.0","s":27.536,"d":21.072},{"k":"L1.1","s":49.208,"d":11.472},{"k":"L1.2","s":61.28,"d":18.456},{"k":"L1.4","s":80.336,"d":8.544},{"k":"L1.6","s":89.48,"d":11.016},{"k":"L2.h","s":101.096,"d":3.912},{"k":"L2.0","s":105.508,"d":19.392},{"k":"L2.1","s":125.5,"d":13.296},{"k":"L2.2","s":139.396,"d":25.2},{"k":"L2.3","s":165.196,"d":11.136},{"k":"L3.h","s":176.932,"d":3.624},{"k":"L3.0","s":181.056,"d":8.808},{"k":"L3.1","s":190.464,"d":14.88},{"k":"L3.2","s":205.944,"d":16.44},{"k":"L3.3","s":222.984,"d":17.952},{"k":"L3.4","s":241.536,"d":10.368},{"k":"L4.h","s":252.504,"d":4.608},{"k":"L4.0","s":257.612,"d":10.488},{"k":"L4.1","s":268.7,"d":25.296},{"k":"L4.2","s":294.596,"d":11.448},{"k":"L4.3","s":306.644,"d":22.032},{"k":"L4.5","s":329.276,"d":17.376},{"k":"L5.h","s":347.252,"d":3.984},{"k":"L5.0","s":351.736,"d":20.952},{"k":"L5.1","s":373.288,"d":17.28},{"k":"L5.2","s":391.168,"d":6.744},{"k":"L5.4","s":398.512,"d":10.56},{"k":"L6.h","s":409.672,"d":5.04},{"k":"L6.0","s":415.212,"d":17.352},{"k":"L6.1","s":433.164,"d":9.768},{"k":"L6.2","s":443.532,"d":13.08},{"k":"L6.3","s":457.212,"d":11.016},{"k":"end","s":468.828,"d":6.36}],"file":"aa-t08-responsive-search-ads-ad-strength-qualit.mp3"}},{"n":9,"level":"Intermediate","platform":"Both","kicker":"Intermediate · Meta + Google · Bidding and budgets","title":"Bidding, budgets and the learning phase on both platforms","sub":"How to tell the machine what you want, how much to spend, and when to keep your hands off","goal":["Pick the right bid strategy on Meta and Google for a given business goal.","Explain how daily budgets can flex on each platform, so you can calm a worried client.","Explain the learning phase and avoid the edits that reset it."],"lead":["Bidding is where you tell the machine what success looks like. Budget is how much fuel it gets. Learning is the warm-up you must not interrupt.","Get these three right and most accounts run calmly. Get them wrong and you'll spend your days chasing noise."],"lessons":[{"title":"Meta bid strategies: spend, goal or cap.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Meta groups its bid strategies into three families.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Spend-based: Highest volume gets the most results from your budget; Highest value focuses the budget on the most valuable purchases. Both spend the full budget and don't control cost per result.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Goal-based: Cost per result goal keeps the average cost near a target you set. ROAS goal keeps return on ad spend near a target. Meta's example: if you want one hundred dollars to produce about a hundred and ten in purchases, set the ROAS control at 1.100. For these, Meta recommends fifty to a hundred or more weekly conversions for best performance, and warns results may not exactly match the goal.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Manual: Bid cap sets the maximum bid in every auction. Meta says it's meant for advertisers with a strong understanding of predicted conversion rates.","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Choosing","text":"A new shop with 15 sales a week: Highest volume. A mature shop with 300 sales a week and a clear profit target: try a cost per result goal or ROAS goal. Bid cap: leave it until you truly know your numbers.","k":"L1.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Start most new campaigns on Highest volume or Highest value. Add a goal only when you have the weekly volume Meta recommends and a target based on real margins.","k":"L1.5"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"Google bid strategies: Smart Bidding and the rest.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Google's Smart Bidding uses AI to set bids for conversions or conversion value in every single auction, which Google calls auction-time bidding. It uses signals like device, location and time of day.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"The four Smart Bidding strategies pair up neatly. Maximize conversions spends the budget to get as many conversions as possible; Target CPA aims for conversions at a target cost. Maximize conversion value spends the budget for the most value; Target ROAS aims for value at a target return.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Google notes that from June 2026 it's renaming two of them: \"Maximize conversions with a Target CPA\" becomes \"Target CPA\", and \"Maximize conversion value with a Target ROAS\" becomes \"Target ROAS\".","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Other goals use other bidding: clicks for traffic, viewable impressions for awareness, and views for video.","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"warn","text":"Google's own note: before conversion-focused bidding, make sure conversion tracking is right and the actions you want are set as Primary. Smart Bidding is only as good as Unit 5's tracking.","k":"L2.4"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/google-help-smart-bidding.png","cap":"Screenshot: Google Ads Help, \"About Smart Bidding\" (note the June 2026 naming change at the top). Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L2.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"For Target ROAS on Search, Google lists at least fifteen conversions in the past thirty days. Below that, start with Maximize conversions or Maximize conversion value.","k":"L2.6"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"Translating targets: from margin to bid goal.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Targets must come from the business, not from a guess.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"For a CPA target, ask what a customer is worth after costs. If a clinic earns three thousand kronor profit per new patient and wants to spend at most a third of it, the target CPA is a thousand kronor.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"For a ROAS target, start from break-even ROAS, one divided by margin, from Unit 6. Forty percent margin means break-even ROAS 2.5. The target must sit above that if the first order must be profitable.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Google adds a practical tip for Target ROAS: lowering the target lets the strategy enter more auctions for more volume; raising it focuses on value and efficiency. Same logic on Meta.","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Two dials","text":"Target ROAS 400 percent gives 40 sales a week. The client wants growth. Lower to 350 percent, accept slightly lower efficiency, and watch total profit, not ROAS alone.","k":"L3.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write the target and the reasoning in the account notes: \"Target CPA 1,000 kr = one-third of 3,000 kr profit per patient.\" Then nobody changes it on a whim.","k":"L3.5"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"How budgets actually spend.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Clients panic when a 500-kronor daily budget spends 800 on Tuesday. Both platforms allow this, within limits.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"On Meta, with a daily budget, Meta's help centre says you may spend up to seventy-five percent more than your daily budget on a given day, but never more than one hundred and seventy-five percent of it in one day. Over a calendar week, ending Saturday at midnight, you won't spend more than seven times the daily budget. With ad set budget sharing, an ad set can get up to twenty percent extra.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"On Google, a campaign might spend up to twice its average daily budget on a given day, and by the end of the month no more than thirty point four times the daily budget.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta also offers Advantage+ campaign budget: one budget at campaign level, spread in real time to the ad sets with the best opportunities. Meta says to judge it at campaign level.","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d09-budget-flex.svg","cap":"Diagram: daily budget flex. Meta up to 175% in a day, 7× per week; Google up to 2× in a day, 30.4× per month (Meta Business Help Center; Google Ads Help).","k":"L4.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Send this one-liner before a client asks: \"Daily budgets are averages. Some days spend more, some less, but the week (Meta) or month (Google) stays within the cap.\"","k":"L4.5"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"The learning phase: hands off.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Every new campaign has a warm-up while the system learns who converts.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"On Meta, an ad set usually exits learning after about fifty results in the week after its last significant edit. During learning, performance is less stable and cost per result is usually higher. If it can't get enough results, it shows \"Learning limited\".","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta's advice: don't edit during learning, avoid unnecessary edits that restart it, avoid running too many ads and ad sets, and use realistic budgets without frequent changes. Meta also says you shouldn't try to avoid learning completely: testing new things is how accounts improve.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"p","text":"On Google, major changes, like a new campaign or bid strategy, can show a \"Learning\" status for up to seven days. After big changes to conversion tracking, Google suggests giving the system one to two conversion cycles to adapt.","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"The fifty test","text":"An ad set has a 200 kr cost per purchase and 300 kr a day budget. That's about ten purchases a week. It will struggle to reach fifty. Options: merge ad sets, raise budget, or optimise for an earlier event with more volume.","k":"L5.4"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/meta-help-learning-phase.png","cap":"Screenshot: Meta Business Help Center, \"About the learning phase\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L5.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"Before launch, do the maths: weekly budget divided by expected cost per result. If it's far below fifty on Meta, change the plan before you launch, not after.","k":"L5.6"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"Making changes without breaking things.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"You will need to change things. Do it deliberately.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Batch changes: one planned edit beats five small ones over five days. Change budgets in steps rather than wild swings, and give the system time to settle after each one. Meta warns that frequent budget changes can affect learning.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"And keep a change log: date, what changed, why. When performance moves next week, you'll know whether it was you or the market.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"warn","text":"The worst habit in paid media: checking every hour and reacting. Daily numbers are noisy. Judge on a full week, or longer for low volume.","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Start a simple change log for every account today: date, change, reason, what you expect to happen.","k":"L6.4"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["Meta: spend-based (Highest volume / Highest value), goal-based (Cost per result / ROAS goal; 50–100+ weekly conversions recommended), manual (Bid cap).","Google Smart Bidding: Max conversions / Target CPA; Max conversion value / Target ROAS. Tracking + Primary actions first. Search Target ROAS: ≥15 conversions in 30 days.","Targets come from margins: CPA from profit per customer; ROAS above break-even (1 ÷ margin).","Meta daily budget: up to +75% a day (max 175%), ≤7× per week; budget sharing up to 20%.","Google: up to 2× daily on a day, ≤30.4× per month.","Meta learning: ~50 results in the week after the last significant edit. Google: \"Learning\" up to 7 days; 1–2 conversion cycles after conversion changes.","Batch changes, keep a change log, judge on weeks not hours."],"src":[{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About bid strategies","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/1619591734742116"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About the learning phase","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/112167992830700"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About daily budgets","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/190490051321426"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About budgets (Advantage+ campaign budget)","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/458847204894307"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About Smart Bidding","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/7065882"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: Determine a bid strategy based on your goals","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/2472725"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About Target ROAS bidding","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/6268637"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About primary and secondary conversion actions","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/11461796"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About overdelivery and your average daily budget","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/2375423"}],"quiz":[{"q":"A new shop gets about 15 sales a week. Which Meta bid strategy is a sensible start?","o":["Highest volume","Bid cap","ROAS goal at 5.0","Cost per result goal at half the current cost"],"a":0,"e":"Meta recommends 50–100+ weekly conversions for goal-based strategies."},{"q":"On Meta, roughly how many results does an ad set need to exit learning?","o":["5 in a day","About 50 in the week after the last significant edit","500 impressions","1,000 clicks"],"a":1,"e":"Meta: about 50 results in the week after the last significant edit."},{"q":"A Meta ad set has a 500 kr daily budget. What's the most it can spend in one day?","o":["500 kr","625 kr","875 kr","1,000 kr"],"a":2,"e":"Never more than 175% of the daily budget in one day: 875 kr."},{"q":"A Google campaign has a 300 kr average daily budget. What's the most it can spend on one day?","o":["300 kr","450 kr","600 kr","900 kr"],"a":2,"e":"Google: up to twice the average daily budget on a given day."},{"q":"What does Google say to check before using conversion-focused Smart Bidding?","o":["That your ads have emojis","That conversion tracking works and the right actions are Primary","That you use broad match only","That your budget is above 1,000 kr"],"a":1,"e":"Smart Bidding optimises for Primary conversions. Bad tracking, bad bidding."},{"q":"A Target ROAS campaign is profitable but the client wants more volume. What's the usual lever?","o":["Raise the target ROAS","Lower the target ROAS a little, so it enters more auctions","Pause the campaign for a week","Switch to manual CPC immediately"],"a":1,"e":"Google: lowering target ROAS can increase volume; raising it focuses on value."},{"q":"Margin 40%. Which ROAS target is too low if the first order must be profitable?","o":["2.0","2.8","3.0","3.5"],"a":0,"e":"Break-even ROAS = 1 ÷ 0.4 = 2.5. A 2.0 target loses money on the first order."}],"drills":[{"type":"calc","title":"Budget and learning maths","prompt":["Work out each number."],"checks":[],"model":["Budgets are averages with caps. Learning needs volume: budget ÷ cost per result tells you if you'll get there."],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[{"q":"Meta daily budget 400 kr. Maximum spend in one day?","a":700.0,"tol":1.0,"e":"400 × 1.75 = 700"},{"q":"Meta daily budget 400 kr. Maximum spend in a calendar week?","a":2800.0,"tol":1.0,"e":"400 × 7 = 2,800"},{"q":"Google average daily budget 250 kr. Maximum spend in one day?","a":500.0,"tol":1.0,"e":"250 × 2 = 500"},{"q":"Google average daily budget 250 kr. Maximum monthly spend?","a":7600.0,"tol":1.0,"e":"250 × 30.4 = 7,600"},{"q":"Weekly budget 7,000 kr, expected cost per purchase 350 kr. Purchases per week?","a":20.0,"tol":0.5,"e":"7,000 ÷ 350 = 20 (well below Meta's ~50)"},{"q":"Profit per new patient 3,000 kr; spend at most a third. Target CPA in kr?","a":1000.0,"tol":1.0,"e":"3,000 ÷ 3 = 1,000"}]},{"type":"sort","title":"Which bid strategy fits?","prompt":["Match each situation to the best-fitting strategy."],"checks":[],"model":["No data yet: let it spend and learn. Enough volume plus a real target: add the goal. Values vary: bid on value."],"items":["New lead-gen account, little data yet => Maximize conversions / Highest volume","Clinic with steady volume and a firm 1,000 kr max per lead => Target CPA / Cost per result goal","Shop with varied basket sizes and a profit-based ROAS target => Target ROAS / ROAS goal","Event: fill the venue, cost per attendee doesn't matter => Maximize conversions / Highest volume","Mature account, 120 leads a week, needs cost control => Target CPA / Cost per result goal"],"cats":["Maximize conversions / Highest volume","Target CPA / Cost per result goal","Target ROAS / ROAS goal"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"order","title":"A calm change routine","prompt":["Put the steps of a safe change in order."],"checks":[],"model":["Plan, log, change once, wait, compare. That's how professionals avoid chasing noise."],"items":["Check a full week of data, not today","Decide one change and why","Write it in the change log with what you expect","Make the change in one batch","Leave it alone through learning","Compare the result with what you expected"],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t11-1-06","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-06","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-06","title":"Ad A: CTR 2.5%, CPA 900 kr. Ad B: CTR 0.9%, CPA 350 kr. Goal: bo…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.1"},{"ref":"taste:t11-1-07","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-07","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-07","title":"Example: CPM rose from 60 kr to 95 kr this week while CTR and co…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.1"},{"ref":"taste:t6-1-06","app":"taste","id":"t6-1-06","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t6-1-06","title":"Mixed: which ad set structure fits Meta's Andromeda guidance?","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"6.1"}],"words":1212,"audio":{"duration":516.508,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. Meta bid strategies: spend, goal or cap.","s":22.98},{"t":"2. Google bid strategies: Smart Bidding and the rest.","s":114.37},{"t":"3. Translating targets: from margin to bid goal.","s":202.54},{"t":"4. How budgets actually spend.","s":285.07},{"t":"5. The learning phase: hands off.","s":359.61},{"t":"6. Making changes without breaking things.","s":455.82},{"t":"End","s":508.88}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":5.136},{"k":"lead.0","s":6.036,"d":8.328},{"k":"lead.1","s":14.964,"d":7.416},{"k":"L1.h","s":22.98,"d":4.872},{"k":"L1.0","s":28.352,"d":4.2},{"k":"L1.1","s":33.152,"d":12.168},{"k":"L1.2","s":45.92,"d":28.032},{"k":"L1.3","s":74.552,"d":10.176},{"k":"L1.4","s":85.328,"d":16.68},{"k":"L1.5","s":102.608,"d":11.16},{"k":"L2.h","s":114.368,"d":4.704},{"k":"L2.0","s":119.572,"d":13.632},{"k":"L2.1","s":133.804,"d":19.104},{"k":"L2.2","s":153.508,"d":14.16},{"k":"L2.3","s":168.268,"d":7.008},{"k":"L2.4","s":175.876,"d":13.104},{"k":"L2.6","s":189.58,"d":12.36},{"k":"L3.h","s":202.54,"d":4.416},{"k":"L3.0","s":207.456,"d":3.432},{"k":"L3.1","s":211.488,"d":13.392},{"k":"L3.2","s":225.48,"d":15.96},{"k":"L3.3","s":242.04,"d":12.96},{"k":"L3.4","s":255.6,"d":16.008},{"k":"L3.5","s":272.208,"d":12.264},{"k":"L4.h","s":285.072,"d":2.88},{"k":"L4.0","s":288.452,"d":8.76},{"k":"L4.1","s":297.812,"d":24.216},{"k":"L4.2","s":322.628,"d":9.888},{"k":"L4.3","s":333.116,"d":12.216},{"k":"L4.5","s":345.932,"d":13.08},{"k":"L5.h","s":359.612,"d":3.504},{"k":"L5.0","s":363.616,"d":4.224},{"k":"L5.1","s":368.44,"d":17.376},{"k":"L5.2","s":386.416,"d":19.8},{"k":"L5.3","s":406.816,"d":15.816},{"k":"L5.4","s":423.232,"d":19.512},{"k":"L5.6","s":443.344,"d":11.88},{"k":"L6.h","s":455.824,"d":3.96},{"k":"L6.0","s":460.284,"d":3.192},{"k":"L6.1","s":464.076,"d":15.6},{"k":"L6.2","s":480.276,"d":8.208},{"k":"L6.3","s":489.084,"d":10.584},{"k":"L6.4","s":500.268,"d":8.016},{"k":"end","s":508.884,"d":6.024}],"file":"aa-t09-bidding-budgets-and-the-learning-phase-o.mp3"}},{"n":10,"level":"Intermediate","platform":"Both","kicker":"Intermediate · Meta + Google · Creative","title":"Creative strategy and briefs: hook, body, call to action","sub":"How to plan a stream of ads that find new people, and brief them so a designer can build them","goal":["Structure a video or image ad as hook, body and call to action, built for a phone with the sound off.","Apply Meta's mobile-first guidance and Google's ABCDs to a script.","Write a one-page creative brief with an insight, an audience and a direction."],"lead":["On both platforms the machine now decides who sees your ad. What it can't do is make the ad. That's your job, and it's where most of the money is won or lost.","This unit turns \"make some ads\" into a repeatable system: angles, scripts, and a brief you can hand to anyone."],"lessons":[{"title":"The three parts of every ad.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Every effective ad, image or video, does three jobs in order.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"The hook stops the scroll. It's the first frame, the first line, the first second.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"The body makes the case: the problem, the product solving it, and proof that it's true.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"p","text":"The call to action tells people exactly what to do next and why now.","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"p","text":"You've written hooks, offers and calls to action in Unit 4 and in the Copy Workshop drills. This unit puts them together into ads you can produce every week.","k":"L1.4"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d10-hook-body-cta.svg","cap":"Diagram: hook (stop) → body (problem, demo, proof) → call to action (one clear next step) (own diagram).","k":"L1.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"For any ad you see in the feed this week, name the three parts out loud. If you can't find one, that's the ad's weakness.","k":"L1.6"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"Built for a phone, with the sound off.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Meta's mobile-first guidance is the best short rulebook for social video.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta says feed viewing is frequent, fast and often sound-free. So: get your main message across in the first three seconds. Make videos fifteen seconds or less, with one clear, single-minded message. Don't rely on sound to tell the story, but reward people who turn it on. Start bold, with motion and speed.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Use the right shapes. Meta's guidance mentions 4:5 for feed and 9:16 for Stories. Meta's ads guide for a feed image lists 4:5 at 1440 by 1800 pixels, primary text of 50 to 150 characters, and a 27-character headline.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"Sound-off test","text":"A physio clinic video starts with a logo for four seconds. Fail. Version two opens on a runner stopping mid-stride, with the text \"Knee stops you at 5 km?\" Wait, that line implies the reader has a condition; Unit 11 explains why that breaks Meta's rules. Version three: \"Running again after a break? Start here.\" Hook in one second, no assumption about the viewer.","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Watch your ad on mute on a phone. If the first three seconds don't make sense without sound, re-cut it.","k":"L2.4"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"Google's ABCDs for video.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"For YouTube, Google publishes the ABCDs of effective video ads: Attention, Branding, Connection, Direction.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Attention: jump into the story fast, with tight framing, supporting audio and text, bright high-contrast visuals.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Branding: show your brand or product early and throughout, and mention it in the audio.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Connection: feature people, keep the message focused and simple, and use emotion like humour, surprise or intrigue.","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"p","text":"Direction: include a clear call to action, and reinforce it with voice-over.","k":"L3.4"},{"t":"p","text":"Google cites Google and Kantar research saying applying ABCDs can deliver up to a thirty percent lift in short-term sales likelihood. That's Google's study, so treat it as Google's claim.","k":"L3.5"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/google-help-abcd.png","cap":"Screenshot: Google Ads Help, \"About the ABCDs of effective video ads\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L3.6"},{"t":"apply","text":"Score your next script A, B, C, D, one point each. Anything under four, fix before shooting.","k":"L3.7"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"Angles: one product, many reasons to buy.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Unit 7 showed why variety matters: Meta says creative diversification is how its system finds different audiences. Variety starts with angles, not with colours.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"An angle is one reason a specific person would buy. Meta suggests building personas around different customer needs and blockers to purchase, then matching value propositions to each.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Five angle types work for almost any product. The problem: the pain the product removes. The outcome: life after. Proof: a review, a number, a demonstration. The offer: price, guarantee, bonus. And the objection: answer the reason people hesitate.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Then vary the format for each angle: a talking person, a product demo, a static image with a strong line, a customer review on screen.","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Coffee subscription, five angles","text":"Problem: \"Supermarket beans taste flat?\" Outcome: \"Café coffee at your kitchen table.\" Proof: \"Roasted Monday, at your door Wednesday.\" Offer: \"First bag half price, skip any month.\" Objection: \"Cancel in two clicks. No phone calls.\"","k":"L4.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Make an angle grid for one product: five angles across, three formats down. That's fifteen genuinely different ads from one brief.","k":"L4.5"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"The creative brief.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"A brief is one page that lets a designer, a creator or an AI tool make the right ad without a meeting.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta recommends reviewing creative weekly with buyers and creative teams, and says a good brief includes three things: an insight into what's currently working, a new opportunity or audience to reach, and a creative direction that could resonate.","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Add the practical parts: the objective, the offer, mandatory lines such as legal text, formats and sizes, and the deadline.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"The one-page template","text":"Insight: what's working now, with the number. Audience or opportunity: who we want next, and their main blocker. Direction: angle, hook ideas, format, tone. Offer and call to action. Must-haves: brand, legal lines, policy notes. Specs: 9:16 and 4:5, length, captions on. Deadline and number of variations.","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Use this template for every brief, even a quick one in a chat message. It forces you to say why the ad should work.","k":"L5.4"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"The weekly creative loop.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Put it together into a loop you run every week.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Monday: read the numbers by ad. Which hooks have the best hook rate? Which bodies hold? Which angles convert? Write two or three insights.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Tuesday: brief new ads from those insights. Meta suggests twenty to thirty percent of budget for testing new creative.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Later in the week: launch them, and keep the winners running. Retire ads only when the numbers say they're tired, like rising frequency with falling click-through rate.","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"warn","text":"Don't judge an ad on one day or a few hundred impressions. Give each new ad enough delivery to say something before you decide.","k":"L6.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Start a creative log: ad name, angle, format, hook, and its hook rate, CTR and CPA. In a month, it becomes your client's most valuable document.","k":"L6.5"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["Every ad: hook (stop) → body (problem, demo, proof) → call to action (one next step).","Meta mobile-first: main message in 3 s, ≤15 s, works sound-off, start bold; 4:5 feed, 9:16 Stories.","Meta feed image: 4:5, 1440×1800; primary text 50–150 chars; headline 27.","Google ABCDs: Attention, Branding, Connection, Direction.","Angles: problem, outcome, proof, offer, objection. × formats = real diversification.","Brief: insight, audience/opportunity, direction (+ offer, must-haves, specs, deadline).","Weekly loop: read by ad → insights → brief → launch → keep winners → retire on fatigue."],"src":[{"label":"Meta for Business: Adopt a mobile-first creative approach for your video ads","url":"https://en-gb.facebook.com/business/news/mobile-first-creative"},{"label":"Meta Ads Guide: Image ad specs on Facebook Feed","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/ads-guide/update/image/facebook-feed"},{"label":"Meta for Business: Three steps to optimize your performance with creative diversification","url":"https://en-gb.facebook.com/business/news/three-steps-to-optimize-your-performance-with-creative-diversification"},{"label":"Meta for Business: Unlocking the power of diversification with Meta Andromeda","url":"https://en-gb.facebook.com/business/news/the-creative-advantage-unlocking-the-power-of-diversification-with-meta-andromeda"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About the ABCDs of effective video ads","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/14783551"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Meta's mobile-first guidance say about the first three seconds?","o":["Show your logo","Get your main message across","Play music only","Show the price list"],"a":1,"e":"Meta: main message in the first 3 seconds; videos 15 seconds or less."},{"q":"What do Google's ABCDs stand for?","o":["Ads, Bids, Clicks, Data","Attention, Branding, Connection, Direction","Audience, Budget, Creative, Delivery","Awareness, Benefit, Call, Deal"],"a":1,"e":"Google's four principles for effective YouTube ads."},{"q":"Which is a different angle, not just a variation?","o":["Same image, new background colour","Switching from a price offer to a customer review about results","Same video, different font","Same ad, new campaign name"],"a":1,"e":"An angle is a different reason to buy."},{"q":"Meta says a good creative brief includes which three things?","o":["Logo, colour, font","An insight into what's working, a new opportunity or audience, and a creative direction","Budget, bid, schedule","Three headlines and a CTA button"],"a":1,"e":"Insight, opportunity or audience, direction."},{"q":"Why should social video work with the sound off?","o":["Sound is banned in feeds","Meta says feed viewing is often sound-free","Captions cost less","Music lowers CPM"],"a":1,"e":"Don't rely on sound, but reward those who turn it on."},{"q":"What size does Meta's ads guide list for a 4:5 feed image?","o":["1080 × 1080","1440 × 1800","1920 × 1080","600 × 600"],"a":1,"e":"Meta Ads Guide, Facebook Feed image: 4:5, 1440 × 1800 pixels."},{"q":"Your best ad's frequency is rising and CTR falling for two weeks. What do you do?","o":["Nothing, frequency is good","Brief new ads from its winning angle and give them budget","Delete the whole campaign","Double the budget on the tired ad"],"a":1,"e":"Signs of fatigue. Keep the insight, refresh the execution."}],"drills":[{"type":"order","title":"Build a 15-second script","prompt":["Put the parts of a 15-second sound-off video in order."],"checks":[],"model":["Hook first, message by second three, proof before the ask, and one clear action at the end."],"items":["0–1 s: Bold first frame with text that names the situation","1–3 s: The main message in one line","3–9 s: Demo of the product doing its job","9–12 s: Proof: a review or number on screen","12–15 s: Offer and one call to action, on screen and in voice-over"],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"sort","title":"Which angle is it?","prompt":["A coffee subscription. Label each line with its angle."],"checks":[],"model":["Five angles give five different ads that find five different buyers."],"items":["\"Supermarket beans taste flat?\" => Problem","\"Café coffee at your kitchen table.\" => Outcome","\"Roasted Monday, at your door Wednesday.\" => Proof","\"First bag half price.\" => Offer","\"Cancel in two clicks. No phone calls.\" => Objection"],"cats":["Problem","Outcome","Proof","Offer","Objection"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"write","title":"Write a mini brief","prompt":["Write a creative brief for a teeth whitening clinic in 5 lines: insight, audience, direction, offer, specs."],"checks":[{"kind":"lines","arg":"5","label":"Five lines"},{"kind":"number","arg":"","label":"Uses at least one number (insight or spec)"},{"kind":"include","arg":"9:16,4:5,second","label":"Includes a format or length spec"},{"kind":"include","arg":"insight,working,best","label":"States an insight about what's working"}],"model":["Insight: before/after-free \"process\" videos get 2× our hook rate. / Audience: brides-to-be booking 2–3 months ahead. / Direction: calm walkthrough of a 60-minute visit, hygienist on camera. / Offer: free shade check, book online. / Specs: 9:16 and 4:5, 15 seconds, captions on."],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"ws:w:w2-071","app":"ws","id":"w:w2-071","url":"workshop/index.html?embed=1#/w/w2-071","title":"Primary text: hook line — Bow Strength Studio","kind":"Copy rep"},{"ref":"taste:t3-3-10","app":"taste","id":"t3-3-10","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t3-3-10","title":"BRIEF: Write 3 different video hooks (first line, ≤12 words each…","kind":"Write it","module":"3.3"},{"ref":"taste:t3-3-11","app":"taste","id":"t3-3-11","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t3-3-11","title":"BRIEF: Write a 15-second Reels script (hook / body / CTA) for a …","kind":"Write it","module":"3.3"},{"ref":"taste:t9-1-03","app":"taste","id":"t9-1-03","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t9-1-03","title":"TikTok/Reels hook for a skincare brand (DTC). Which first line i…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"9.1"},{"ref":"taste:t9-2-02","app":"taste","id":"t9-2-02","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t9-2-02","title":"BRIEF: Write a 20-second UGC script (with timestamps) for a DTC …","kind":"Write it","module":"9.2"},{"ref":"ws:w:w4-107","app":"ws","id":"w:w4-107","url":"workshop/index.html?embed=1#/w/w4-107","title":"Ad offer — Östermalm Dental English","kind":"Copy rep"},{"ref":"ws:w:w5-124","app":"ws","id":"w:w5-124","url":"workshop/index.html?embed=1#/w/w5-124","title":"Ad call to action — Bow Strength Studio","kind":"Copy rep"}],"words":1021,"audio":{"duration":469.852,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. The three parts of every ad.","s":25.64},{"t":"2. Built for a phone, with the sound off.","s":72.97},{"t":"3. Google's ABCDs for video.","s":164.57},{"t":"4. Angles: one product, many reasons to buy.","s":231.94},{"t":"5. The creative brief.","s":325.77},{"t":"6. The weekly creative loop.","s":400.7},{"t":"End","s":462.13}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":5.952},{"k":"lead.0","s":6.852,"d":10.008},{"k":"lead.1","s":17.46,"d":7.584},{"k":"L1.h","s":25.644,"d":3.36},{"k":"L1.0","s":29.504,"d":4.968},{"k":"L1.1","s":35.072,"d":5.448},{"k":"L1.2","s":41.12,"d":6.312},{"k":"L1.3","s":48.032,"d":4.224},{"k":"L1.4","s":52.856,"d":10.392},{"k":"L1.6","s":63.848,"d":8.52},{"k":"L2.h","s":72.968,"d":3.432},{"k":"L2.0","s":76.9,"d":4.8},{"k":"L2.1","s":82.3,"d":22.104},{"k":"L2.2","s":105.004,"d":21.168},{"k":"L2.3","s":126.772,"d":29.424},{"k":"L2.4","s":156.796,"d":7.176},{"k":"L3.h","s":164.572,"d":3.816},{"k":"L3.0","s":168.888,"d":8.304},{"k":"L3.1","s":177.792,"d":8.592},{"k":"L3.2","s":186.984,"d":5.616},{"k":"L3.3","s":193.2,"d":8.856},{"k":"L3.4","s":202.656,"d":5.736},{"k":"L3.5","s":208.992,"d":12.816},{"k":"L3.7","s":222.408,"d":8.928},{"k":"L4.h","s":231.936,"d":4.536},{"k":"L4.0","s":236.972,"d":12.096},{"k":"L4.1","s":249.668,"d":11.568},{"k":"L4.2","s":261.836,"d":20.352},{"k":"L4.3","s":282.788,"d":9.408},{"k":"L4.4","s":292.796,"d":21.696},{"k":"L4.5","s":315.092,"d":10.08},{"k":"L5.h","s":325.772,"d":2.496},{"k":"L5.0","s":328.768,"d":7.704},{"k":"L5.1","s":337.072,"d":15.552},{"k":"L5.2","s":353.224,"d":8.736},{"k":"L5.3","s":362.56,"d":28.128},{"k":"L5.4","s":391.288,"d":8.808},{"k":"L6.h","s":400.696,"d":2.88},{"k":"L6.0","s":404.076,"d":3.552},{"k":"L6.1","s":408.228,"d":9.408},{"k":"L6.2","s":418.236,"d":8.52},{"k":"L6.3","s":427.356,"d":10.776},{"k":"L6.4","s":438.732,"d":9.072},{"k":"L6.5","s":448.404,"d":13.128},{"k":"end","s":462.132,"d":6.12}],"file":"aa-t10-creative-strategy-and-briefs-hook-body-c.mp3"}},{"n":11,"level":"Intermediate","platform":"Both","kicker":"Intermediate · Meta + Google · Policies","title":"Ad policies: health, clinics and personal attributes","sub":"The rules that get ads rejected and accounts restricted, and how to write ads that still sell","goal":["Spot copy that breaks Meta's personal attributes policy, and rewrite it.","Apply Meta's health and wellness rules: age 18+, no negative self-perception, no cure claims.","Explain Google's healthcare policy and its personalised advertising restrictions for health."],"lead":["Clinics, dentists, physios and skincare brands are some of the best clients you can have. They're also the easiest to get rejected.","The good news: the rules are learnable, and policy-safe ads are usually better ads. Specific, honest and about the service, not about the reader's body."],"lessons":[{"title":"Meta's personal attributes rule.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"This is the rule juniors break most. Meta's Transparency Center says ads must not assert or imply personal attributes. That includes health and medical information, financial status, age, religion and more.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"The reason Meta gives: ads that make assumptions about people can feel intrusive, unsettling or inaccurate.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"In practice, watch the word \"you\" when it's attached to a condition. Meta's own examples: \"Depression getting you down? Get help now.\" is not allowed. \"Ready to upgrade your skin to look younger?\" is not allowed. But \"Our new lotion and creams fight wrinkles like never before!\" is allowed, because it describes the product, not the reader.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta also allows \"you\" language when it doesn't reference a personal attribute.","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Rewrite it","text":"Not allowed: \"Suffering from back pain?\" Allowed: \"Back pain treatment with a licensed physio. Book online.\" The second describes the service. It doesn't claim to know anything about the reader.","k":"L1.4"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/meta-policy-personal-attributes.png","cap":"Screenshot: Meta Transparency Center, \"Privacy Violations and Personal Attributes\" policy. Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L1.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"Read every ad line and ask: does this tell the reader something about their body, health, money or identity? If yes, rewrite it to describe the product or service instead.","k":"L1.6"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"Meta's health and wellness rules.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Meta's health and wellness policy sets age rules. Ads for dietary, health, weight loss or weight gain products and services must target people at least eighteen. So must ads for cosmetic products, procedures or surgeries. Meta lists examples including dermal fillers, injectable treatments, chemical peels, micro-needling and laser treatments.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"The policy also bans several kinds of content. No statements of inferiority about physical appearance: negative terms or questions that attack someone's appearance, body parts or hygiene. No claims to cure, heal or eliminate the incurable diseases on Meta's list, such as diabetes, psoriasis or cancer. And no clickbait in a health context: sensational, exaggerated claims, or promises of specific outcomes in a set time without disclaimers or qualifiers.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Some things are outside the age rule, such as general wellbeing services like gyms, and non-permanent beauty products like creams and makeup.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"warn","text":"Combine this with Unit 5: the Pixel must not send health information to Meta. Clinic URLs like \"/book/eczema-treatment\" need care.","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"For every clinic or cosmetic campaign, set minimum age eighteen as a hard control, even with Advantage+ audience, and run the copy through the three bans: appearance, cure, clickbait.","k":"L2.4"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"Google's healthcare and medicines policy.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Google's healthcare and medicines policy covers many restricted areas: online pharmacies, prescription drug terms, pharmaceutical manufacturers, unapproved substances, speculative and experimental treatments, clinical trial recruitment and more.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Some of these need certification by Google, and some are only allowed in certain countries. Google says that when a campaign targets allowed locations and the domain is certified, the ad may run with the status \"Eligible (limited)\". And Google expects you to know the local law in every location you target.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"For a typical local clinic, the main risks are prescription drug names in ads, keywords or landing pages, and unapproved or overstated claims. Google, for example, doesn't allow non-approved products marketed as safe or effective for preventing, curing or treating a disease.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"Before launching for any clinic, list every treatment name in the ads, keywords and landing pages. Check each against Google's restricted drug terms for the target country.","k":"L3.3"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"Google: no remarketing for health.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Google's personalised advertising policy lists sensitive interest categories, and health is one of them. Advertisers promoting products or services in these categories can't use advertiser-curated audiences, such as Customer Match lists or your own data segments, the audiences formerly called remarketing.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Predefined Google audiences can still be used, because Google says the sensitive signals are automatically excluded from them. And people under eighteen aren't eligible for personalised ads at all.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"This changes your plan for a clinic: no \"we saw you looked at our implant page\" retargeting on Google. Build the campaign on search intent, keywords and good landing pages instead.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"Clinic Google plan","text":"Search campaign on \"dental implants stockholm\" and similar keywords. Strong landing page per treatment. No Customer Match or remarketing lists. Measurement through conversion tracking without sending health details.","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/google-policy-personalized.png","cap":"Screenshot: Google Advertising Policies Help, \"Restricted targeting in Personalized advertising\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L4.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write \"health: sensitive category\" at the top of every clinic account plan, so nobody adds a remarketing list later by mistake.","k":"L4.5"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"Writing policy-safe ads that still sell.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Policy-safe doesn't mean boring. The best clinic ads are specific about the service and the experience.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Describe the service: what happens, how long it takes, who does it. Use real proof: years in practice, number of reviews, certifications, if true. Make a low-risk offer: a free consultation, a clear price, online booking. And use a call to action that's easy: \"Book a 15-minute consultation online.\"","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"For before-and-after images, Meta's policy mentions cosmetic before-and-after in the eighteen-plus section. Check the current policy and local rules every time, and when in doubt, show the process, the clinic and the people, not body transformations.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"Same clinic, two ads","text":"Risky: \"Hate your wrinkles? Look ten years younger in one week!\" Safe and strong: \"Skin consultations with a licensed nurse in Östermalm. 4.9 from 600 reviews. Book a free 15-minute visit.\" The second is specific, believable and policy-safe.","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Keep a \"safe swaps\" list for each client: risky phrase on the left, approved alternative on the right. It speeds up every future brief.","k":"L5.4"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"When an ad gets rejected.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Rejections happen, even to careful buyers. Handle them calmly.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Read the stated reason first. Compare the ad with the exact policy text. Fix the cause, which might be the image, a line of text or the landing page, not just the word that looks guilty. If you truly believe the decision was wrong, both platforms let you request a review.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Keep a record of rejections and fixes. Patterns show you which phrases and images the systems flag for this client.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"warn","text":"Never try to trick review systems, for example by disguising words or swapping landing pages after approval. Repeated violations can restrict a whole ad account, which is a disaster for a client.","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"If an ad is rejected, write a one-line note: reason, fix, outcome. It becomes the client's policy playbook.","k":"L6.4"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["Meta personal attributes: no asserting or implying health, money, age, identity. Describe the product, not the reader.","Meta health & wellness: 18+ for weight, dietary, health and cosmetic ads; no appearance attacks; no cure claims for listed diseases; no clickbait timeframes.","Pixel: never send health information.","Google healthcare policy: restricted drug terms, certification, location limits, local law. \"Eligible (limited)\" is normal.","Google personalised ads: health is a sensitive category → no advertiser-curated audiences (Customer Match, your data segments). Under-18s get no personalised ads.","Safe and strong: specific service, real proof, low-risk offer, easy action.","Rejected? Read reason → compare with policy → fix cause → request review if wrong. Never trick review."],"src":[{"label":"Meta Transparency Center: Privacy violations and personal attributes","url":"https://transparency.meta.com/policies/ad-standards/objectionable-content/privacy-violations-personal-attributes/"},{"label":"Meta Transparency Center: Health and wellness","url":"https://transparency.meta.com/policies/ad-standards/restricted-goods-services/health-wellness"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About Meta Pixel","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/742478679120153"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About ad quality (review requests, repeated violations)","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/423781975167984"},{"label":"Google Advertising Policies Help: Healthcare and medicines","url":"https://support.google.com/adspolicy/answer/176031"},{"label":"Google Advertising Policies Help: Restricted targeting in personalized advertising","url":"https://support.google.com/adspolicy/answer/143465"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which line breaks Meta's personal attributes policy?","o":["\"Physio appointments available this week.\"","\"Struggling with knee pain? We can help.\"","\"Our licensed physios treat sports injuries.\"","\"Book a 30-minute assessment online.\""],"a":1,"e":"It implies knowledge of the reader's medical condition."},{"q":"Meta's minimum targeting age for cosmetic procedure ads is…","o":["13","16","18","21"],"a":2,"e":"Meta: cosmetic products, procedures or surgeries must target people at least 18."},{"q":"Which claim is banned by Meta's health and wellness policy?","o":["\"Licensed nurses since 2012\"","\"Our supplement cures diabetes\"","\"Free 15-minute consultation\"","\"Open Saturdays\""],"a":1,"e":"No claims to cure incurable diseases on Meta's list."},{"q":"Google lists health as a sensitive interest category. What can't a clinic use?","o":["Search keywords","Advertiser-curated audiences like Customer Match or your data segments","Conversion tracking","Responsive search ads"],"a":1,"e":"Sensitive categories can't use advertiser-curated audiences."},{"q":"Which rewrite is policy-safe AND persuasive?","o":["\"Hate your wrinkles? Look 10 years younger in a week!\"","\"Skin consultations with a licensed nurse. 4.9 from 600 reviews. Book a free visit.\"","\"Your skin is ageing. Fix it now.\"","\"Embarrassed by your skin? We can help.\""],"a":1,"e":"Describes the service with proof and an easy offer. No assumptions about the reader."},{"q":"An ad is rejected. What's the right first step?","o":["Change one word and resubmit ten times","Read the stated reason and compare the ad with the policy text","Swap the landing page after approval","Create a new ad account"],"a":1,"e":"Fix the actual cause. Never try to trick review."},{"q":"Why can a Meta clinic campaign go wrong even if the ad copy is fine?","o":["The CPM is too low","The Pixel or URLs might send health information to Meta","The headline is under 27 characters","The video is vertical"],"a":1,"e":"Meta lists health data as prohibited information for the Pixel."}],"drills":[{"type":"sort","title":"Allowed or not?","prompt":["Meta ad lines for health and beauty clients. Allowed or not?"],"checks":[],"model":["Describe the product or service. Don't tell the reader what's wrong with them, and don't promise fast miracles."],"items":["\"Our new cream fights wrinkles like never before.\" => Allowed","\"Ready to upgrade your skin to look younger?\" => Not allowed","\"Licensed physio, open Saturdays. Book online.\" => Allowed","\"Depression getting you down? Get help now.\" => Not allowed","\"Lose 10 kg in 2 weeks, guaranteed!\" => Not allowed","\"Free 15-minute consultation with a dentist.\" => Allowed","\"Embarrassed by your yellow teeth?\" => Not allowed"],"cats":["Allowed","Not allowed"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"write","title":"Rewrite to policy-safe","prompt":["Rewrite this ad so it follows Meta's rules and still sells: \"Tired of your acne scars? Get perfect skin in 7 days at our clinic!\""],"checks":[{"kind":"avoid","arg":"your scars,your acne,tired of,perfect,embarrass,hate","label":"No assumptions about the reader, no \"perfect\""},{"kind":"avoid","arg":"7 days,in a week,overnight","label":"No promised outcome in a set time"},{"kind":"include","arg":"book,consult,visit,call","label":"Has a clear, easy action"},{"kind":"maxwords","arg":"40","label":"40 words or fewer"}],"model":["\"Acne scar treatments with licensed skin nurses in Östermalm. Free 15-minute consultation to plan your treatment. Book online.\""],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"pick","title":"Which plan fits Google's rules for a clinic?","prompt":["Pick the plan that fits Google's personalised advertising policy."],"checks":[],"model":[],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[{"a":"Search campaign on treatment keywords, strong landing pages, no remarketing lists.","b":"Remarketing list of people who viewed the implant page, with a \"Still thinking about implants?\" ad.","ok":"A","e":"Health is a sensitive category: no advertiser-curated audiences, and the second ad also implies knowledge."},{"a":"Check treatment names against restricted drug terms before launch.","b":"Use prescription drug brand names in headlines for more clicks.","ok":"A","e":"Restricted drug terms depend on location and certification."}],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t7-1-01","app":"taste","id":"t7-1-01","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t7-1-01","title":"Clinic ads on Meta: the rules that decide what you can say","kind":"Mini-lesson","module":"7.1"},{"ref":"taste:t7-1-02","app":"taste","id":"t7-1-02","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t7-1-02","title":"Physio clinic. Which hook avoids Meta's personal-attributes prob…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"7.1"},{"ref":"taste:t7-1-03","app":"taste","id":"t7-1-03","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t7-1-03","title":"Dental clinic, cold audience. Which offer line is stronger and p…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"7.1"},{"ref":"taste:t7-1-05","app":"taste","id":"t7-1-05","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t7-1-05","title":"Good or bad? Rate it, then reveal the breakdown.","kind":"Good or bad?","module":"7.1"},{"ref":"taste:t7-1-08","app":"taste","id":"t7-1-08","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t7-1-08","title":"What's missing or wrong? Tick everything that applies, then reve…","kind":"What's missing?","module":"7.1"},{"ref":"taste:t2-4-03","app":"taste","id":"t2-4-03","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t2-4-03","title":"Physio clinic. Which version respects Meta's personal-attributes…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"2.4"},{"ref":"taste:t2-4-04","app":"taste","id":"t2-4-04","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t2-4-04","title":"Aesthetic clinic, skin treatments. Which is policy-safer AND mor…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"2.4"},{"ref":"taste:t7-4-01","app":"taste","id":"t7-4-01","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t7-4-01","title":"BRIEF: Write a policy-safe Meta ad (primary text ≤ 60 words + he…","kind":"Write it","module":"7.4"}],"words":1098,"audio":{"duration":514.9,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. Meta's personal attributes rule.","s":26.29},{"t":"2. Meta's health and wellness rules.","s":110.19},{"t":"3. Google's healthcare and medicines policy.","s":208.04},{"t":"4. Google: no remarketing for health.","s":281.42},{"t":"5. Writing policy-safe ads that still sell.","s":359.35},{"t":"6. When an ad gets rejected.","s":447.26},{"t":"End","s":507.11}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":5.544},{"k":"lead.0","s":6.444,"d":8.136},{"k":"lead.1","s":15.18,"d":10.512},{"k":"L1.h","s":26.292,"d":3.312},{"k":"L1.0","s":30.104,"d":14.64},{"k":"L1.1","s":45.344,"d":7.392},{"k":"L1.2","s":53.336,"d":22.248},{"k":"L1.3","s":76.184,"d":5.064},{"k":"L1.4","s":81.848,"d":14.784},{"k":"L1.6","s":97.232,"d":12.36},{"k":"L2.h","s":110.192,"d":2.832},{"k":"L2.0","s":113.524,"d":24.96},{"k":"L2.1","s":139.084,"d":32.808},{"k":"L2.2","s":172.492,"d":8.544},{"k":"L2.3","s":181.636,"d":11.184},{"k":"L2.4","s":193.42,"d":14.016},{"k":"L3.h","s":208.036,"d":3.864},{"k":"L3.0","s":212.4,"d":16.104},{"k":"L3.1","s":229.104,"d":19.584},{"k":"L3.2","s":249.288,"d":18.672},{"k":"L3.3","s":268.56,"d":12.264},{"k":"L4.h","s":281.424,"d":3.648},{"k":"L4.0","s":285.572,"d":19.536},{"k":"L4.1","s":305.708,"d":11.976},{"k":"L4.2","s":318.284,"d":12.096},{"k":"L4.3","s":330.98,"d":17.784},{"k":"L4.5","s":349.364,"d":9.384},{"k":"L5.h","s":359.348,"d":4.2},{"k":"L5.0","s":364.048,"d":6.72},{"k":"L5.1","s":371.368,"d":22.728},{"k":"L5.2","s":394.696,"d":17.544},{"k":"L5.3","s":412.84,"d":23.016},{"k":"L5.4","s":436.456,"d":10.2},{"k":"L6.h","s":447.256,"d":2.88},{"k":"L6.0","s":450.636,"d":4.392},{"k":"L6.1","s":455.628,"d":17.52},{"k":"L6.2","s":473.748,"d":8.448},{"k":"L6.3","s":482.796,"d":13.968},{"k":"L6.4","s":497.364,"d":9.144},{"k":"end","s":507.108,"d":6.192}],"file":"aa-t11-ad-policies-health-clinics-and-personal.mp3"}},{"n":12,"level":"Expert","platform":"Both","kicker":"Expert · Meta + Google · Diagnosis","title":"Diagnosing performance: the metric tree, relevance diagnostics and the breakdown effect","sub":"What a senior buyer does when the client writes \"results dropped, what happened?\"","goal":["Walk a performance drop down the metric tree to the link that broke.","Read Meta's ad relevance diagnostics and Google's search terms and Quality Score signals.","Avoid the classic misreads: the breakdown effect, hourly noise and judging by the wrong metric."],"lead":["Every account has bad weeks. The difference between a junior and a senior is what happens next: panic changes, or a calm diagnosis with a clear answer.","This unit gives you a routine you can run in fifteen minutes and explain to any client."],"lessons":[{"title":"The metric tree.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with the number the client cares about, usually CPA or ROAS, and split it into its parts.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"CPA equals CPM divided by a thousand, divided by click-through rate, divided by conversion rate. In plain words: cost of attention, times how many people you need to show the ad to for one click, times how many clicks you need for one sale.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"So if CPA rose by fifty percent, one or more of three things changed: attention got more expensive, fewer people clicked, or fewer clickers converted. ROAS adds a fourth: the average order value.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d12-metric-tree.svg","cap":"Diagram: the metric tree. CPA splits into CPM, CTR and conversion rate; ROAS adds order value (own diagram).","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Walk it","text":"Last week: CPM 60, CTR 1.5%, CVR 3%, CPA 133 kr. This week: CPM 62, CTR 1.5%, CVR 1.8%, CPA 230 kr. Only conversion rate moved. Look after the click: site, price, stock, checkout, tracking.","k":"L1.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"For every drop, write the four numbers for this period and the last one side by side. Circle the one that moved most. That's where you start.","k":"L1.5"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"First rule out tracking and the calendar.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Before you touch any ad, rule out the two most common false alarms.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Tracking: did conversions really fall, or did measurement break? Compare platform conversions with the client's own orders or leads. A sudden cliff to near zero is almost always tracking, not ads. Unit 5 covered how.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Calendar: compare like with like. A week with a public holiday, a payday, a sale or a price change isn't comparable with an ordinary week. Ask the client what changed on their side: prices, stock, the website, the offer, the team answering leads.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta's own guidance on fluctuations says to look at overall results, be careful with hourly breakdowns that average out over time, and to focus on the optimisation event you chose rather than secondary metrics.","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"warn","text":"Meta notes that when you optimise for conversions, CPM may not be a good indicator of performance, because the system may buy more expensive impressions if that's how it gets cheaper conversions.","k":"L2.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Keep a client calendar in the account notes: sales, holidays, launches, price changes, site updates. Half of all diagnoses are answered there.","k":"L2.5"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"Meta's ad relevance diagnostics.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Meta gives every ad with at least five hundred impressions three rankings, compared with ads competing for the same audience.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Quality ranking: perceived quality. Engagement rate ranking: expected engagement. Conversion rate ranking: expected conversion rate, compared with ads with the same optimisation goal.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta is clear they're diagnostics, not inputs into the auction. Use them to decide where to work. Meta says they help you understand whether to adjust creative, the post-click experience, or targeting.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Low quality ranking points at the creative itself or low-quality attributes, such as withholding information, sensational language or engagement bait, which Meta lists as low-quality signals. Low engagement ranking: the hook and message aren't interesting to this audience. Low conversion ranking with good engagement: people like the ad, but the offer or the landing page doesn't convert.","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Add the three ranking columns to your reports. Any ad that's below average on two or more is a candidate to replace, not to tweak.","k":"L3.4"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"The breakdown effect: don't fight the system.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Here's the classic misread. You open placements or ads and see that the one with the lowest CPA got the least budget. It looks like Meta is wasting money.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta calls this the breakdown effect: the misinterpretation that the system shifts spend into underperforming ad sets, placements or ads. Meta's example: Facebook Stories starts with a cheaper cost per result, but the system predicts its cost will rise as it spends more, so it shifts most of the budget to Instagram Stories. Instagram ends with the higher average cost, but the campaign gets more results overall.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"The reason: the cheapest results in a small slice don't stay cheap when you push more money into it. Average cost is not marginal cost.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta's rule: judge at the level where automation decides. With Advantage+ campaign budget, judge the campaign. With Advantage+ placements, judge the ad set. With several ads in one ad set, judge the ad set.","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/meta-help-breakdown-effect.png","cap":"Screenshot: Meta Business Help Center, \"About the breakdown effect\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L4.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Before you turn off a \"bad\" placement or ad, check the total at the level above. If the total is fine, leave it alone.","k":"L4.5"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"Google: search terms and Quality Score signals.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"On Google Search, diagnosis starts with what people actually typed. The search terms report shows how your ads performed for real searches.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Look for three things. Irrelevant searches eating budget: add negative keywords. Great searches you're not covering well: add them as keywords or give them their own ad group and landing page. And intent shifts: more \"free\", \"jobs\" or \"how to\" searches usually means wasted spend.","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Then look at Quality Score components for your biggest keywords: expected CTR, ad relevance and landing page experience. A component going from Average to Below average tells you which part to fix.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"p","text":"And check bid strategy and budget: is the campaign limited by budget, did a target change, or is it in a learning period after a change?","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Google drop","text":"Conversions down 30%. Search terms show a new flood of \"free\" and \"DIY\" searches from broad match. Fix: negatives for the irrelevant themes, and check that Smart Bidding has the right primary conversions.","k":"L5.4"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/google-help-search-terms.png","cap":"Screenshot: Google Ads Help, \"About the search terms report\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L5.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"Every week, spend ten minutes in the search terms report of each Search campaign. It's the cheapest win in Google Ads.","k":"L5.6"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"The fifteen-minute diagnosis routine.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Put it all together in one routine.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"One: confirm the drop is real. Tracking matches the client's numbers, and the period is comparable.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Two: walk the metric tree. Which of CPM, CTR, conversion rate or order value moved?","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Three: find where. Which campaign, ad set or ad moved, judged at the right level to avoid the breakdown effect.","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"p","text":"Four: find why. Relevance diagnostics, frequency and hook rate on Meta; search terms and Quality Score on Google; the landing page and the offer for conversion rate.","k":"L6.4"},{"t":"p","text":"Five: one clear action, logged, with a date to review.","k":"L6.5"},{"t":"warn","text":"Don't make five changes at once. If results recover, you won't know which one worked; if they don't, you've reset learning everywhere.","k":"L6.6"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write your diagnosis for the client in three lines: what happened, why we think so, what we're doing and when we'll check.","k":"L6.7"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["CPA = CPM ÷ 1,000 ÷ CTR ÷ CVR. ROAS adds order value. Find the link that moved.","Rule out tracking (compare with real orders) and the calendar first.","Meta: judge the optimisation event; CPM can rise while CPA falls.","Relevance diagnostics (≥500 impressions): quality, engagement, conversion rate rankings. Not auction inputs.","Breakdown effect: lowest average CPA ≠ best place for the next krona. Judge at the level automation decides.","Google: search terms report → negatives / new keywords; Quality Score components; budget, targets, learning.","Routine: real? → tree → where → why → one logged action."],"src":[{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About ad relevance diagnostics","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/403110480493160"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About ad quality","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/423781975167984"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About the breakdown effect","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/770303663944673"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: Understand fluctuations in ad performance","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/1364841787225722"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About the search terms report","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/2472708"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About negative keywords","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/2453972"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About Quality Score for Search campaigns","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/6167118"}],"quiz":[{"q":"CPA rose 60%. CPM and CTR are flat. Conversion rate fell from 3% to 1.8%. Where do you look?","o":["The ad creative","The bid strategy","After the click: landing page, price, stock, checkout, tracking","The audience age range"],"a":2,"e":"Only conversion rate moved, so the break is after the click."},{"q":"Purchases in Ads Manager dropped to almost zero overnight. Most likely cause?","o":["Tracking broke","The audience got tired overnight","CPM fell","The headline is too long"],"a":0,"e":"A cliff to near zero is almost always measurement. Compare with real orders."},{"q":"What does Meta say about ad relevance diagnostics and the auction?","o":["They decide who wins","They are not inputs into the auction","They replace your bid","They only apply to video"],"a":1,"e":"Diagnostics only. Not shown under 500 impressions."},{"q":"The placement with the lowest average CPA got the least budget. What's the right reading?","o":["Meta is wasting money; turn the other placement off","Probably the breakdown effect: judge the total at the ad set level","Double the budget on the cheap placement manually","Restart the campaign"],"a":1,"e":"Meta: the system predicts marginal cost; average cost isn't the next result's cost."},{"q":"With Advantage+ campaign budget, where should you judge performance?","o":["Ad level","Ad set level","Campaign level","Placement level"],"a":2,"e":"Meta: judge at campaign level with Advantage+ campaign budget."},{"q":"Engagement ranking is above average, conversion rate ranking below average. What do you fix?","o":["The hook","The offer or landing page","Nothing","The audience size"],"a":1,"e":"People like the ad but don't convert. Look post-click."},{"q":"Google conversions fell and search terms show many \"free\" and \"DIY\" queries. First fix?","o":["Raise all bids","Add negative keywords for irrelevant themes","Pause the campaign","Switch to Display"],"a":1,"e":"Stop paying for searches that won't convert."}],"drills":[{"type":"calc","title":"Walk the tree","prompt":["Use CPA = CPM ÷ 1,000 ÷ CTR ÷ CVR (CTR and CVR as decimals)."],"checks":[],"model":["Each part multiplies. A one-third drop in conversion rate means a 50% higher CPA."],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[{"q":"CPM 60 kr, CTR 1.5%, CVR 3%. CPA in kr?","a":133.3,"tol":1.0,"e":"60 ÷ 1000 ÷ 0.015 ÷ 0.03 = 133.3"},{"q":"CPM 60 kr, CTR 1.5%, CVR 2%. CPA in kr?","a":200.0,"tol":1.0,"e":"60 ÷ 1000 ÷ 0.015 ÷ 0.02 = 200"},{"q":"CPM 80 kr, CTR 2%, CVR 2.5%. CPA in kr?","a":160.0,"tol":1.0,"e":"80 ÷ 1000 ÷ 0.02 ÷ 0.025 = 160"},{"q":"CPA went from 100 to 150 kr with only CVR changing. Old CVR 3%. New CVR in %?","a":2.0,"tol":0.05,"e":"CPA ∝ 1/CVR: 3% × 100/150 = 2%"}]},{"type":"order","title":"The diagnosis routine","prompt":["Put the five steps in order."],"checks":[],"model":["Real → what → where → why → act once."],"items":["Confirm the drop is real: tracking vs real orders, comparable period","Walk the metric tree: CPM, CTR, CVR, order value","Find where, judged at the level automation decides","Find why: diagnostics, search terms, landing page, offer","One clear action, logged, with a review date"],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"sort","title":"Which link broke?","prompt":["For each finding, which part of the funnel is the problem?"],"checks":[],"model":["Measurement first, then market, creative, post-click. Each has a different fix."],"items":["Ads Manager shows 0 purchases; the shop shows 140 orders => Measurement","CPM up 35% in Black Friday week, CTR and CVR flat => Market","Engagement rate ranking fell to below average => Creative","Checkout error on iPhone since the site update => Post-click","Hook rate dropped from 28% to 14% => Creative","Price raised 20% on Monday, CVR down from Tuesday => Post-click"],"cats":["Measurement","Market","Creative","Post-click"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t11-1-07","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-07","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-07","title":"Example: CPM rose from 60 kr to 95 kr this week while CTR and co…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.1"},{"ref":"taste:t11-1-08","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-08","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-08","title":"What's missing or wrong? Tick everything that applies, then reve…","kind":"What's missing?","module":"11.1"},{"ref":"taste:t12-1-05","app":"taste","id":"t12-1-05","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t12-1-05","title":"Interview: 'Our client's ROAS dropped from 3.5 to 2.4 this month…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"12.1"}],"words":1129,"audio":{"duration":503.164,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. The metric tree.","s":25.28},{"t":"2. First rule out tracking and the calendar.","s":105.56},{"t":"3. Meta's ad relevance diagnostics.","s":188.09},{"t":"4. The breakdown effect: don't fight the system.","s":262.3},{"t":"5. Google: search terms and Quality Score signals.","s":339.69},{"t":"6. The fifteen-minute diagnosis routine.","s":425.99},{"t":"End","s":495.28}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":7.392},{"k":"lead.0","s":8.292,"d":10.128},{"k":"lead.1","s":19.02,"d":5.664},{"k":"L1.h","s":25.284,"d":2.232},{"k":"L1.0","s":28.016,"d":6.84},{"k":"L1.1","s":35.456,"d":16.464},{"k":"L1.2","s":52.52,"d":13.512},{"k":"L1.4","s":66.632,"d":28.104},{"k":"L1.5","s":95.336,"d":9.624},{"k":"L2.h","s":105.56,"d":3.744},{"k":"L2.0","s":109.804,"d":5.184},{"k":"L2.1","s":115.588,"d":16.584},{"k":"L2.2","s":132.772,"d":16.104},{"k":"L2.3","s":149.476,"d":13.176},{"k":"L2.4","s":163.252,"d":12.624},{"k":"L2.5","s":176.476,"d":11.016},{"k":"L3.h","s":188.092,"d":3.648},{"k":"L3.0","s":192.24,"d":7.704},{"k":"L3.1","s":200.544,"d":12.888},{"k":"L3.2","s":214.032,"d":12.648},{"k":"L3.3","s":227.28,"d":24.24},{"k":"L3.4","s":252.12,"d":9.576},{"k":"L4.h","s":262.296,"d":4.08},{"k":"L4.0","s":266.876,"d":10.824},{"k":"L4.1","s":278.3,"d":26.136},{"k":"L4.2","s":305.036,"d":9.624},{"k":"L4.3","s":315.26,"d":14.52},{"k":"L4.4","s":330.38,"d":8.712},{"k":"L5.h","s":339.692,"d":4.464},{"k":"L5.0","s":344.656,"d":9.072},{"k":"L5.1","s":354.328,"d":19.944},{"k":"L5.2","s":374.872,"d":13.056},{"k":"L5.3","s":388.528,"d":9.336},{"k":"L5.4","s":398.464,"d":17.448},{"k":"L5.5","s":416.512,"d":8.88},{"k":"L6.h","s":425.992,"d":3.888},{"k":"L6.0","s":430.38,"d":2.544},{"k":"L6.1","s":433.524,"d":6.96},{"k":"L6.2","s":441.084,"d":7.104},{"k":"L6.3","s":448.788,"d":8.64},{"k":"L6.4","s":458.028,"d":11.112},{"k":"L6.5","s":469.74,"d":4.992},{"k":"L6.6","s":475.332,"d":10.032},{"k":"L6.7","s":485.964,"d":8.712},{"k":"end","s":495.276,"d":6.288}],"file":"aa-t12-diagnosing-performance-the-metric-tree-r.mp3"}},{"n":13,"level":"Expert","platform":"Both","kicker":"Expert · Meta + Google · Testing","title":"Testing that tells the truth: hypotheses, A/B tests and experiments","sub":"How to run tests that end in a decision, not a debate","goal":["Write a test hypothesis with one variable, one metric and a decision rule.","Set up a clean test with Meta's A/B testing and Google's custom experiments.","Judge whether a difference is real or noise, and report it honestly."],"lead":["Most \"tests\" in ad accounts aren't tests. Someone turned an ad off, another one on, and called the result a learning.","Real testing is simple, but strict. This unit gives you the rules, the tools on both platforms, and a sanity check for small numbers."],"lessons":[{"title":"Start with a hypothesis.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Meta's help centre says it plainly: before you choose a variable to test, choose a hypothesis.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"A good hypothesis has four parts. The change: what's different. The metric: how you'll judge it. The reason: why you expect it to win. And the decision: what you'll do if it wins or loses.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"ex","head":"A hypothesis you can act on","text":"\"If we lead with the price, 'First bag half price', instead of the taste angle, cost per purchase will fall, because our reviews say price is the main blocker. If it wins, we brief five more price-led ads; if it loses, we keep the taste angle and test proof next.\"","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Test one variable at a time, or at least one idea at a time. If you change the image, the headline and the audience together, a win tells you nothing you can reuse.","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write the hypothesis in the test name or the change log, before you launch. If you can't write the decision part, don't run the test.","k":"L1.4"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"Meta: the A/B test tool, not on-off switching.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Meta's A/B testing compares versions of an ad strategy by changing a variable, such as images, text, audience or placement. Each version is shown to a separate segment of your audience, so nobody sees both, and the test determines which performs best.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta explicitly does not recommend testing informally by turning ad sets or campaigns on and off by hand, because it can lead to inefficient delivery and unreliable results.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"You can create an A/B test in Ads Manager, by duplicating a campaign, ad set or ad and changing one variable, or in the Experiments tool, which can compare up to five existing campaigns. You choose a key metric and a schedule long enough to yield reliable results.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"warn","text":"If you duplicate a campaign for a test, Meta notes that the budget is duplicated too. Check the total spend before you press publish.","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"For any decision worth more than a week's budget, use the A/B test tool. For everyday creative refresh, the normal system of many ads in one ad set is fine; that's optimisation, not a test.","k":"L2.4"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"Google: custom experiments.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"On Google, custom experiments let you test a change against your original campaign. The experiment shares the original campaign's traffic and budget, so you get a fair split.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Typical tests: a new bid strategy, a different landing page, broad match versus your current match types, or new ad text. Google supports custom experiments for Search, Display, Video and Hotel campaigns.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Google notes a few rules worth remembering. Changes you make to the original campaign during the experiment aren't copied to the experiment, and making changes to either side while it runs makes results harder to read. You can choose a success metric and ask Google to require statistical significance before declaring a winner, and optionally apply the winning changes automatically.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"Use an experiment for any change that can move a whole campaign, like switching bid strategy. Don't flip a big campaign and hope.","k":"L3.3"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"Is the difference real?","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Small numbers lie. Ten conversions against seven is not a winner; it's a coin landing slightly differently.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Three rules keep you honest. Decide the length and the metric before you start, and don't stop the moment one side is ahead, because checking every day and stopping at the first lead finds false winners. Get enough conversions on each side before you judge, which for small accounts may mean testing bigger ideas less often. And use the tool's own significance or confidence reading where there is one.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"ex","head":"A false winner","text":"Day 2: version B has 6 purchases, A has 3. \"B doubles sales!\" Day 14: A 41, B 38. There was never a real difference. The early lead was noise.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"warn","text":"Platforms report their own winners using their own measurement. That's useful, but say so in the report: \"According to Meta's A/B test…\".","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"When you have little volume, test big swings, like a new angle or a new offer, not button colours. Big differences are the only ones small accounts can detect.","k":"L4.4"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"What to test, in order.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Test what moves results most first.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"One: the offer, what people get and what it costs. Two: the angle, the reason to buy. Three: the hook, the first three seconds or the first line. Four: the format, video versus image versus carousel. Five: landing page changes. Last: small details like button colour or one word.","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"On Google Search, big tests are often bidding and match type strategies, and landing pages. On Meta, they're usually offer, angle and creative concept.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"Keep a test backlog for each client, ranked by expected impact and cost. Run the top one, log the result, and move to the next.","k":"L5.3"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"Reporting a test.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"A test report should take a busy client thirty seconds to read.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Say what you tested and why, the result with the numbers and the confidence, what you'll do now, and what you'll test next. Report losses too: a clean loss saves future money and is a real learning.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"ex","head":"Test report, 4 sentences","text":"\"We tested a price-led hook against our taste-led hook for 14 days, with an even split in Meta's A/B test tool. Price-led cost 18% less per purchase, 212 vs 259 kr, and Meta reported it as the winner. We're moving budget to price-led and briefing five new price-led variants. Next we'll test adding customer reviews to the landing page.\"","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write every test report in four sentences: what and why, result with numbers, what we're doing now, what's next.","k":"L6.3"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["Hypothesis first: change + metric + reason + decision.","One variable (or one idea) per test.","Meta A/B test: nobody sees both versions; Ads Manager or Experiments (up to 5 campaigns). Meta: don't test by switching on/off. Duplicating duplicates budget.","Google custom experiments: share traffic and budget; Search, Display, Video, Hotel; don't edit either side mid-test; optional significance requirement and auto-apply.","Fix length and metric in advance; don't stop at the first lead; get enough conversions.","Small accounts: test big swings (offer, angle), not button colours.","Report in 4 sentences, including losses."],"src":[{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About A/B testing for Meta ads","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/1738164643098669"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: Create an A/B test in Experiments","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/3506622486044209"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: Set up a custom experiment","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/6261395"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Meta say to do before choosing a variable to test?","o":["Set a bid cap","Choose a hypothesis","Double the budget","Duplicate the Pixel"],"a":1,"e":"Meta: choose a hypothesis first."},{"q":"Why does Meta not recommend testing by turning ad sets on and off?","o":["It's against policy","It can lead to inefficient delivery and unreliable results","It's too expensive","It deletes your data"],"a":1,"e":"Use the A/B test tool so audiences are split and nobody sees both."},{"q":"In a Meta A/B test, who sees each version?","o":["Everyone sees both","Separate segments; nobody sees both","Only people who clicked before","Only Instagram users"],"a":1,"e":"The audience is split so results are comparable."},{"q":"On Google, what happens if you edit the original campaign during a custom experiment?","o":["The change copies to the experiment","It isn't copied, and edits make results harder to interpret","The experiment stops","Google doubles the budget"],"a":1,"e":"Google: changes aren't reflected in the experiment."},{"q":"Day 2: B has 6 purchases, A has 3. What do you do?","o":["Declare B the winner","Keep running to the planned end and enough conversions","Pause A immediately","Double B's budget"],"a":1,"e":"Early leads are often noise. Stopping at the first lead finds false winners."},{"q":"A small clinic gets 40 leads a month. What's a sensible test?","o":["Button colour","A big swing, like a new offer or angle","10 versions of one headline","A one-word change"],"a":1,"e":"Small volume can only detect big differences."},{"q":"Which order of testing usually moves results most?","o":["Offer → angle → hook → format → landing page → small details","Button colour → font → offer","Small details first, offer last","Random order"],"a":0,"e":"Test the biggest levers first."}],"drills":[{"type":"write","title":"Write a hypothesis","prompt":["Write a test hypothesis for a dental clinic: change, metric, reason, decision. One or two sentences."],"checks":[{"kind":"include","arg":"if","label":"States the change (\"If we…\")"},{"kind":"include","arg":"cost per,cpa,leads,booking,conversion,ctr,rate","label":"Names a metric"},{"kind":"include","arg":"because","label":"Gives a reason"},{"kind":"include","arg":"then,we will,we'll,if it wins,if it loses","label":"States the decision"}],"model":["\"If we offer a free 15-minute consultation instead of 'Book a check-up', cost per lead will fall, because new patients hesitate to commit; if it wins we roll it out to all ads, if it loses we test showing the price.\""],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"pick","title":"Real test or not?","prompt":["Pick the setup that gives a trustworthy answer."],"checks":[],"model":[],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[{"a":"Meta A/B test, one variable changed, 14 days planned.","b":"Turn ad A off on Monday, ad B on, compare the weeks.","ok":"A","e":"Meta advises against informal on-off testing; weeks differ too."},{"a":"Google custom experiment for a new bid strategy, no edits mid-test.","b":"Switch the whole campaign to the new bid strategy and compare with last month.","ok":"A","e":"An experiment splits traffic at the same time; last month had different conditions."},{"a":"Change one idea: price-led vs taste-led hook.","b":"Change image, headline, audience and offer at once.","ok":"A","e":"One variable makes the result reusable."}],"calc":[]},{"type":"order","title":"Write the report","prompt":["Put the four sentences of a test report in order."],"checks":[],"model":["What and why → result → action → next. Thirty seconds for the client."],"items":["What we tested and why","The result with numbers and confidence","What we're doing now","What we'll test next"],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t11-2-01","app":"taste","id":"t11-2-01","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-2-01","title":"Reading a test: is the difference real?","kind":"Mini-lesson","module":"11.2"},{"ref":"taste:t11-2-02","app":"taste","id":"t11-2-02","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-2-02","title":"You check your A/B test every morning and stop it the first day …","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.2"},{"ref":"taste:t11-2-03","app":"taste","id":"t11-2-03","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-2-03","title":"Baseline conversion 2%, you want to detect a 20% relative lift (…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.2"},{"ref":"taste:t11-2-04","app":"taste","id":"t11-2-04","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-2-04","title":"Small clinic, ~40 leads/month. What kind of test makes sense?","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.2"},{"ref":"taste:t11-2-05","app":"taste","id":"t11-2-05","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-2-05","title":"Upworthy test: 79 clicks / 2,736 impressions vs 35 / 2,593. Is t…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.2"},{"ref":"taste:t11-2-06","app":"taste","id":"t11-2-06","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-2-06","title":"BRIEF: Write a short test report (≤ 120 words) for a client. Exa…","kind":"Write it","module":"11.2"}],"words":1021,"audio":{"duration":434.188,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. Start with a hypothesis.","s":26.56},{"t":"2. Meta: the A/B test tool, not on-off switching.","s":93.22},{"t":"3. Google: custom experiments.","s":172.9},{"t":"4. Is the difference real?","s":237.12},{"t":"5. What to test, in order.","s":313.24},{"t":"6. Reporting a test.","s":364.38},{"t":"End","s":425.89}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":7.248},{"k":"lead.0","s":8.148,"d":7.848},{"k":"lead.1","s":16.596,"d":9.36},{"k":"L1.h","s":26.556,"d":2.688},{"k":"L1.0","s":29.744,"d":6.528},{"k":"L1.1","s":36.872,"d":13.56},{"k":"L1.2","s":51.032,"d":21.072},{"k":"L1.3","s":72.704,"d":10.536},{"k":"L1.4","s":83.84,"d":8.784},{"k":"L2.h","s":93.224,"d":4.68},{"k":"L2.0","s":98.404,"d":17.328},{"k":"L2.1","s":116.332,"d":11.904},{"k":"L2.2","s":128.836,"d":18.072},{"k":"L2.3","s":147.508,"d":9.552},{"k":"L2.4","s":157.66,"d":14.64},{"k":"L3.h","s":172.9,"d":3.504},{"k":"L3.0","s":176.904,"d":10.872},{"k":"L3.1","s":188.376,"d":15.336},{"k":"L3.2","s":204.312,"d":22.728},{"k":"L3.3","s":227.64,"d":8.88},{"k":"L4.h","s":237.12,"d":2.472},{"k":"L4.0","s":240.092,"d":6.864},{"k":"L4.1","s":247.556,"d":25.608},{"k":"L4.2","s":273.764,"d":15.984},{"k":"L4.3","s":290.348,"d":10.272},{"k":"L4.4","s":301.22,"d":11.424},{"k":"L5.h","s":313.244,"d":2.736},{"k":"L5.0","s":316.48,"d":2.592},{"k":"L5.1","s":319.672,"d":22.032},{"k":"L5.2","s":342.304,"d":10.872},{"k":"L5.3","s":353.776,"d":10.008},{"k":"L6.h","s":364.384,"d":2.544},{"k":"L6.0","s":367.428,"d":4.416},{"k":"L6.1","s":372.444,"d":12.912},{"k":"L6.2","s":385.956,"d":30.072},{"k":"L6.3","s":416.628,"d":8.664},{"k":"end","s":425.892,"d":6.696}],"file":"aa-t13-testing-that-tells-the-truth-hypotheses.mp3"}},{"n":14,"level":"Expert","platform":"Both","kicker":"Expert · Meta + Google · Automation","title":"Performance Max, AI Max and Advantage+: steering the automation","sub":"The machines run the auctions. Your job is the inputs, the guardrails and the judgement","goal":["Explain how Performance Max works: asset groups, audience signals, search themes and controls.","Explain what AI Max for Search adds, and how it fits with keywords.","Set guardrails on automated campaigns and judge them fairly against manual ones."],"lead":["Both platforms now push you towards campaigns where AI picks the audience, the placement and sometimes even the text. Fighting that is a losing game. Feeding it blindly is worse.","The expert skill is steering: give the system the right goal, the right material and the right fences, then check its work."],"lessons":[{"title":"What automation needs from you.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Every automated campaign type, on either platform, runs on the same four inputs.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"The goal: which conversions count and what they're worth. That's tracking and primary conversions from Unit 5, and targets from Unit 9.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"The material: creative assets, product feeds and landing pages. The more varied and relevant, the more combinations the system can try.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"p","text":"The signals: who your customers are, through customer lists, audience signals and search themes.","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"p","text":"The fences: what it must never do. Locations, ages, brand exclusions, negative keywords, policy rules.","k":"L1.4"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d14-automation-inputs.svg","cap":"Diagram: automation inputs. Goal, material, signals and fences go in; the system chooses auctions, audiences and combinations (own diagram).","k":"L1.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"When an automated campaign underperforms, check the four inputs before blaming the machine. Most problems are a wrong goal or thin material.","k":"L1.6"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"Performance Max: one campaign, all of Google.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Google describes Performance Max as a goal-based campaign type that reaches all Google Ads inventory, including YouTube, Display, Search, Discover, Gmail and Maps, from a single campaign. Google positions it as a complement to keyword-based Search campaigns.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"You create it with the Sales, Leads, Website traffic, or Local store visits objectives. It bids with Smart Bidding, optionally with a CPA or ROAS target.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Inside, you build asset groups: collections of images, text, logos and videos, each centred on a theme or audience. Google mixes and matches them into ads for each placement.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Audience signals, like your customer lists, tell the system where to start looking. Google says they accelerate learning. Search themes guide it towards valuable search intent.","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Asset groups for a bike shop","text":"Asset group 1: e-bikes, with e-bike photos, \"Test ride an e-bike this weekend\". Asset group 2: service, with workshop photos, \"Same-day service if in by 10\". Different themes, different signals, different landing pages.","k":"L2.4"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/google-help-pmax.png","cap":"Screenshot: Google Ads Help, \"About Performance Max campaigns\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L2.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"Build one asset group per real theme or product line, each with its own images, text and landing page. Don't dump everything into one.","k":"L2.6"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"Performance Max controls and the Search overlap.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Performance Max can show on Search, so you need to know how it interacts with your Search campaigns.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Google's rule: when a search matches an exact match keyword in a Search campaign, that Search campaign is prioritised over Performance Max. Search themes carry the same priority as phrase and broad match keywords. If the search isn't identical to an eligible keyword, the campaign or ad with the highest Ad Rank is chosen.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Google lists the controls you have: brand exclusions and negative keywords to prevent overlaps and block unwanted searches, search themes, final URL expansion, which lets Google send people to other relevant pages on your site, and brand safety settings like placement exclusions.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"warn","text":"With final URL expansion on, Google notes you remain responsible for the accuracy and compliance of the landing pages and any generated assets. For clinics and regulated clients, review or limit it.","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Protect your brand searches: run a brand Search campaign with exact match brand keywords, and consider brand exclusions in Performance Max, so you can see what brand demand really costs.","k":"L3.4"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"AI Max for Search campaigns.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"AI Max is a set of features you switch on inside a normal Search campaign. Google describes three parts: search term matching that expands beyond your keywords using broad match and keywordless technology; text customisation that adapts your headlines and descriptions; and final URL expansion to match the landing page to the searcher's intent.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Google's own figure: advertisers who activate AI Max typically see fourteen percent more conversions or conversion value at a similar CPA or ROAS, based on Google's internal 2025 data for non-retail advertisers.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"The trade-off is control. More matching means more searches you didn't choose, and generated text you didn't write.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"If you test AI Max, run it as a Google custom experiment from Unit 13, check the search terms weekly, and review the generated text for accuracy and policy.","k":"L4.3"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"Meta's automation, one more time.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"On Meta, the same pattern is Advantage+: Advantage+ sales campaigns, audience, placements, campaign budget and creative, covered in Units 7 and 9.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"The inputs map exactly. Goal: the conversion event and bid strategy. Material: many diversified ads. Signals: customer lists and audience suggestions. Fences: location, minimum age, language and exclusions.","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"ex","head":"Same thinking, two platforms","text":"A shop runs Advantage+ sales on Meta and Performance Max on Google. Both get the same purchase event with value, the same customer list as a signal, fresh creative every week, and the same guardrail: existing customers defined, so new-customer results can be reported.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write a one-page \"automation brief\" per client: goal and target, material refresh schedule, signals, and fences. Use it for both platforms.","k":"L5.3"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"Judging automation fairly.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Automated campaigns are hard to judge by looking inside them. Judge them by outcomes, at the right level.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Compare total results, not slices: the breakdown effect from Unit 12 applies here too. Give them a fair learning period without constant edits. Watch for cannibalisation: if Performance Max \"wins\" mostly on brand searches you'd have got anyway, its real value is lower. And where you can, use a proper experiment instead of a before-and-after comparison.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"warn","text":"Platform-reported results are measured by the platform selling you the ads. Cross-check with the client's own sales data, and be careful when both platforms claim the same sale.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"Every month, ask one question for each automated campaign: did total business results improve compared with what we'd have had without it? If you can't answer, plan a test.","k":"L6.3"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["Automation inputs: goal, material, signals, fences.","Performance Max: all Google inventory, one campaign; Sales, Leads, Website traffic, Local store visits; Smart Bidding.","Asset groups = themed bundles of assets; audience signals accelerate learning; search themes guide intent.","Exact match Search keyword beats PMax; search themes = phrase/broad priority; otherwise highest Ad Rank.","PMax controls: brand exclusions, negative keywords, search themes, final URL expansion (you own the accuracy), placement exclusions.","AI Max for Search: search term matching, text customisation, final URL expansion. Google claims +14% (internal, non-retail).","Judge by total outcomes, watch brand cannibalisation, cross-check with real sales, test with experiments."],"src":[{"label":"Google Ads Help: About Performance Max campaigns","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/10724817"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About AI Max for Search campaigns","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/15910366"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: Ad Rank: Definition","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/1752122"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About keyword matching options","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/7478529"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About Advantage+ sales campaigns","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/1362234537597370"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a Performance Max asset group?","o":["A list of keywords","A collection of images, text, logos and videos centred on a theme or audience","A budget split","A remarketing list"],"a":1,"e":"Google mixes and matches the assets into ads across inventory."},{"q":"A search exactly matches an exact match keyword in your Search campaign and could also match PMax. Which serves?","o":["The Search campaign with the exact match keyword","Performance Max always","Whichever has the larger budget","Both at once"],"a":0,"e":"Google prioritises the exact match Search keyword."},{"q":"What does Google say audience signals do in Performance Max?","o":["Restrict who can see ads","Accelerate learning by showing where to start","Replace conversion tracking","Set the budget"],"a":1,"e":"Signals are a starting point, not a hard fence."},{"q":"Which is NOT one of the three parts of AI Max for Search?","o":["Search term matching","Text customisation","Final URL expansion","Manual CPC bidding"],"a":3,"e":"AI Max: matching, text customisation, final URL expansion."},{"q":"With final URL expansion on, who is responsible for landing page and generated asset accuracy?","o":["Google","You, the advertiser","Nobody","The website host"],"a":1,"e":"Google: you remain responsible for compliance and accuracy."},{"q":"Performance Max reports great ROAS, but most conversions come from brand searches. What's the concern?","o":["None, ROAS is ROAS","Cannibalisation: those buyers were already looking for you","The CPM is too low","Asset groups are too many"],"a":1,"e":"Brand buyers might have converted anyway. Protect brand and judge new demand."},{"q":"Best way to judge whether turning on AI Max helped?","o":["Compare this month with last month","Run a custom experiment with a fair traffic split","Ask Google's rep","Look at Ad Strength"],"a":1,"e":"Experiments split traffic at the same time; before/after mixes in other changes."}],"drills":[{"type":"sort","title":"Which input is it?","prompt":["Sort each item into the automation input it belongs to."],"checks":[],"model":["Goal says what to find, material gives options, signals show where to start, fences say what's off-limits."],"items":["Purchase with value set as the primary conversion => Goal","Twelve new videos and images per month => Material","Customer list uploaded as an audience signal => Signals","Brand exclusions in Performance Max => Fences","Target ROAS 400% => Goal","Search themes like \"e-bike test ride\" => Signals","Negative keywords for \"jobs\" and \"free\" => Fences","A product feed with good titles and images => Material"],"cats":["Goal","Material","Signals","Fences"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"pick","title":"Which setup is better steered?","prompt":["Pick the better-steered automated setup."],"checks":[],"model":[],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[{"a":"PMax with 3 asset groups by product line, each with its own landing page; brand Search campaign on exact match.","b":"PMax with one asset group containing everything; no brand campaign.","ok":"A","e":"Themed asset groups and protected brand searches give cleaner signals and fairer reporting."},{"a":"AI Max tested as an experiment, search terms checked weekly.","b":"AI Max switched on in every campaign at once, nobody checks the search terms.","ok":"A","e":"Test, then watch the new matches and generated text."}],"calc":[]},{"type":"write","title":"Your automation brief","prompt":["Write a 4-line automation brief for an online furniture shop: goal, material, signals, fences."],"checks":[{"kind":"lines","arg":"4","label":"Four lines"},{"kind":"include","arg":"roas,cpa,purchase,value","label":"Goal names a conversion or target"},{"kind":"include","arg":"video,image,feed,asset,creative","label":"Material mentions creative or feed"},{"kind":"include","arg":"exclu,negative,brand,location","label":"Fences include an exclusion or limit"}],"model":["Goal: purchases with value, Target ROAS 350% (break-even 250%). / Material: product feed + 10 new lifestyle images and 4 videos per month. / Signals: customer list, search themes \"solid oak dining table\", \"sofa delivery\". / Fences: Sweden only, brand exclusions in PMax, negatives for \"used\" and \"free\"."],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t6-1-06","app":"taste","id":"t6-1-06","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t6-1-06","title":"Mixed: which ad set structure fits Meta's Andromeda guidance?","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"6.1"},{"ref":"taste:t10-1-04","app":"taste","id":"t10-1-04","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t10-1-04","title":"Two RSAs in an ad group. Which set of headlines gives Google mor…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"10.1"}],"words":988,"audio":{"duration":460.636,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. What automation needs from you.","s":29.92},{"t":"2. Performance Max: one campaign, all of Google.","s":86.17},{"t":"3. Performance Max controls and the Search overlap.","s":178.01},{"t":"4. AI Max for Search campaigns.","s":257.64},{"t":"5. Meta's automation, one more time.","s":321.16},{"t":"6. Judging automation fairly.","s":391.02},{"t":"End","s":452.72}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":6.816},{"k":"lead.0","s":7.716,"d":12.96},{"k":"lead.1","s":21.276,"d":8.04},{"k":"L1.h","s":29.916,"d":2.736},{"k":"L1.0","s":33.152,"d":5.856},{"k":"L1.1","s":39.608,"d":8.688},{"k":"L1.2","s":48.896,"d":9.48},{"k":"L1.3","s":58.976,"d":6.624},{"k":"L1.4","s":66.2,"d":8.616},{"k":"L1.6","s":75.416,"d":10.152},{"k":"L2.h","s":86.168,"d":4.896},{"k":"L2.0","s":91.564,"d":17.28},{"k":"L2.1","s":109.444,"d":11.088},{"k":"L2.2","s":121.132,"d":13.632},{"k":"L2.3","s":135.364,"d":10.464},{"k":"L2.4","s":146.428,"d":19.872},{"k":"L2.6","s":166.9,"d":10.512},{"k":"L3.h","s":178.012,"d":4.32},{"k":"L3.0","s":182.832,"d":5.688},{"k":"L3.1","s":189.12,"d":20.952},{"k":"L3.2","s":210.672,"d":18.168},{"k":"L3.3","s":229.44,"d":14.88},{"k":"L3.4","s":244.92,"d":12.12},{"k":"L4.h","s":257.64,"d":3.36},{"k":"L4.0","s":261.5,"d":21.648},{"k":"L4.1","s":283.748,"d":16.848},{"k":"L4.2","s":301.196,"d":7.08},{"k":"L4.3","s":308.876,"d":11.688},{"k":"L5.h","s":321.164,"d":3.384},{"k":"L5.0","s":325.048,"d":10.968},{"k":"L5.1","s":336.616,"d":18.288},{"k":"L5.2","s":355.504,"d":22.704},{"k":"L5.3","s":378.808,"d":11.616},{"k":"L6.h","s":391.024,"d":3.216},{"k":"L6.0","s":394.74,"d":7.056},{"k":"L6.1","s":402.396,"d":23.784},{"k":"L6.2","s":426.78,"d":12.0},{"k":"L6.3","s":439.38,"d":12.744},{"k":"end","s":452.724,"d":6.312}],"file":"aa-t14-performance-max-ai-max-and-advantage-ste.mp3"}},{"n":15,"level":"Expert","platform":"Both","kicker":"Expert · Meta + Google · Measurement and scaling","title":"Measurement and scaling: attribution, incrementality and growing spend","sub":"Which sales did the ads really cause, and how do you spend more without wrecking results?","goal":["Explain Meta's attribution models and settings, and Google's data-driven attribution.","Explain why Meta and Google both claim the same sale, and how to report the truth.","Scale an account through budget, targets and creative without breaking learning."],"lead":["Every platform counts the sales it can link to its ads. Add them up across platforms and the total is often bigger than the shop's real sales.","An expert knows why, can explain it to a client in one minute, and scales spend based on real business results, not on the platform's best-case story."],"lessons":[{"title":"Attribution: who gets the credit?","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Attribution is the rule that decides which ad gets credit for a conversion.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"On Meta, you choose an attribution model at the ad set level. Standard attribution credits conversions within time windows after a click, a view or an engagement. Incremental attribution optimises and reports on conversions Meta's models predict were caused by the ad. Custom attribution lets you share data from your own analytics tool.","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"For standard attribution, Meta lists these settings for website conversions: click-through within one day or seven days after a link click; view-through within one day after an impression; and engage-through within one day after a non-link click, or a qualifying video play.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta warns that you can't compare results across ad sets with different attribution models, because they count differently.","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"warn","text":"Always note which attribution setting a report uses. \"ROAS 4 on 7-day click and 1-day view\" and \"ROAS 4 on 1-day click\" are very different claims.","k":"L1.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"When you inherit a Meta account, open the attribution setting of each ad set and write it at the top of your report.","k":"L1.5"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"Google's data-driven attribution.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"On Google, each conversion action has an attribution model. Google says data-driven attribution is the default for most conversion actions.","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Data-driven attribution compares the paths of people who convert with those who don't, across Search, Shopping, YouTube, Display and Demand Gen interactions, and shares credit based on which interactions actually made a difference. It's specific to each advertiser.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"All conversion actions are eligible, but Google says it works better with more data, and recommends at least two hundred conversions and two thousand ad interactions in supported networks within thirty days. With little data, it can give the same results as last click.","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"p","text":"And because Smart Bidding uses this credit, attribution changes what you pay for, not just what you see.","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"ex","head":"Google's own example, retold","text":"A tour company finds that people who first click a general \"bike tour New York\" ad and later a specific \"Brooklyn waterfront\" ad are more likely to buy. Data-driven attribution gives the first ad some credit, so bidding values it too.","k":"L2.4"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/google-help-attribution-dda.png","cap":"Screenshot: Google Ads Help, \"About data-driven attribution\". Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L2.5"},{"t":"apply","text":"Check the attribution model of every primary conversion action. If an old one is still on last click, ask why.","k":"L2.6"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"Why the platforms add up to more than 100%.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"A customer sees a Meta video on Monday and clicks a Google ad on Thursday, then buys. Meta can count the sale under its attribution window. Google can count it too. Both are right by their own rules. The shop made one sale.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"That's why the expert's first report number comes from the client's own data: total sales, new customers, revenue. Platform numbers explain where things happened; the business numbers tell you whether it worked.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"A simple cross-check is the blended view: total ad spend across all platforms divided into total revenue, or total new customers. Many agencies call these blended ROAS and blended cost per acquisition. If platform ROAS rises but blended results don't, the platforms are claiming more credit, not creating more sales.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"fig","file":"diagrams/d15-double-count.svg","cap":"Diagram: one sale, two claims. A Meta view and a Google click both get credit under their own rules; the shop counts one sale (own diagram).","k":"L3.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Put one blended line at the top of every monthly report: total spend, total revenue or leads from the client's system, and the blended ratio.","k":"L3.4"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"Incrementality: did the ads cause it?","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Attribution says which ad was near the sale. Incrementality asks the harder question: would the sale have happened without the ad?","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Brand searches and retargeting often look brilliant in attribution, because those people were already on their way. Prospecting to new people often looks weaker, but may create more truly new sales.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Meta offers incremental attribution, which predicts whether a conversion was caused by an ad. Proper lift tests and experiments, which hold back a group that doesn't see ads, give the strongest answer. Google's custom experiments, from Unit 13, are one tool for testing changes.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"Brand trap","text":"Brand search ROAS is 15. The client wants to double it. But most of those searchers would have clicked the organic result anyway. Doubling brand spend won't double sales. Prospecting with ROAS 2.5 may be creating more new customers.","k":"L4.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"When a channel looks too good, ask: \"Who would have bought anyway?\" Then plan a test if the money at stake is big.","k":"L4.4"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"Clean data: enhanced conversions and consent.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Measurement is getting harder as browsers and privacy rules limit cookies. Two tools from Unit 5 matter here at expert level.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Enhanced conversions on Google send hashed first-party data, such as email, to improve measurement accuracy. The Conversions API on Meta sends events from your server alongside the Pixel.","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Consent mode on Google passes consent choices to tags. In advanced mode, tags send cookieless pings when consent is denied, which supports modelling specific to your account. Basic mode blocks tags until consent and uses a general model.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"p","text":"For European clients, these choices directly affect how many conversions you see, and so how well bidding works.","k":"L5.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"For every European account, write down: consent mode basic or advanced, enhanced conversions on or off, Conversions API on or off. That's your measurement health line.","k":"L5.4"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"Scaling without breaking it.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Scaling means spending more while keeping results acceptable. Results usually get more expensive as you spend more, because the cheapest customers are found first. So decide in advance what CPA or ROAS you'll accept at the margin.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"There are three levers. Budget: raise it in planned steps, and remember Meta says frequent budget changes can affect learning. Targets: on Google, lowering target ROAS or raising target CPA lets bidding enter more auctions. Creative and reach: more diversified ads, new angles and new audiences, new placements or new campaign types give the system more room to find buyers.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Watch total business results as you scale, not only platform ROAS.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"warn","text":"Don't scale a campaign that's still in learning or whose tracking you haven't checked. You'll just spend more on noise.","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write a scaling plan before increasing spend: the step size, the review date, the marginal CPA or ROAS you'll accept, and what you'll do if it's worse.","k":"L6.4"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["Meta attribution models: standard, incremental, custom. Standard settings: click 1 or 7 days, view 1 day, engage 1 day. Don't compare across models.","Google: data-driven attribution is the default for most actions; works better with ≥200 conversions and 2,000 interactions in 30 days; affects Smart Bidding.","Platforms each count the same sale by their own rules. Report business numbers first, plus blended spend ÷ revenue.","Incrementality: would it have happened anyway? Brand and retargeting often overstate. Test when stakes are big.","Measurement health: consent mode (basic/advanced), enhanced conversions, Conversions API.","Scale with budget steps, targets and creative; decide your acceptable marginal CPA/ROAS first."],"src":[{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About attribution models and attribution settings","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/2198119873776795"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About data-driven attribution","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/6394265"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About enhanced conversions","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/9888656"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About consent mode","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/10000067"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About Conversions API","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/2041148702652965"},{"label":"Meta Business Help Center: About the learning phase","url":"https://www.facebook.com/business/help/112167992830700"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: About Target ROAS bidding","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/6268637"},{"label":"Google Ads Help: Set up a custom experiment","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/6261395"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which Meta standard attribution settings exist for website conversions?","o":["28-day click, 7-day view","1-day or 7-day click, 1-day view, 1-day engage","Only last click","30-day view"],"a":1,"e":"Meta's current standard settings."},{"q":"Meta's incremental attribution model…","o":["Counts every view for 28 days","Optimises and reports on conversions predicted to be caused by the ad","Is the same as last click","Only works on Google"],"a":1,"e":"Incremental attribution predicts whether a conversion was caused by an ad."},{"q":"Which Google attribution model is the default for most conversion actions?","o":["Last click","First click","Data-driven","Linear"],"a":2,"e":"Google: data-driven attribution is the default for most conversion actions."},{"q":"Meta reports 120 purchases and Google reports 110. The shop had 180 orders. What's the most likely explanation?","o":["Fraud","Both platforms credit some of the same sales under their own rules","The shop is wrong","Tracking is double-counting inside each platform"],"a":1,"e":"Overlap across platforms is normal. Report business numbers first."},{"q":"Brand search shows ROAS 15. Why be careful about doubling the budget?","o":["Brand keywords are banned","Many of those buyers would have bought anyway, so extra spend may add few new sales","ROAS 15 is too low","Google caps brand spend"],"a":1,"e":"Attribution isn't incrementality."},{"q":"What does Google say data-driven attribution works best with?","o":["At least 10 conversions","At least 200 conversions and 2,000 ad interactions in 30 days","Only Display campaigns","Manual CPC bidding"],"a":1,"e":"It works with any volume, but better with more data."},{"q":"Which is a safe scaling move?","o":["Triple the budget every day until it breaks","Raise budget in planned steps with a review date and an accepted marginal CPA","Scale while the campaign is still in learning","Turn off tracking to save cost"],"a":1,"e":"Plan, step, review, against a limit you set in advance."}],"drills":[{"type":"calc","title":"Blended numbers","prompt":["Work out the blended figures from the client's own data."],"checks":[],"model":["Platform numbers overlap. Blended numbers from the business can't."],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[{"q":"Meta spend 30,000 kr, Google spend 20,000 kr, shop revenue from all sources 200,000 kr. Blended ROAS (x)?","a":4.0,"tol":0.05,"e":"200,000 ÷ 50,000 = 4"},{"q":"Total ad spend 50,000 kr, 250 new customers in the shop system. Blended cost per new customer in kr?","a":200.0,"tol":1.0,"e":"50,000 ÷ 250 = 200"},{"q":"Meta claims 120 sales, Google 110, the shop had 180. How many sales are claimed twice (at least)?","a":50.0,"tol":0.5,"e":"120 + 110 − 180 = 50"}]},{"type":"sort","title":"Attribution or incrementality?","prompt":["Is each statement about attribution (who gets credit) or incrementality (what the ads caused)?"],"checks":[],"model":["Attribution assigns credit. Incrementality measures cause."],"items":["\"The sale came within 7 days of a click on our Meta ad.\" => Attribution","\"Would they have bought anyway without the ad?\" => Incrementality","\"Data-driven model gave the first click 40% of the credit.\" => Attribution","\"We held back 10% of the audience from ads and compared sales.\" => Incrementality","\"Google and Meta both counted this purchase.\" => Attribution"],"cats":["Attribution","Incrementality"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"order","title":"Scaling plan","prompt":["Put the steps of a scaling plan in order."],"checks":[],"model":["Safe base → limit → step → wait → decide."],"items":["Check tracking and that the campaign is out of learning","Decide the marginal CPA or ROAS you'll accept","Raise budget one planned step (or loosen the target)","Wait and review at the planned date","Keep, step again, or roll back based on business results"],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t12-1-05","app":"taste","id":"t12-1-05","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t12-1-05","title":"Interview: 'Our client's ROAS dropped from 3.5 to 2.4 this month…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"12.1"},{"ref":"taste:t11-1-04","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-04","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-04","title":"Example numbers (DTC store): ad spend 10,000 kr, revenue 32,000 …","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.1"},{"ref":"taste:t11-1-09","app":"taste","id":"t11-1-09","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t11-1-09","title":"Example: a DTC store has a 40% gross margin. What's the break-ev…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"11.1"}],"words":1099,"audio":{"duration":484.684,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. Attribution: who gets the credit?","s":28.5},{"t":"2. Google's data-driven attribution.","s":109.16},{"t":"3. Why the platforms add up to more than 100%.","s":192.82},{"t":"4. Incrementality: did the ads cause it?","s":260.71},{"t":"5. Clean data: enhanced conversions and consent.","s":335.08},{"t":"6. Scaling without breaking it.","s":403.05},{"t":"End","s":476.34}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":7.104},{"k":"lead.0","s":8.004,"d":9.432},{"k":"lead.1","s":18.036,"d":9.864},{"k":"L1.h","s":28.5,"d":3.264},{"k":"L1.0","s":32.264,"d":4.704},{"k":"L1.1","s":37.568,"d":22.224},{"k":"L1.2","s":60.392,"d":17.688},{"k":"L1.3","s":78.68,"d":7.056},{"k":"L1.4","s":86.336,"d":13.92},{"k":"L1.5","s":100.856,"d":7.704},{"k":"L2.h","s":109.16,"d":3.552},{"k":"L2.0","s":113.212,"d":9.096},{"k":"L2.1","s":122.908,"d":17.16},{"k":"L2.2","s":140.668,"d":16.128},{"k":"L2.3","s":157.396,"d":6.984},{"k":"L2.4","s":164.98,"d":18.528},{"k":"L2.6","s":184.108,"d":8.112},{"k":"L3.h","s":192.82,"d":4.536},{"k":"L3.0","s":197.856,"d":14.904},{"k":"L3.1","s":213.36,"d":13.008},{"k":"L3.2","s":226.968,"d":21.984},{"k":"L3.4","s":249.552,"d":10.56},{"k":"L4.h","s":260.712,"d":3.624},{"k":"L4.0","s":264.836,"d":8.76},{"k":"L4.1","s":274.196,"d":13.32},{"k":"L4.2","s":288.116,"d":18.72},{"k":"L4.3","s":307.436,"d":18.384},{"k":"L4.4","s":326.42,"d":8.064},{"k":"L5.h","s":335.084,"d":4.92},{"k":"L5.0","s":340.504,"d":9.168},{"k":"L5.1","s":350.272,"d":12.936},{"k":"L5.2","s":363.808,"d":16.848},{"k":"L5.3","s":381.256,"d":7.704},{"k":"L5.4","s":389.56,"d":12.888},{"k":"L6.h","s":403.048,"d":3.144},{"k":"L6.0","s":406.692,"d":15.144},{"k":"L6.1","s":422.436,"d":27.24},{"k":"L6.2","s":450.276,"d":5.232},{"k":"L6.3","s":456.108,"d":7.56},{"k":"L6.4","s":464.268,"d":11.472},{"k":"end","s":476.34,"d":6.744}],"file":"aa-t15-measurement-and-scaling-attribution-incr.mp3"}},{"n":16,"level":"Expert","platform":"Both","kicker":"Expert · Meta + Google · The agency job","title":"Reporting to clients and doing the job: audits, reports, research and interviews","sub":"Turn everything you've learned into the weekly work an agency pays for","goal":["Write a client report that leads with business results and ends with clear next steps.","Run a first-day account audit on Meta and Google.","Research competitors with the Meta Ad Library and Google Ads Transparency Center, and talk about your work in an interview."],"lead":["Clients don't buy CPMs. They buy more customers, and the confidence that someone competent is watching their money.","This last unit is the job itself: the audit you run on day one, the report you send every month, the research you do before every brief, and how you talk about it in an interview."],"lessons":[{"title":"The first-day audit.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"When you get access to a new account, don't change anything for the first hour. Look.","k":"L1.0"},{"t":"p","text":"One: tracking. Is the main conversion firing, does it match the client's own numbers, are the right actions primary on Google, is deduplication set up on Meta?","k":"L1.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Two: goals. What's the business goal, what's a customer worth, what's the margin? Without these, no target means anything.","k":"L1.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Three: structure. How many campaigns and ad sets, and how many conversions does each get per week? Too many small ones can't learn.","k":"L1.3"},{"t":"p","text":"Four: creative. How many ads are running, how different are they, how old are they, what are hook rates and frequency?","k":"L1.4"},{"t":"p","text":"Five: Google specifics. Search terms, negatives, match types, Quality Score components, Ad Strength, sitelinks, Performance Max overlaps with brand.","k":"L1.5"},{"t":"p","text":"Six: policy. Any rejected ads, any health or personal-attribute risks, any remarketing lists in sensitive categories.","k":"L1.6"},{"t":"apply","text":"Use these six headings as your audit template. Write one line per heading, with the biggest risk and the biggest opportunity.","k":"L1.7"}],"k":"L1.h"},{"title":"The monthly report.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"A good report answers four questions, in this order. Did it work? Why? What did we learn? What's next?","k":"L2.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Start with business results from the client's own data: sales or leads, revenue, new customers, and spend, compared with last month and the same month last year if seasonal. Then platform metrics that explain why, using the metric chain. Then the tests you ran and what they taught you. Then the plan for next month, with what you need from the client.","k":"L2.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Keep the language plain. Replace \"CTR rose 0.4 points\" with \"more people clicked: 2.1% vs 1.7%\".","k":"L2.2"},{"t":"ex","head":"Report opener, three lines","text":"\"October: 212 booked consultations from ads, up 18% on September, at 410 kr each, down from 455 kr. The win came from the new price-led videos, which halved cost per lead on Meta. In November we'll extend that angle to Google with new ad text, and we need the December opening hours by the 15th.\"","k":"L2.3"},{"t":"warn","text":"Never hide bad news in a report. Say it first, with the cause and the fix. Clients forgive bad months; they don't forgive surprises.","k":"L2.4"},{"t":"apply","text":"Write the first three lines of every report before making a single chart. If the opener is clear, the rest is support.","k":"L2.5"}],"k":"L2.h"},{"title":"Talking to clients between reports.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Most client trust is built in short messages, not in reports.","k":"L3.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Set expectations at the start: learning phases, normal daily fluctuation, and how long before you judge. Unit 9's budget one-liner is perfect for this.","k":"L3.1"},{"t":"p","text":"When something goes wrong, send a short note before they notice: what happened, what you're doing, when you'll update. When a test wins, share it with one number.","k":"L3.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"Keep a short weekly note to each client: one result, one learning, one next step. Three lines, every week.","k":"L3.3"}],"k":"L3.h"},{"title":"Competitor research: Meta Ad Library.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Before every brief, look at what competitors are running. On Meta, the Ad Library lets anyone search all the ads currently running across Meta technologies. It also includes ads about social issues, elections or politics from the past seven years, and ads that have run anywhere in the EU in the past year. Meta says an ad appears in the library within twenty-four hours of its first impression.","k":"L4.0"},{"t":"p","text":"What to look for: angles, offers, formats, hooks, and how long ads have been running. An ad that's been live for months is a decent sign it works for that advertiser, though it's not proof.","k":"L4.1"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/meta-ad-library.png","cap":"Screenshot: Meta Ad Library start page (facebook.com/ads/library). Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L4.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"For each new client, save ten competitor ads from the Ad Library into a swipe file, with a note on the angle and how long each has run.","k":"L4.3"}],"k":"L4.h"},{"title":"Competitor research: Google Ads Transparency Center.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"On Google, the Ads Transparency Center lets you search ads by advertiser name or website, across Google products like Search and YouTube.","k":"L5.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Use it like the Ad Library: which messages and offers competitors use, which formats, and whether they're advertising at all. If no competitor advertises on a valuable search, that's an opportunity.","k":"L5.1"},{"t":"fig","file":"shots/google-ads-transparency.png","cap":"Screenshot: Google Ads Transparency Center start page (adstransparency.google.com). Captured with headless Chrome, November 2026; public page shown for teaching.","k":"L5.2"},{"t":"apply","text":"Before writing RSA headlines, search three competitors in the Transparency Center. Make sure your headlines say something theirs don't.","k":"L5.3"}],"k":"L5.h"},{"title":"The interview: show, don't claim.","items":[{"t":"p","text":"Agencies hiring juniors look for people who can think with numbers and make ads. Prove both.","k":"L6.0"},{"t":"p","text":"Bring a small portfolio: a teardown of a real brand's ads from the Ad Library, a three-ad test plan, a sample report with made-up but realistic numbers clearly labelled as an example, and a few ads you wrote.","k":"L6.1"},{"t":"p","text":"Practise answering the classic question: \"ROAS dropped, what do you do?\" Use Unit 12's routine: confirm it's real, walk the tree, find where, find why, one action.","k":"L6.2"},{"t":"p","text":"Official certifications help show you know the platforms. Google offers Google Ads certifications on Skillshop, and Meta offers certifications through Meta Blueprint.","k":"L6.3"},{"t":"apply","text":"Do the interview practice tasks in the Practice area, and record yourself answering \"Tell me about a campaign you'd run for a local clinic\" in under two minutes.","k":"L6.4"}],"k":"L6.h"}],"sheet":["Day-one audit: tracking, goals, structure, creative, Google specifics, policy. Look before you touch.","Monthly report: did it work (business numbers) → why (metric chain) → what we learned (tests) → what's next (plan + asks).","Bad news first, with cause and fix.","Weekly 3-line note: result, learning, next step.","Meta Ad Library: all active ads across Meta; EU ads for a year; appears within 24 h.","Google Ads Transparency Center: search by advertiser or website.","Interview: portfolio (teardown, test plan, sample report, ads) + the diagnosis routine. Certifications: Google Skillshop, Meta Blueprint."],"src":[{"label":"Meta Ad Library","url":"https://www.facebook.com/ads/library/"},{"label":"Google Ads Transparency Center","url":"https://adstransparency.google.com/"},{"label":"Google Skillshop","url":"https://skillshop.withgoogle.com/"},{"label":"Meta Blueprint","url":"https://www.facebookblueprint.com/"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What should a monthly report lead with?","o":["CPM and CTR charts","Business results from the client's own data, compared with last month","A list of every change made","Platform ROAS only"],"a":1,"e":"Did it work? Then why, what we learned, what's next."},{"q":"First hour in a new account. What do you do?","o":["Pause the worst campaigns","Raise budgets on the best ones","Look: tracking, goals, structure, creative, Google specifics, policy","Rewrite all ads"],"a":2,"e":"Audit before you touch."},{"q":"What does the Meta Ad Library show?","o":["Only political ads","All ads currently running across Meta, plus some past ads (e.g. EU ads from the past year)","Competitors' budgets and ROAS","Only your own ads"],"a":1,"e":"Meta: all active ads across Meta technologies, and more."},{"q":"An October report has bad results. Where does the bad news go?","o":["First, with cause and fix","Last, after the good news","Leave it out","In an appendix"],"a":0,"e":"Clients forgive bad months, not surprises."},{"q":"How can you search the Google Ads Transparency Center?","o":["By the competitor's budget","By advertiser name or website","By their Quality Score","By their conversion rate"],"a":1,"e":"Search by advertiser or website."},{"q":"In an interview: \"Our ROAS dropped, what would you do?\" Best answer structure?","o":["\"Raise the budget.\"","Confirm it's real, walk the metric tree, find where and why, then one action","\"Change all the ads.\"","\"Switch to Google.\""],"a":1,"e":"Unit 12's diagnosis routine."},{"q":"Which is a strong portfolio piece for a junior?","o":["A list of courses","A teardown of a real brand's ads from the Ad Library, with what you'd test next","A screenshot of a certificate only","A logo you designed"],"a":1,"e":"Show thinking with real ads, not just claims."}],"drills":[{"type":"order","title":"The monthly report","prompt":["Put the sections of a monthly report in order."],"checks":[],"model":["Did it work → why → learned → next → asks."],"items":["Business results vs last month (client's data)","Why: the metric chain on each platform","What we learned: tests and results","Next month's plan","What we need from the client"],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"sort","title":"Which audit heading?","prompt":["Sort each audit finding under the right heading."],"checks":[],"model":["Each heading has its own fix. Tracking first, always."],"items":["Purchases in Ads Manager are 30% higher than real orders => Tracking","14 ad sets, most with under 10 conversions a week => Structure","Only 3 ads, all 5 months old, frequency 7 => Creative","Clinic remarketing list used in Google Search => Policy","\"Page view\" set as a primary conversion => Tracking","Ad text: \"Tired of your wrinkles?\" => Policy"],"cats":["Tracking","Structure","Creative","Policy"],"pairs":[],"calc":[]},{"type":"write","title":"Write the report opener","prompt":["Write a three-line report opener for a shop: result with numbers, why, next step. Use realistic example numbers."],"checks":[{"kind":"lines","arg":"3","label":"Three lines"},{"kind":"number","arg":"","label":"Includes numbers"},{"kind":"include","arg":"because,came from,thanks to,driven by,due to,the win","label":"Explains why"},{"kind":"include","arg":"next,in november,in december,we'll,we will","label":"States the next step"}],"model":["\"October: 1,240 orders from ads, up 12%, at a blended cost of 96 kr per order. / The win came from the new review-led videos on Meta and tighter negatives on Google. / Next month we'll scale review-led creative and test a free-shipping offer.\""],"items":[],"cats":[],"pairs":[],"calc":[]}],"try":[{"ref":"taste:t12-1-01","app":"taste","id":"t12-1-01","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t12-1-01","title":"What DTC agencies look for in an intern","kind":"Mini-lesson","module":"12.1"},{"ref":"taste:t12-1-02","app":"taste","id":"t12-1-02","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t12-1-02","title":"BRIEF: Pick any DTC brand and open its ads in the Meta Ad Librar…","kind":"Write it","module":"12.1"},{"ref":"taste:t12-1-03","app":"taste","id":"t12-1-03","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t12-1-03","title":"BRIEF: Write a 3-ad test plan for a DTC brand (example: Swedish …","kind":"Write it","module":"12.1"},{"ref":"taste:t12-1-04","app":"taste","id":"t12-1-04","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t12-1-04","title":"BRIEF: Interview question — 'Tell us about a campaign you ran an…","kind":"Write it","module":"12.1"},{"ref":"taste:t12-1-06","app":"taste","id":"t12-1-06","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t12-1-06","title":"Interview: 'Why do you think this ad has run for 8 months?' Stro…","kind":"Pick the winner","module":"12.1"},{"ref":"taste:t6-1-01","app":"taste","id":"t6-1-01","url":"taste/index.html?embed=1#/t/t6-1-01","title":"FIELD EXERCISE (real ads, Meta Ad Library). Open the Ad Library …","kind":"Write it","module":"6.1"},{"ref":"ws:w:w6-161","app":"ws","id":"w:w6-161","url":"workshop/index.html?embed=1#/w/w6-161","title":"Full cold email — Östermalm Dental English","kind":"Copy rep"}],"words":918,"audio":{"duration":404.908,"markers":[{"t":"Start","s":0.3},{"t":"1. The first-day audit.","s":29.6},{"t":"2. The monthly report.","s":106.64},{"t":"3. Talking to clients between reports.","s":199.47},{"t":"4. Competitor research: Meta Ad Library.","s":241.25},{"t":"5. Competitor research: Google Ads Transparency Center.","s":297.14},{"t":"6. The interview: show, don't claim.","s":335.97},{"t":"End","s":396.61}],"segs":[{"k":"title","s":0.3,"d":7.536},{"k":"lead.0","s":8.436,"d":8.04},{"k":"lead.1","s":17.076,"d":11.928},{"k":"L1.h","s":29.604,"d":2.232},{"k":"L1.0","s":32.336,"d":5.496},{"k":"L1.1","s":38.432,"d":10.944},{"k":"L1.2","s":49.976,"d":8.088},{"k":"L1.3","s":58.664,"d":9.24},{"k":"L1.4","s":68.504,"d":6.792},{"k":"L1.5","s":75.896,"d":11.328},{"k":"L1.6","s":87.824,"d":9.144},{"k":"L1.7","s":97.568,"d":8.472},{"k":"L2.h","s":106.64,"d":2.448},{"k":"L2.0","s":109.588,"d":7.44},{"k":"L2.1","s":117.628,"d":22.176},{"k":"L2.2","s":140.404,"d":10.056},{"k":"L2.3","s":151.06,"d":27.168},{"k":"L2.4","s":178.828,"d":10.56},{"k":"L2.5","s":189.988,"d":8.88},{"k":"L3.h","s":199.468,"d":3.6},{"k":"L3.0","s":203.568,"d":5.064},{"k":"L3.1","s":209.232,"d":11.064},{"k":"L3.2","s":220.896,"d":10.152},{"k":"L3.3","s":231.648,"d":9.0},{"k":"L4.h","s":241.248,"d":4.8},{"k":"L4.0","s":246.548,"d":26.112},{"k":"L4.1","s":273.26,"d":12.264},{"k":"L4.3","s":286.124,"d":10.416},{"k":"L5.h","s":297.14,"d":5.088},{"k":"L5.0","s":302.728,"d":9.096},{"k":"L5.1","s":312.424,"d":12.504},{"k":"L5.3","s":325.528,"d":9.84},{"k":"L6.h","s":335.968,"d":3.432},{"k":"L6.0","s":339.9,"d":5.904},{"k":"L6.1","s":346.404,"d":13.92},{"k":"L6.2","s":360.924,"d":12.0},{"k":"L6.3","s":373.524,"d":11.04},{"k":"L6.4","s":385.164,"d":10.848},{"k":"end","s":396.612,"d":6.696}],"file":"aa-t16-reporting-to-clients-and-doing-the-job-a.mp3"}}],"glossary":[{"t":"Ad Rank","d":"Google's score that decides if your ad can show and where. Uses your bid, ad and landing page quality, thresholds, search context and expected asset impact. Calculated twice per auction.","u":8},{"t":"Ad set","d":"Meta's middle level: audience, placements, budget (unless set at campaign level), schedule and optimisation.","u":2},{"t":"Ad group","d":"Google's middle level: one narrow theme of keywords plus the ads for them.","u":3},{"t":"Ad Strength","d":"Google's rating (Poor to Excellent) of how varied and keyword-relevant your responsive search ad is. Feedback only, not used in the auction.","u":8},{"t":"Advantage+","d":"Meta's name for its automated options: Advantage+ sales campaigns, audience, placements, campaign budget and creative.","u":7},{"t":"Advantage+ audience","d":"Meta finds the audience for you. Location, minimum age, language and exclusions stay hard limits; the rest are suggestions.","u":7},{"t":"AI Max","d":"A set of Google features inside a Search campaign: broader search term matching, text customisation and final URL expansion.","u":14},{"t":"Andromeda","d":"Meta's next-generation AI model in its ads system. Meta links it to creative diversification.","u":7},{"t":"Asset group","d":"In Performance Max: a themed bundle of images, text, logos and videos that Google mixes into ads.","u":14},{"t":"Attribution","d":"The rule for which ad gets credit for a conversion, e.g. within 7 days of a click.","u":15},{"t":"Audience signal","d":"In Performance Max: a hint (like a customer list) about where to start looking. Not a hard limit.","u":14},{"t":"Bid cap","d":"Meta's manual bid strategy: the maximum you'll bid in any auction.","u":9},{"t":"Blended ROAS","d":"Total revenue from the business divided by total ad spend across all platforms. Not a platform metric.","u":15},{"t":"Break-even ROAS","d":"1 ÷ margin. Below it, the first order loses money. A 40% margin gives 2.5.","u":6},{"t":"Breakdown effect","d":"Meta's name for the misreading that its system moves money into worse ad sets, ads or placements.","u":12},{"t":"Broad match","d":"Google's default keyword match type: ads can show on searches related to the keyword's meaning.","u":3},{"t":"Call to action","d":"The one next step you ask for: book, buy, call. Short form: CTA.","u":4},{"t":"Consent mode","d":"Google feature that passes a visitor's cookie consent choice to Google tags. Basic or advanced. Doesn't show a banner.","u":5},{"t":"Conversion","d":"The action you're paying for: a purchase, lead, booking, sign-up.","u":5},{"t":"Conversion rate","d":"Conversions ÷ clicks (Google: ÷ ad interactions). Mostly about the landing page and offer.","u":6},{"t":"Conversions API","d":"Meta's server-side connection: sends events from your server, platform or CRM. Used together with the Pixel.","u":5},{"t":"CPA","d":"Cost per acquisition (or per result): spend ÷ conversions.","u":6},{"t":"CPC","d":"Cost per click: spend ÷ clicks.","u":6},{"t":"CPM","d":"Cost per thousand impressions: spend ÷ impressions × 1,000.","u":6},{"t":"Creative diversification","d":"Running many genuinely different ads (angles, people, formats) so the system can find different buyers.","u":7},{"t":"CTR","d":"Click-through rate: clicks ÷ impressions.","u":6},{"t":"Custom audience","d":"A Meta audience built from your own data: site visitors, customer lists, video viewers and more.","u":7},{"t":"Data-driven attribution","d":"Google's default attribution model for most conversions: shares credit based on which ad interactions made a difference.","u":15},{"t":"Deduplication","d":"Counting one sale once when the Pixel and Conversions API both send it. Needs the same event name and event ID.","u":5},{"t":"Enhanced conversions","d":"Google feature that sends hashed first-party data (like email) to improve conversion measurement.","u":5},{"t":"Exact match","d":"Google match type in [brackets]: searches with the same meaning or intent as the keyword.","u":3},{"t":"Frequency","d":"Meta: the average number of times each person saw your ad (impressions ÷ reach).","u":6},{"t":"Hold rate","d":"Industry convention: ThruPlays ÷ 3-second video plays. Did the body keep people watching?","u":6},{"t":"Hook","d":"The first line or first seconds of an ad. Its job is to stop the scroll.","u":4},{"t":"Hook rate","d":"Industry convention: 3-second video plays ÷ impressions. Meta has a matching metric, '3-second video plays rate per impressions'.","u":6},{"t":"Impression","d":"One time an ad is on screen.","u":6},{"t":"Incrementality","d":"Whether the ads caused the sale, or it would have happened anyway.","u":15},{"t":"Learning phase","d":"Meta's warm-up while delivery learns. Usually ends after about 50 results in the week after the last significant edit.","u":9},{"t":"Lookalike audience","d":"Meta audience of new people similar to a source audience, such as your best customers.","u":7},{"t":"Negative keyword","d":"A word or phrase that stops your Google ad showing on searches you don't want.","u":3},{"t":"Objective","d":"Meta's campaign goal: Awareness, Traffic, Engagement, Leads, App promotion or Sales.","u":2},{"t":"Performance Max","d":"Google campaign type that runs across all Google inventory (Search, YouTube, Display, Discover, Gmail, Maps) from one campaign.","u":14},{"t":"Phrase match","d":"Google match type in \"quotes\": searches that include the meaning of the keyword.","u":3},{"t":"Pixel","d":"Meta Pixel: code on your website that logs events like purchases and sends them to Meta.","u":5},{"t":"Placement","d":"Where an ad shows, e.g. Facebook Feed, Instagram Stories, Reels.","u":7},{"t":"Primary conversion","d":"Google conversion action used for bidding and shown in the Conversions column. Secondary ones are for observation.","u":5},{"t":"Quality Score","d":"Google's 1–10 keyword diagnostic from expected CTR, ad relevance and landing page experience. Not an auction input.","u":8},{"t":"Relevance diagnostics","d":"Meta's quality, engagement rate and conversion rate rankings for ads with 500+ impressions. Not auction inputs.","u":12},{"t":"ROAS","d":"Return on ad spend: ad revenue ÷ ad spend. 5 kr back for 1 kr spent = ROAS 5 (500%).","u":6},{"t":"RSA","d":"Responsive search ad: up to 15 headlines and 4 descriptions that Google combines for each search.","u":8},{"t":"Search terms report","d":"Google report of the real searches that triggered your ads.","u":12},{"t":"Smart Bidding","d":"Google's AI bid strategies for conversions or value: Target CPA, Target ROAS, Maximize conversions, Maximize conversion value.","u":9},{"t":"Target CPA","d":"Bid strategy that aims for conversions at a set average cost.","u":9},{"t":"Target ROAS","d":"Bid strategy that aims for conversion value at a set return on ad spend.","u":9},{"t":"ThruPlay","d":"A Meta video metric and video-views performance goal.","u":6}],"built":"2026-11-11 14:51"};